        "evidence": "This is false for the usual strong definition of m-dependence (independence of separated blocks). Let a fair regime S be known at time zero, and let iid innovations epsilon_n be uniform on {0,1,2,3}. Define the binary outcome at n+2 to be zero on A_n: in regime 0, epsilon_(n+1)<2; in regime 1, epsilon_(n+1)=0 or epsilon_(n+2)=0 or both are 1. Both regimes give P(A_n|F_n)=1/2, so the ideal two-step forecast is Bernoulli(1/2). Independent randomized PITs are uniform, and their lower-half events are A_n. The adjacent-pair probability is 16/64 in regime 0 and 22/64 in regime 1. Distant adjacent pairs use disjoint innovations conditional on S but share S: their intersection probability exceeds the product by 9/4096. Thus the ideal PIT sequence is not 1-dependent. Single PITs at sufficiently separated origins, and each h-spaced subsequence, can still be independent; that is weaker than block independence.",
