      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 10,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "The Action Topology and Analytic Results",
      "statement": "\\label{cor:action-metrisation}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, and let\n    \\begin{enumerate}[-]\n        \\item $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple space over $X$ endowed with the tuple Hausdorff distance (Theorem~\\ref{thm:tuple-space-metrisation}),\n        \\item $(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}),d_H)$ be the set of finite subsets of the natural numbers, endowed with the Hausdorff distance induced by the Euclidean distance (Definition~\\ref{defn:Hausdorff-distance}), and\n        \\item $(\\mathscr{L}(X),d_H^\\ast)$ be the set of labelled finite sets over $X$, endowed with the index-augmented Hausdorff distance (Theorem~\\ref{thm:labelled-properties}).\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Let $A = \\{\\oplus, \\ominus, \\circledast\\}$ be the set of operations defined in Definitions~\\ref{defn:concat-tuple}, \\ref{defn:cancel}, and \\ref{defn:modification}, respectively. Let\n    \\begin{align*}\n        F_\\alpha = \\begin{cases}\n            \\mathscr{T}(X) & \\alpha = \\oplus\\\\\n            \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}) & \\alpha = \\ominus \\\\ \n            \\mathscr{L}(X) &\\alpha = \\circledast\n        \\end{cases},\\quad\\quad d_\\alpha = \\begin{cases}\n            \\mathscr{d}_H & \\alpha = \\oplus\\\\\n            d_H & \\alpha = \\ominus \\\\ \n            d_H^\\ast &\\alpha = \\circledast\n        \\end{cases},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and let $\\mathscr{A}(X) = \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} F_\\alpha$. Define $\\Delta : \\mathscr{A}(X)\\times \\mathscr{A}(X) \\to [0,\\infty]$ by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Delta((o,\\alpha),(o^\\prime,\\alpha^\\prime))=\\begin{cases}\n            d_\\alpha(o,o^\\prime)&\\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime \\\\\n            \\infty &\\alpha \\neq \\alpha^\\prime.\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $(\\mathscr{A}(X), \\Delta)$ is a metric space. Moreover, if $(X,d)$ is complete, then so is $(\\mathscr{A}(X), \\Delta)$. Likewise, if $(X,d)$ is separable, then so is $(\\mathscr{A}(X), \\Delta)$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 552,
        "references": [
          "prop:disjoint-union-metrisation",
          "thm:tuple-space-properties",
          "thm:labelled-properties",
          "prop:finite-hausdorff-uniformly-discrete",
          "prop:uniformly-discrete-complete",
          "prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties",
          "prop:finite-separable"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/3_market_actions.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/cor__action-metrisation__proof.svg",
        "text": "By Proposition~\\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metrisation}, $\\Delta$ is indeed a metric on the disjoint union $\\mathscr{A}(X)$.\n\n    If $(X,d)$ is complete, then $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ is complete by Theorem~\\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}, $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$ is complete by Theorem~\\ref{thm:labelled-properties}, and $(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}), d_H)$ is complete because $(\\mathbb{N},|\\cdot|)$ is uniformly discrete, hence Proposition~\\ref{prop:finite-hausdorff-uniformly-discrete} makes $\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N})$ uniformly discrete and Proposition~\\ref{prop:uniformly-discrete-complete} then yields completeness. Therefore, Proposition~\\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties} implies that $(\\mathscr{A}(X),\\Delta)$ is complete.\n\n    If $(X,d)$ is separable, then $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ is separable by Theorem~\\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}, $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$ is separable by Theorem~\\ref{thm:labelled-properties}, and $(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}), d_H)$ is separable by Proposition~\\ref{prop:finite-separable}, since $(\\mathbb{N},|\\cdot|)$ is countable and hence separable. Another application of Proposition~\\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties} then yields that $(\\mathscr{A}(X),\\Delta)$ is separable."
      },
