      "section": "Weak Transport of Structure",
      "statement": "\\label{cor:grothendieck-vectorization-inner-product-space}\n    Let $X$ be a non-empty set, $(V, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ a real inner product space, and $\\varphi : X \\to V$ a weakly convex operator. Let $T:\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi) \\to \\mathrm{span}~\\varphi(X)$ be the linear isomorphism \n    \\begin{align*}\n        T([(x_\\varphi,z_\\varphi)]) = \\Phi(x_\\varphi) - \\Phi(z_\\varphi).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Endow $\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi)$ with the bilinear form $\\llangle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rrangle_{\\mathscr{V}} :\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi) \\times \\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\llangle [(x_\\varphi,z_\\varphi)], [(x^\\prime_\\varphi, z^\\prime_\\varphi)] \\rrangle_{\\mathscr{V}} &= \\langle T([(x_\\varphi,z_\\varphi)]), T([(x^\\prime_\\varphi, z^\\prime_\\varphi)])\\rangle \\\\ &= \\langle \\Phi(x_\\varphi) - \\Phi(z_\\varphi), \\Phi(x^\\prime_\\varphi) - \\Phi(z^\\prime_\\varphi) \\rangle. \n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $(\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi), \\llangle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rrangle_{\\mathscr{V}})$ is an inner product space, and $T$ is an isometric linear isomorphism. In addition,\n    \\begin{enumerate}\n        \\item The completion of $\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi)$ and the completion of $\\mathrm{span}~\\varphi(X)$ are isometrically isomorphic. \n        \\item The operator $T$ extends to a linear isometric isomorphism between the completion of $\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi)$ and the completion of ${\\mathrm{span}~\\varphi(X)}$. \n        \\item The map $x_\\varphi\\mapsto[(x_\\varphi,0_\\varphi)]$, where $0_\\varphi$ denotes the cone zero induced by the transported cone structure, is an isometric embedding of the convex cone $X_\\varphi$ into $\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi)$; here $X_\\varphi$ carries the metric $(x_\\varphi,x^\\prime_\\varphi)\\mapsto\\lVert\\Phi(x_\\varphi)-\\Phi(x^\\prime_\\varphi)\\rVert$ pulled back from $V$. The closure of the embedded image is a closed convex cone in the completion of $\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi)$.\n        \\item There is a unique metric projection from the completion of $\\mathscr{V}(X,\\varphi)$ onto the closure of the embedded cone.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1325,
        "references": [
          "thm:grothendieck-vectorization-is-span",
          "thm:closed-convex-cone-closure-projection"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/cor__grothendieck-vectorization-inner-product-space__proof.svg",
        "text": "Corollary~\\ref{thm:grothendieck-vectorization-is-span} makes $T$ a linear isomorphism, and the displayed definition makes it inner-product preserving; this proves the inner-product and isometry claims. An isometry extends uniquely to an isometry of the completions, which proves items 1 and 2. For the map $J(x_\\varphi)=[(x_\\varphi,0_\\varphi)]$, we have $T(J(x_\\varphi))=\\Phi(x_\\varphi)$ because $\\Phi(0_\\varphi)=0$, so $J$ is isometric. It also preserves addition and multiplication by non-negative scalars, hence its image is a convex cone. Its closure in the Hilbert completion is therefore a non-empty closed convex cone, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:closed-convex-cone-closure-projection} supplies the unique metric projection in item 4."
      },
