        "text": "We proceed by induction on $i \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$. For $i = 0$, $\\Phi^{(0)} = \\mathrm{Id}$ is the identity map on $\\mathscr{S}(U,\\preceq^{\\mathrm{buy}})\\times\\mathscr{S}(U,\\preceq^{\\mathrm{sell}})$, which is continuous and hence Borel measurable. For $i = 1$, $\\Phi^{(1)} = \\Phi$ is Borel measurable by Proposition~\\ref{prop:single-stage-execution}. Suppose now that $\\Phi^{(i-1)}$ is Borel measurable for some $i > 1$. Since $\\Phi^{(i)} = \\Phi^{(i-1)} \\circ \\Phi$ and the composition of Borel measurable maps is Borel measurable, it follows that $\\Phi^{(i)}$ is Borel measurable. By induction, $\\Phi^{(i)}$ is Borel measurable for all $i \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$."
