      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 22,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{cor:minimum-sorting-map-idempotent}\n    Let $(X, \\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set. Then, the minimum sorting map $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ defined by sorting each comparability component separately and concatenating the results is an idempotent sorting map.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1597,
        "references": [
          "prop:minimum-sorting-map-is-sorting-map",
          "defn:sorting-finite-sets"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/cor__minimum-sorting-map-idempotent__proof.svg",
        "text": "By Proposition~\\ref{prop:minimum-sorting-map-is-sorting-map}, $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}$ is a sorting map and its image lies in $\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$. We first note that the rule of Definition~\\ref{defn:sorting-finite-sets} returns every sorted tuple unchanged, in the sense that if $\\bm{z}\\in\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ and $\\xi = \\{(i_r,\\bm{z}(r)) \\mid r \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{z})\\}\\in\\mathscr{L}(X)$ with labels $i_1<\\dots<i_{\\ell(\\bm{z})}$, then $\\varsigma(\\xi) = \\bm{z}$. Indeed, suppose that the first $r-1$ steps have chosen the elements with labels $i_1,\\dots,i_{r-1}$. Then $(i_r,\\bm{z}(r))$ is minimal among the remaining elements, since $\\bm{z}$ is sorted and so no later entry lies strictly below $\\bm{z}(r)$, and it carries the smallest remaining label; it is therefore chosen at step $r$.\n\n    Now let $\\bm{x}\\in\\mathscr{T}(X)$ with $\\ell(\\bm{x})>0$, let $C_1,\\dots,C_\\kappa$ be the equivalence classes of $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim}$ in order of first appearance, and let $\\sigma$ be the bijection from the proof of Proposition~\\ref{prop:minimum-sorting-map-is-sorting-map}, so that $\\bm{y} = \\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{x}) = \\bm{x}\\circ\\sigma$. Put $s_0 = 0$, $s_r = |C_1|+\\dots+|C_r|$ and $B_r = \\{s_{r-1}+1,\\dots,s_r\\}$. Then $\\sigma(B_r) = C_r$, and the entries of $\\bm{y}$ at the positions $B_r$ form the block $\\varsigma(K_r)$.\n\n    The comparability components of $\\bm{y}$ are its blocks, in the same order. Comparability depends only on the entries, so $p \\overset{\\bm{y}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} q$ if and only if $\\sigma(p) \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} \\sigma(q)$, and hence $p \\overset{\\bm{y}}{\\sim} q$ if and only if $\\sigma(p) \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} \\sigma(q)$. The equivalence classes of $\\overset{\\bm{y}}{\\sim}$ are thus $\\sigma^{-1}(C_r) = B_r$, and since $\\min B_1 < \\dots < \\min B_\\kappa$, the $r$-th comparability component of $\\bm{y}$ is $\\{(p,\\bm{y}(p)) \\mid p \\in B_r\\}$.\n\n    This component lists the block $\\varsigma(K_r)$ with increasing labels, and $\\varsigma(K_r)$ is sorted by Definition~\\ref{defn:sorting-finite-sets}, so by the observation above, sorting it returns $\\varsigma(K_r)$. Hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{y}) = \\bigoplus_{r=1}^{\\kappa}\\varsigma(K_r) = \\bm{y}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Together with $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{e}) = \\bm{e}$, this shows that $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}\\circ\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}=\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}$."
      },
