            "text": "Fix $\\alpha\\in A$, and let $x,y\\in X_\\alpha$ be arbitrary. By Definition~\\ref{defn:disjoint-union-metrisation} of the disjoint-union metric, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(\\iota_\\alpha(x),\\iota_\\alpha(y))=d_\\alpha(x,y).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence, the map $(i,x)\\mapsto(i,\\iota_\\alpha(x))$ preserves the metric on the graphs of tuples. By the definition of the induced tuple Hausdorff metrics, for all $\\bm{x},\\bm{y}\\in\\mathscr{T}(X_\\alpha)$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{\\iota}_\\alpha\\bm{x},\\bm{\\iota}_\\alpha\\bm{y})\n        =\\mathscr{d}_{H,\\alpha}(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus, $\\bm{\\iota}_\\alpha$ is an isometry from $(\\mathscr{T}(X_\\alpha),\\mathscr{d}_{H,\\alpha})$ into $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$, and it is therefore continuous and Borel measurable, as claimed.",
