        "text": "We must show that the composite mapping $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}:\\mathscr{T}(O)\\to[0,\\infty)$ is Borel measurable. First, note that the separation map $\\bm{m} \\mapsto (\\bm{b}, \\bm{s})$ is continuous by Proposition~\\ref{prop:separation-continuous}, and the coordinate projections $(\\bm{b}, \\bm{s}) \\mapsto ((\\bm{p}^{\\bm{b}}, \\bm{q}^{\\bm{b}}), (\\bm{p}^{\\bm{s}}, \\bm{q}^{\\bm{s}}))$ are continuous by Proposition~\\ref{prop:tuple-projection-continuous}, so their composition is Borel measurable.\n\n    Second, on the zero-length layer $\\{\\bm{e}\\} \\times \\{\\bm{e}\\}$, each sorted-pair map $(\\bm{p}, \\bm{q}) \\mapsto (\\bm{p}[\\pi^\\preceq(\\bm{p})], \\bm{q}[\\pi^\\preceq(\\bm{p})])$ is constant at $(\\bm{e}, \\bm{e})$ and hence Borel measurable. On each positive-length matching layer $\\mathbb{R}^n \\times [0,\\infty)^n$ with $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, Proposition~\\ref{prop:minimal-sorting-measurable} establishes that $\\bm{p} \\mapsto \\pi^\\preceq(\\bm{p})$ is Borel measurable, and Proposition~\\ref{prop:permuting-continuous} establishes that the permutation map $(\\bm{x}, \\pi) \\mapsto \\bm{x}[\\pi]$ is continuous. Because the fixed-length layers of $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})$ and $\\mathscr{T}([0,\\infty))$ are clopen by the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}), the matching-length product layers form a countable Borel partition of the matching-length domain, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:measurable-partition} pastes the layerwise maps into Borel measurable maps $\\bm{m} \\mapsto (\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}, \\widehat{\\bm{q}}^{\\bm{b}})$ and $\\bm{m} \\mapsto (\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}, \\widehat{\\bm{q}}^{\\bm{s}})$.\n\n    Third, by Lemma~\\ref{lem:cumulative-summation-continuous}, the cumulative summation map $\\bm{\\Sigma} : \\mathscr{T}([0,\\infty)) \\to \\mathscr{T}([0,\\infty))$ is continuous. Since the sorted price tuples $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}$ and $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}$ belong to $\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq)$ and $\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq)$, the cumulative quantities $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}$ and $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}$ are non-decreasing, and reindexing together with $\\bm{\\Sigma}$ preserves tuple lengths, the image of the map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{m} \\mapsto \\Bigl(\\bigl(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m}), \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})\\bigr), \\bigl(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m}), \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})\\bigr)\\Bigr)\n    \\end{align*}\n    lies in $\\mathfrak{C}(\\leq) \\times \\mathfrak{C}(\\geq)$. By the product identity for Borel $\\sigma$-algebras on separable metric spaces noted in Subsection~\\ref{subsec:step-3-sorting}, componentwise measurability implies that this map is Borel measurable into $\\mathfrak{C}(\\leq) \\times \\mathfrak{C}(\\geq)$. Composing it with the Borel measurable functional $\\mathscr{Q}$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable} proves that $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable, as desired."
