      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Aggregation and Executable Volume",
      "statement": "\\label{cor:state-midpoint-price-measurable}\nLet $O=\\bigsqcup_{\\kappa\\in\\{\\mathrm{buy},\\mathrm{sell}\\}}\\mathbb{R}\\times[0,\\infty)$ carry the disjoint-union metric induced by the maximum product metric on $\\mathbb{R}\\times[0,\\infty)$, and equip $\\mathscr{T}(O)$ with its tuple Hausdorff Borel $\\sigma$-algebra. For $\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{T}(O)$, let $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}$, $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}$, $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}$, $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}$, and $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ be the sorted price, cumulative-quantity, and maximum executable volume maps from Corollary~\\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}. For $a\\in\\{\\bm{s},\\bm{b}\\}$ and $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})>0$, let $\\iota^a(\\bm{m})$ be the least $i\\in\\mathrm{domain}(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^a)$ such that $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^a(i)\\geq\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$. Then the map $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}:\\mathscr{T}(O)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ given by\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,\n&\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})=0,\\\\[2pt]\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m}))\n+\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\iota^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m}))}{2},\n&\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})>0,\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{align*}\nis well-defined, and $(\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}},\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}):\\mathscr{T}(O)\\to\\mathbb{R}\\times[0,\\infty)$ is Borel measurable for the product Borel $\\sigma$-algebra.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 769,
        "references": [
          "cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable",
          "thm:the-standard-machinery",
          "thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable",
          "thm:measurable-partition"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/4_market_clearing.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/cor__state-midpoint-price-measurable__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ is well-defined on $\\mathscr{T}(O)$ and that the paired map $(\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}},\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}) : \\mathscr{T}(O) \\to \\mathbb{R} \\times [0,\\infty)$ is Borel measurable.\n\nFirst, by Corollary~\\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}, the tuple maps $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}$, $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}$, $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}$, and $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}$, as well as the scalar functional $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}$, are Borel measurable. If $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})>0$ and $(i^*,j^*) \\in \\mathfrak{I}(\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}, \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}})$ attains the maximum in the definition of $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$, then $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})=\\min(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}(i^*),\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}(j^*))$, so $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}(i^*)\\geq\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$ and $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}(j^*)\\geq\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$. Hence the minimal indices $\\iota^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})\\leq i^*$ and $\\iota^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})\\leq j^*$ exist in $\\mathrm{domain}(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}})$ and $\\mathrm{domain}(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}})$, respectively, and $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ is well-defined. Moreover, since the sell prices are sorted in ascending order and the buy prices in descending order, we have\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m}))\\leq\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(i^*)\\leq\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(j^*)\\leq\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\iota^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})),\n\\end{align*}\nso the midpoint lies between two price-compatible marginal prices.\n\nIt remains to show that $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}} : \\mathscr{T}(O) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is Borel measurable. Set $A_0 = \\{\\bm{m} \\in \\mathscr{T}(O) \\mid \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}) = 0\\}$ and $A_+ = \\{\\bm{m} \\in \\mathscr{T}(O) \\mid \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}) > 0\\}$. Because $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable by Corollary~\\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}, both $A_0$ and $A_+$ belong to $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(O))$. On $A_0$, the map $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ is identically zero and hence measurable.\n\nOn $A_+$, we decompose the domain by the lengths of the separated sell and buy sub-tuples. For each pair $(n_s, n_b) \\in \\mathbb{N}^2$, define\n\\begin{align*}\n    A_{n_s, n_b} = \\bigl\\{\\bm{m} \\in A_+ \\;\\big|\\; \\ell(\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})) = n_s \\;\\land\\; \\ell(\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})) = n_b\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nSince the fixed-length layers of $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})$ are clopen by the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}) and the maps $\\bm{m} \\mapsto \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})$ and $\\bm{m} \\mapsto \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})$ are Borel measurable, the sets $(A_{n_s, n_b})_{(n_s, n_b) \\in \\mathbb{N}^2}$, together with $A_0$, form a countable measurable partition of $\\mathscr{T}(O)$.\n\nFix $(n_s, n_b) \\in \\mathbb{N}^2$, and recall from the proof of Theorem~\\ref{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable} the continuous coordinate evaluation maps $\\mathrm{ev}_i : \\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R}) \\to \\mathbb{R}$, with $\\mathrm{ev}_0 \\equiv 0$. For each $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n_s\\}$ and $j \\in \\{1, \\dots, n_b\\}$, define the subset\n\\begin{align*}\n    B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j} = \\bigl\\{\\bm{m} \\in A_{n_s, n_b} \\;\\big|\\; \\iota^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m}) = i \\;\\land\\; \\iota^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m}) = j\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nBy the definition of the minimal index $\\iota^a(\\bm{m})$ and the monotonicity of $\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^a(\\bm{m})$, we have\n\\begin{align*}\n    B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j}\n    &= \\bigl\\{\\bm{m} \\in A_{n_s, n_b} \\;\\big|\\; \\mathrm{ev}_{i-1}(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})) < \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}) \\leq \\mathrm{ev}_i(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m}))\\bigr\\}\\\\\n    &\\quad \\cap \\bigl\\{\\bm{m} \\in A_{n_s, n_b} \\;\\big|\\; \\mathrm{ev}_{j-1}(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})) < \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}) \\leq \\mathrm{ev}_j(\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m}))\\bigr\\},\n\\end{align*}\nwhich is a finite intersection of Borel sets and therefore Borel measurable. Moreover, on each cell $B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j}$, the map $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ coincides with the fixed linear combination\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\bm{m} \\mapsto \\frac{1}{2}\\Bigl(\\mathrm{ev}_i\\bigl(\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})\\bigr) + \\mathrm{ev}_j\\bigl(\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m})\\bigr)\\Bigr),\n\\end{align*}\nwhich is a composition of Borel measurable maps and hence Borel measurable. Because the family $\\{A_0\\} \\cup \\{B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j} \\mid (n_s, n_b) \\in \\mathbb{N}^2, 1 \\leq i \\leq n_s, 1 \\leq j \\leq n_b\\}$ is a countable measurable partition of $\\mathscr{T}(O)$, Theorem~\\ref{thm:measurable-partition} implies that $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable. Finally, since $\\mathbb{R} \\times [0, \\infty)$ is separable, $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R} \\times [0, \\infty)) = \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}) \\otimes \\mathcal{B}([0, \\infty))$, and componentwise measurability yields the joint Borel measurability of $(\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}, \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}})$, as desired."
      },
