      "section": "Ordering the Market Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{cor:strict-disjoint-total-order}\n    Let $(A, <)$ be a strictly totally ordered set (that is, $<$ is irreflexive and transitive, and $a \\neq a^\\prime$ implies $a < a^\\prime$ or $a^\\prime < a$) and let $(X_\\alpha, \\prec_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be a family of strictly totally ordered sets. Let $X = \\sqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha$ and define the order $\\prec$ on $X$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (x_\\alpha,\\alpha) \\prec (x_{\\alpha^\\prime}^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime) \\iff (\\alpha < \\alpha^\\prime) \\lor (\\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime \\land x_\\alpha \\prec_\\alpha x_{\\alpha^\\prime}^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $(X,\\prec)$ is a strictly totally ordered set.",
