      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{cor:tuple-metrisation}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, and let $\\mathscr{T}(X)$ be the associated tuple space. Define the mapping $\\mathscr{d}_\\infty : \\mathscr{T}(X) \\times \\mathscr{T}(X) \\to [0,\\infty]$ by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_\\infty(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime) = \\begin{cases}\n            0 & \\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}^\\prime) = 0\\\\\n            \\max_{i \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\ell(\\bm{x})\\}} d(\\bm{x}(i), \\bm{x}^\\prime(i)) & \\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}^\\prime) > 0\\\\\n            \\infty & \\ell(\\bm{x})\\neq\\ell(\\bm{x}^\\prime). \n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_\\infty)$ is a metric space. Moreover, if $(X,d)$ is separable, then $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_\\infty)$ is separable. Similarly, if $(X,d)$ is complete, then $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_\\infty)$ is complete.",
      "status": "verified",
