      "order": 46,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Price Curves as a Space",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:admissible-price-curve-parametrisation}\n    Let $\\mathpzc{P}$ be the space of price curves of Definition~\\ref{defn:space-of-price-curves}, and for each $f\\in\\mathpzc{P}$, let $\\Gamma(f)=\\{(x,f(x))\\mid x\\in\\mathrm{domain}(f)\\}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^2$ denote its graph. Let $\\Theta$ be an operator from $\\mathpzc{P}$ to $\\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R}^2)$ such that, for each $f \\in \\mathpzc{P}$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Theta f \\equiv \\Theta(f) : [-1, 1] \\to \\Gamma(f),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and suppose that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Theta f(t) = (\\vartheta_f(t), (f \\circ \\vartheta_f)(t))\n    \\end{align*}\n    for an increasing, continuous, and surjective function $\\vartheta_f : [-1,1] \\to \\mathrm{domain}(f)$ satisfying $\\vartheta_f(0) = 0$. We call $\\Theta$ an \\emph{admissible parametrisation operator} and $\\vartheta_f$ the parametriser of $f$.",
