      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Measurable Spaces and Measures",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:atom-diffuse}\\index{diffuse measure}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ be a measure space. A set $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$ is called an atom of $\\mu$ if $\\mu(A)>0$ and for every $E \\in \\mathcal{F}$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\min\\{\\mu(A \\cap E),\\mu(A \\setminus E)\\}=0.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The measure $\\mu$ is called purely atomic if every $E \\in \\mathcal{F}$ with $\\mu(E)>0$ contains an atom. It is called diffuse if it has no atoms.",
