      "section": "The Lebesgue and Bochner Integrals",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:bochner-integral}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ be a measure space and let $(V,\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert)$ be a separable Banach space endowed with its Borel $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal{B}(V)$. A simple $\\mathcal{F}/\\mathcal{B}(V)$-measurable function represented on a finite measurable partition $(E_i)_{i=1}^n$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        s(\\omega)=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbbm{1}_{E_i}(\\omega) v_i\n    \\end{align*}\n    is called Bochner integrable if\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\int_\\Omega \\lVert s(\\omega)\\rVert \\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(\\omega)\n        =\n        \\sum_{i=1}^n \\lVert v_i\\rVert \\mu(E_i)\n        < \\infty.\n    \\end{align*}\n    In that case, we define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\int_\\Omega s(\\omega) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(\\omega)\n        =\n        \\sum_{i=1}^n v_i \\mu(E_i).\n    \\end{align*}\nIn the scalar norm sum we use $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$. In the vector sum we omit every term with $v_i=0$; the integrability condition ensures that $\\mu(E_i)<\\infty$ whenever $v_i\\neq0$.\n    Any finite simple-function representation may be refined to such a measurable partition, and the resulting value is independent of the chosen partition representation.\n\n    A measurable function $f:\\Omega \\to V$ is called Bochner integrable if there exists a sequence of Bochner-integrable simple functions $(s_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\int_\\Omega \\lVert f(\\omega)-s_n(\\omega)\\rVert \\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(\\omega) \\to 0.\n    \\end{align*}\n    We then define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\int_\\Omega f(\\omega) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(\\omega)\n        =\n        \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\int_\\Omega s_n(\\omega) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\mu(\\omega),\n    \\end{align*}\n    where the limit is taken in $V$.",
