      "section": "Natural Numbers, Cardinality, and Sequences",
      "statement": "{kunen2014set}}]\\label{defn:cardinality}\\index{cardinality}\n    Let $X$ and $Y$ be sets. We write $|X|\\leq|Y|$ if there is an injective function from $X$ to $Y$, and we write $|X|=|Y|$ and call $X$ and $Y$ equinumerous if $X\\cong Y$. The set $X$ is called\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item finite if $|X|=|\\{1,\\dots,n\\}|$ for some $n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$, in which case we write $|X|=n$ and $|X|<\\infty$, and infinite otherwise, and\n        \\item countable if $|X|\\leq|\\mathbb{N}|$, countably infinite if $|X|=|\\mathbb{N}|$, and uncountable if it is not countable.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Finally, $X$ has the cardinality of the continuum if $|X|=|\\mathbb{R}|$.",
