      "order": 67,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Analytic Results for Primitive Actions",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:composite-actions}\n    Let $X$ be a non-empty set, and let $\\mathscr{A}(X)$ be the associated action space. Then, we call \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{C}(X) = \\mathscr{T}(\\mathscr{A}(X))\n    \\end{align*}\n    the set of composite actions. Moreover, the application map, $\\lhd:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\times \\mathscr{A}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(X)$, extends to a mapping $\\lhd :\\mathscr{T}(X) \\times \\mathscr{C}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(X)$ defined by  \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x} \\lhd \\bm{c} = \\begin{cases}\n            \\bm{x} &\\bm{c}=\\bm{e}\\\\ \n            \\bm{x} \\lhd \\bm{c}(1) \\lhd \\dots \\lhd \\bm{c}(n) & \\ell(\\bm{c}) = n > 0,\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $\\lhd$ is evaluated from left to right, i.e.\\ $\\bm{x} \\lhd \\bm{c}(1) \\lhd \\dots \\lhd \\bm{c}(n)=(\\cdots((\\bm{x} \\lhd \\bm{c}(1)) \\lhd \\bm{c}(2))\\cdots) \\lhd \\bm{c}(n)$.",
      "status": "verified",
