      "statement": "\\label{defn:convergent-sequence}\\index{convergent sequence}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and let $(x_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence in $X$. We say that $(x_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ converges if there exists $x \\in X$ such that for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ for which\n    \\begin{align*}\n        n \\geq N \\implies d(x_n,x) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    In that case, $x$ is called the limit of $(x_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$, and we write $x_n \\to x$ in $(X,d)$, equivalently $d(x_n,x)\\to 0$, or simply $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} x_n = x$ if the metric is clear from context.",
      "status": "verified",
