      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Functional Principal Components",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:coordinate-scaling-operators}\n    Let $T$ be a finite non-empty index set, let $(Z_t)_{t\\in T}$ be a family in $L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)$ with empirical mean $\\bar{Z}$, and let $s_q,s_p>0$. The \\emph{coordinate scaling operator} $\\Upsilon: L^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R}^2) \\to L^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R}^2)$ and its inverse $\\Upsilon^{-1}: L^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R}^2) \\to L^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R}^2)$ are defined by\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:standardizing-operator}\n        \\Upsilon f = x \\mapsto \\begin{bmatrix}\n            s_q & 0\\\\\n            0 & s_p\n        \\end{bmatrix} f(x),\n        \\qquad\n        \\Upsilon^{-1} f  = x \\mapsto \\begin{bmatrix}\n            \\frac{1}{s_q} & 0\\\\\n            0 & \\frac{1}{s_p}\n        \\end{bmatrix} f(x).\n    \\end{align}\n    For each $t\\in T$, the \\emph{normalised deviation} of $Z_t$ from $\\bar{Z}$ is defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi_t =  \\Upsilon^{-1}(Z_t - \\bar{Z}),\n    \\end{align*}\n    so that $Z_t = \\bar{Z} + \\Upsilon \\xi_t$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
