      "statement": "\\label{defn:crossing-price-and-equilibrium-operator}\n    Let $\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{T}(O)$ be a market state with supply curve $S^{\\bm{m}}:[0,s_*]\\to\\mathcal{P}$, demand curve $D^{\\bm{m}}:[0,b_*]\\to\\mathcal{P}$, and level sets $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)\\subseteq\\mathcal{P}$ for $q\\in[-b_*,s_*]$. The \\emph{crossing price} of $\\bm{m}$ is defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        P(\\bm{m})=\\frac{1}{2}\\bigl(\\min J_{\\bm{m}}(0)+\\max J_{\\bm{m}}(0)\\bigr),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and the \\emph{equilibrium quantity} of $\\bm{m}$ is defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        Q(\\bm{m})=\\max\\bigl(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{q\\in(0,\\min(s_*,b_*)]\\mid S^{\\bm{m}}(q)\\leq P(\\bm{m})\\leq D^{\\bm{m}}(q)\\}\\bigr).\n    \\end{align*}\n    The \\emph{equilibrium operator} is the map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathcal{E} : \\mathscr{T}(O) &\\to \\mathbb{R} \\times [0,\\infty)\\\\\n        \\bm{m} &\\mapsto (P(\\bm{m}), Q(\\bm{m})).\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
