      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:disjoint-union-metrisation}\\index{disjoint union!metric}\n    Let $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be a family of metric spaces and $X = \\sqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha$. Define the mapping $d:X\\times X\\to [0,\\infty]$ by\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:disjoint-metric}\n        d((x,\\alpha), (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime)) = \\begin{cases}\n            d_\\alpha(x,x^\\prime) & \\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime\\\\\n            \\infty & \\alpha \\neq \\alpha^\\prime.\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align}\n    We call $d$ the disjoint-union metric induced by $(d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\in A}$, or simply the disjoint-union metric if the metrics $(d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\in A}$ are clear from context. Moreover, we refer to $(X,d)$ as the disjoint union metric space.",
