      "section": "Data and Exploratory Analysis",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:empirical-coordinate-scales}\\index{empirical mean}\n    Let $T$ be a finite non-empty index set, let $(Z_t)_{t\\in T}$ be a family in $L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)$, and let $\\operatorname{pr}_q,\\operatorname{pr}_p:L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)\\to L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R})$ denote the first and second coordinate projections, respectively. The \\emph{empirical mean} of $(Z_t)_{t\\in T}$ is defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bar{Z} = \\frac{1}{|T|} \\sum_{t\\in T} Z_t,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and the \\emph{empirical coordinate scales} $s_q, s_p \\geq 0$ are defined by\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:domain-std}\n        s_q = \\frac{1}{|T|} \\sum_{t \\in T} \\left\\lVert\\operatorname{pr}_q (Z_t - \\bar{Z})\\right\\rVert_{L^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R})}\n    \\end{align}\n    and\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:image-std}\n        s_p =\\frac{1}{|T|} \\sum_{t \\in T} \\left\\lVert\\operatorname{pr}_p (Z_t - \\bar{Z})\\right\\rVert_{L^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R})}.\n    \\end{align}",
