      "order": 85,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Price-Impact Curves",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:excess-supply-and-completed-graph}\n    Let $\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{T}(O)$ be a market state with supply curve $S^{\\bm{m}}:[0,s_*]\\to\\mathcal{P}$ and demand curve $D^{\\bm{m}}:[0,b_*]\\to\\mathcal{P}$, where $\\mathcal{P}=[-500,\\overline{p}]$. The \\emph{excess supply function} $E^{\\bm{m}}:\\mathbb{R}\\to[-b_*,s_*]$ is defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        E^{\\bm{m}}(p)\n        &=\\sup\\bigl(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{q\\in(0,s_*]\\mid S^{\\bm{m}}(q)\\leq p\\}\\bigr)\\\\\n        &\\quad-\\sup\\bigl(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{q\\in(0,b_*]\\mid D^{\\bm{m}}(q)\\geq p\\}\\bigr).\n    \\end{align*}\n    The \\emph{completed graph} of $E^{\\bm{m}}$ over $\\mathcal{P}$ is the set of points $(p,e)\\in\\mathcal{P}\\times\\mathbb{R}$ satisfying $E^{\\bm{m}}(p-)\\leq e\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p+)$, and for each level $q\\in[-b_*,s_*]$, the corresponding level set is\n    \\begin{align*}\n        J_{\\bm{m}}(q)=\\bigl\\{p\\in\\mathcal{P}\\bigm| E^{\\bm{m}}(p-)\\leq q\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p+)\\bigr\\}.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
