      "section": "Fixed-Grid Representation",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:fixed-grid-representative}\nFix $m=500$, set $h=1/m$, and define the grid nodes $u_j=j/m$ for $j\\in\\{-m,\\ldots,m\\}$ and the grid cells $K_j=[u_{j-1},u_j]$ for $j\\in\\{-m+1,\\ldots,m\\}$, yielding $1{,}001$ nodes ($500$ on each side of $u_0=0$) and $1{,}000$ cells of width $h$. For any selected square-integrable function pair $F=(\\psi,\\phi)\\in\\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)$, with the pointwise values of $\\psi$ at the nodes $(u_j)_{j=-m}^{m}$ included as part of the input, the \\emph{numerical representative} of $F$ is the pair of vectors\n\\begin{align}\\label{eq:501-node-representative}\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\mathsf{A}(F)=\\bigl((a_j)_{j=-m}^{m},(c_j)_{j=-m+1}^{m}\\bigr)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1001}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{1000},\\\\\na_j=\\psi(u_j),\\qquad c_j=\\frac{1}{h}\\int_{K_j}\\phi(u)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\end{align}\nBecause $a_j=\\psi(u_j)$ evaluates $\\psi$ on the finite node set, the quantity samples depend on the selected representative $\\psi$, whereas the price cell averages $c_j$ are invariant under almost-everywhere modifications of $\\phi$. In our curve application, $\\psi=\\psi_f$ is the continuous quantity coordinate of Definition~\\ref{defn:parametrisation-operator} and Section~\\ref{sec:piecewise-linear-curves}.",
