      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Functional Analysis",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:frechet-derivative}\n    Let $\\mathcal{H}$ and $\\mathcal{G}$ be real Hilbert spaces. Let $f: \\mathcal{H} \\to \\mathcal{G}$ be a function. The function $f$ is said to be \\emph{Fréchet} differentiable at $x \\in \\mathcal{H}$ if there exists a bounded linear operator $A \\in \\mathcal{L}(\\mathcal{H}, \\mathcal{G})$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\lim_{\\lVert h\\rVert\\to 0} \\frac{\\lVert f(x + h) - f(x) - A h\\rVert_{\\mathcal{G}}}{\\lVert h\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}}   = 0.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, we call $A$ the Fréchet derivative of $f$ at $x$ and we write $D f(x)=A$. If $f$ is differentiable at every $x \\in \\mathcal{H}$, we write\n    \\begin{align*}\n        Df &: \\mathcal{H} \\to \\mathcal{L}(\\mathcal{H}, \\mathcal{G})\\\\\n        x &\\mapsto D f(x).\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
