      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Valuation and Control",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:frechet-martingale}\n    Following the time-discrete theory of \\citet{sturm2002nonlinear}, let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},(\\mathcal{F}_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0},\\mathbb{P})$ be a filtered probability space and let $Z=(Z_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0}$ be an adapted process with values in a complete separable metric space $(E,d)$. Fix $z_0\\in E$, assume $\\mathbb{E}[d(Z_n,z_0)^2]<\\infty$ for every $n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$, and let $\\mathcal{L}^2(\\mathcal{F}_n;E)$ denote the set of $\\mathcal{F}_n$-measurable $E$-valued random elements $Y$ with $\\mathbb{E}[d(Y,z_0)^2]<\\infty$, modulo almost-sure equality. For every $Y\\in\\mathcal{L}^2(\\mathcal{F}_n;E)$, the loss $d(Z_{n+1},Y)^2$ is measurable and integrable. We call $Z$ a \\emph{Fr\\'echet martingale} if\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:frechet-martingale}\n        Z_n \\in \\argmin_{Y\\in\\mathcal{L}^2(\\mathcal{F}_n;E)} \\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[d(Z_{n+1},Y)^2\\right], \\qquad n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0.\n    \\end{align}",
      "status": "verified",
