      "section": "Relations and Functions",
      "statement": "{kunen2014set}}]\\label{defn:function}\n    Let $X$ and $Y$ be sets and let $f \\subseteq X\\times Y$ be a relation. Then $f$ is said to be a function if for every $x \\in X$ there is exactly one $y\\in Y$ such that $(x,y) \\in f$, and we write $f(x)$ for the unique $y$. In addition, we say that $f$ maps $X$ to $Y$, we also call $f$ a map, and we write $f:X\\to Y$.",
