      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Functional Analysis",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:inner-product}\\index{inner product}\n    Let $(V,+,\\cdot)$ be a real vector space, and let $\\langle \\cdot,\\cdot \\rangle:V \\times V \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be a function such that\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $\\langle u,v \\rangle = \\langle v,u \\rangle$ for all $u,v \\in V$;\n        \\item $\\langle a \\cdot u + b \\cdot v,w \\rangle = a\\langle u,w \\rangle + b\\langle v,w \\rangle$ for all $u,v,w \\in V$ and $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$;\n        \\item $\\langle v,v \\rangle \\geq 0$ for all $v \\in V$;\n        \\item $\\langle v,v \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $v$ is the zero vector of $V$.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Then $\\langle \\cdot,\\cdot \\rangle$ is called an inner product, and $(V,\\langle \\cdot,\\cdot \\rangle)$ is called an inner product space.",
      "status": "verified",
