      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Finite Measures and Random Measures",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:kallenberg-random-measure}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space and let $(X,d)$ be a complete and separable metric space with Borel $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal{B}(X)$. A map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi:\\Omega \\times \\mathcal{B}(X) \\to [0,\\infty]\n    \\end{align*}\n    is called a random measure if\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item for each $B \\in \\mathcal{B}(X)$, the partial map $\\omega \\mapsto \\xi(\\omega,B)$ is measurable;\n        \\item for each $\\omega \\in \\Omega$, the partial map $B \\mapsto \\xi(\\omega,B)$ is a finite measure on $(X,\\mathcal{B}(X))$.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    If in addition $B \\mapsto \\xi(\\omega,B)$ is integer-valued for every $\\omega$, then $\\xi$ is called an integer-valued random measure, or (finite) point process, as in Chapter~\\ref{chap:stochastic-markets}. Its values $\\xi(\\omega,\\cdot)$ lie in $\\mathcal{N}(X)$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
