      "section": "Product Measures, Kernels, and Disintegration",
      "statement": "{kallenberg2017random}}] \\label{defn:markov-kernel}\\index{Markov kernel}\n    Let $(S,\\mathcal{S})$ and $(T,\\mathcal{T})$ be measurable spaces. A map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        Q:S\\times \\mathcal{T} \\to [0,1]\n    \\end{align*}\n    is called a Markov kernel from $S$ to $T$ if\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item for each $s \\in S$, the map $B \\mapsto Q(s,B)$ is a probability measure on $(T,\\mathcal{T})$;\n        \\item for each $B \\in \\mathcal{T}$, the map $s \\mapsto Q(s,B)$ is $\\mathcal{S}/\\mathcal{B}([0,1])$-measurable.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
