      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Measurable Spaces and Measures",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:measure}\\index{probability measure}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F})$ be a measurable space. A map $\\mu:\\mathcal{F} \\to [0,\\infty]$ is called a measure if\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $\\mu(\\emptyset)=0$;\n        \\item for every pairwise disjoint sequence $(E_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}$,\n        \\begin{align*}\n            \\mu\\left(\\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} E_n\\right) = \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mu(E_n).\n        \\end{align*}\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Then $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ is called a measure space. If in addition $\\mu(\\Omega)=1$, then $\\mu$ is called a probability measure and $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ is called a probability space.",
