      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Metric Spaces",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:metric}\n    Let $X$ be a set, and let $d:X \\times X \\to [0,\\infty]$ be a function such that for every $x,x^\\prime,x^\\ast \\in X$:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $d(x,x^\\prime) = 0$ if and only if $x = x^\\prime$; \\label{eq:metric-1}\n        \\item $d(x,x^\\prime) = d(x^\\prime,x)$; \\label{eq:metric-2}\n        \\item $d(x,x^\\prime) \\leq d(x,x^\\ast) + d(x^\\ast,x^\\prime)$. \\label{eq:metric-3}\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Then $d$ is called a metric, and $(X,d)$ is called a metric space.",
