      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 131,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "The Axiom of Choice and Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:ordered-sets}\n    Let $X$ be a set and let $\\leq \\subseteq X\\times X$ be a relation such that, for all $x, x^\\prime, x^\\ast \\in X$:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $x \\leq x$,\n        \\item $x \\leq x^\\prime$ and $x^\\prime \\leq x$ implies $x = x^\\prime$, and\n        \\item $x \\leq x^\\prime$ and $x^\\prime \\leq x^\\ast$ implies $x\\leq x^\\ast$.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Then $(X,\\leq)$ is called a partially ordered set. Now, let\n    \\begin{align*}\n         \\mathord{<}\\;:=\\;\\leq \\setminus \\{(x,x) \\mid x \\in X\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If, in addition, $\\leq$ satisfies\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\addtocounter{enumi}{3}\n        \\item $x \\neq x^\\prime$ implies $x < x^\\prime$ or $x^\\prime < x$,\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    then we call $(X, \\leq)$ a totally ordered set and we say that $\\leq$ is a total order. Finally, if $(X,\\leq)$ is a totally ordered set in which each non-empty subset has a least element, i.e.,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\forall S\\left((S\\subseteq X \\land S\\neq\\emptyset) \\implies \\exists x\\in S \\forall x^\\prime \\in S(x\\leq x^\\prime)\\right),\n    \\end{align*}\n    then we call $(X,\\leq)$ a well-ordered set and we say that $\\leq$ is a well-order.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
