      "section": "Metric Spaces",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:pseudometric}\\index{pseudometric}\n    Let $X$ be a set, and let $d:X \\times X \\to [0,\\infty]$ be a function such that for every $x,x^\\prime,x^\\ast \\in X$:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $d(x,x) = 0$; \\label{eq:pseudometric-1}\n        \\item $d(x,x^\\prime) = d(x^\\prime,x)$; \\label{eq:pseudometric-2}\n        \\item $d(x,x^\\prime) \\leq d(x,x^\\ast) + d(x^\\ast,x^\\prime)$. \\label{eq:pseudometric-3}\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Then $d$ is called a pseudometric, and $(X,d)$ is called a pseudometric space.",
