      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:sorting-finite-sets}\nLet $(X,\\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set and let $\\prec$ denote its strict part. Lift $\\prec$ to a strict partial order on $\\mathbb{N} \\times X$ by defining\n\\begin{align*}\n    (n, x) \\prec (m,y) \\iff x \\prec y.  \n\\end{align*}\nMoreover, let $\\mathscr{L}(X)$ be the set of labelled finite sets. Let $\\varsigma^\\ast : \\mathscr{L}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{N} \\times X)$ be defined recursively, with $\\varsigma^\\ast(\\emptyset) = \\bm{e}$. If $\\xi \\neq \\emptyset$, set $\\xi^{(1)} = \\xi$ and, for $n \\in \\{1, \\dots, |\\xi|\\}$, define\n\\begin{align*}\n    M^{(n)} &= \\{(i,x) \\in \\xi^{(n)} \\mid \\not\\exists (j,y) \\in \\xi^{(n)}: (j,y) \\prec  (i,x)\\}\n\\end{align*}\nand\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\varsigma^\\ast(\\xi)(n) &= \\argmin_{(i,x) \\in M^{(n)}} i,\n\\end{align*}\nwith \n\\begin{align*}\n    \\xi^{(n+1)} = \\xi^{(n)} \\setminus \\{\\varsigma^\\ast(\\xi)(n)\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nThe set $M^{(n)}$ is non-empty because every finite non-empty partially ordered set has a minimal element, and the displayed $\\argmin$ is taken over the finite non-empty set of natural indices appearing in $M^{(n)}$; it is a single element by Lemma~\\ref{lem:labelled-finite-set-natural-projection-injective}. The output is sorted. Indeed, if $p < q$ and $\\varsigma^\\ast(\\xi)(q) \\prec \\varsigma^\\ast(\\xi)(p)$, then at step $p$ the element chosen at step $q$ was still available and strictly below the element chosen at step $p$, contradicting the minimality of that choice. We call $\\varsigma^\\ast$ the sorting map for labelled finite sets. Likewise, let $\\Pi_X$ and $\\Pi_{\\mathbb{N}}$ be the projections onto $X$ and $\\mathbb{N}$ respectively. Then define the mapping $\\varsigma:\\mathscr{L}(X) \\to \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq)$ by\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\varsigma(\\xi)(n) = \\Pi_X(\\varsigma^\\ast(\\xi)(n)),\n\\end{align*}\nand likewise, we define the mapping $\\pi:\\mathscr{L}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{N})$ by\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\pi(\\xi)(n) = \\Pi_{\\mathbb{N}}(\\varsigma^\\ast(\\xi)(n)).\n\\end{align*}",
