      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{defn:sorting-map}\\index{sorting map}\n    For a partially ordered set $(X,\\preceq)$ let $\\mathscr{T}(X)$ and $\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ be the sets of tuples and of sorted tuples, respectively. Any function $\\varsigma:\\mathscr{T}(X)\\to \\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ such that, for every $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$, there exists a bijective map $\\pi:\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}) \\to \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$ such that\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:sort-permute}\n        \\varsigma(\\bm{x}) = \\bm{x} \\circ \\pi\n    \\end{align}\n    is called a sorting map.",
