      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 188,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Structure of Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:adm-static}\\index{admissible orders}\\index{static market}\n    Consider a simple static market, in which each order can be represented by $(p, q)$ where $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is the price\\footnote{Classically, one often assumes prices to be positive, but in power markets prices may be negative. Real venues also impose product-specific price ticks and admissible price ranges; for European spot power examples, see \\citet{epex2026tradingproducts,nemocommitteeSIDC}.} and $q \\in [0,\\infty)$ is the quantity\\footnote{Like prices, actual order quantities are venue- and product-specific and may be restricted by minimum volume increments or lot sizes \\citep{epex2026tradingproducts,nemocommitteeSIDC}.}. Each order can be classified as a buy or sell order, such that $((p,q), \\kappa)$ is an order with direction $\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}$. As such, the space of admissible orders may be given by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}} \\mathbb{R}\\times [0,\\infty).\n    \\end{align*}\n    However, in this example, there is no difference between using disjoint unions or the product space\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{R}\\times [0,\\infty) \\times \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Indeed, one defines the map \n    \\begin{align*}\n        ((p,q), \\kappa) \\mapsto (p,q,\\kappa),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which is easily shown to be an isomorphism. In this example, one may view the disjoint union as a nuisance, but, as we shall later see, it has advantages.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
