      "section": "Structure of Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:agent-adm-static}\nWe now consider a slight modification of Example~\\ref{exmp:adm-static}, where one considers a set of agents, $A$, who can engage with the market. Suppose that the admissible orders of each agent are limited in price and quantity based on direction. The corresponding bounds may vary across agents.\n\n    Such a scenario may be covered by letting $P_{\\kappa, a}\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $Q_{\\kappa, a}\\subseteq [0,\\infty)$ be the sets of potential admissible prices and quantities for agent $a\\in A$ when taking positions in direction $\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}$. Therefore, each order can be seen as an element of the space \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{(\\kappa, a) \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}\\times A} P_{\\kappa, a} \\times Q_{\\kappa, a}. \n    \\end{align*}\n    The mapping\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:agent-adm-static-inj}\n        ((p,q),(\\kappa, a)) \\mapsto (p,q,\\kappa, a)\n    \\end{align}\n    is generally no longer a bijection, although it is still injective. If it is not a bijection, then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{(\\kappa, a) \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}\\times A} P_{\\kappa, a} \\times Q_{\\kappa, a} \\subsetneq \\mathbb{R}\\times [0,\\infty) \\times \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}\\times A,\n    \\end{align*}\n    where the strict inclusion is meant under the map \\eqref{eq:agent-adm-static-inj}.",
