      "order": 191,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Ordering the Market Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:completing-order-agent-based-static-market}\\index{order completion}\n    We continue from Example~\\ref{exmp:agent-adm-static}, in which we considered the orders as elements of the set \n    \\begin{align*}\n        O = \\bigsqcup_{(\\kappa, a) \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}\\times A} P_{\\kappa, a} \\times Q_{\\kappa, a},  \n    \\end{align*}\n    where $A$ is the set of agents in the market, and $P_{\\kappa,a}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}$ is the set of permissible prices in direction $\\kappa\\in\\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}$ for agent $a\\in A$, and similarly $Q_{\\kappa,a}\\subseteq [0,\\infty)$ is the set of permissible quantities in direction $\\kappa\\in\\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}$ for agent $a\\in A$. Notice that attempting to follow a construction analogous to that in Example~\\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market} is not satisfactory; endowing $\\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}\\times A$ with a total order has the adverse consequence of favouring certain agents in the market. To rectify this, one may consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $O$ defined by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        ((p,q),(\\kappa, a)) \\sim ((p^\\prime,q^\\prime), (\\kappa^\\prime, a^\\prime)) \\iff ((p,q),\\kappa) = ((p^\\prime,q^\\prime), \\kappa^\\prime),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which states that two orders are equivalent if their price, quantity, and direction coincide, regardless of the agent that submitted the order. It is therefore natural for the market to consider the quotient space\n    \\begin{align*}\n        O_{\\sim} = \\{[((p,q),\\kappa,a)]_{\\sim} \\mid ((p,q), \\kappa, a) \\in O\\}, \n    \\end{align*}\n    where \n    \\begin{align*}\n        [((p,q),\\kappa,a)]_{\\sim} = \\{((p^\\ast, q^\\ast), (\\kappa^\\ast, a^\\ast)) \\in O  \\mid ((p^\\ast, q^\\ast), (\\kappa^\\ast, a^\\ast))\\sim ((p,q),(\\kappa,a))\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\pi&:O_\\sim \\to \\bigsqcup_{\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}} \\left(\\bigcup_{a \\in A} P_{\\kappa, a} \\times Q_{\\kappa, a}\\right)\\\\\n        [((p,q),(\\kappa,a))]_{\\sim} &\\mapsto ((p,q),\\kappa)\n    \\end{align*}\n    is well-defined and bijective. Therefore, one can conclude that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        O_{\\sim} \\cong \\bigsqcup_{\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}} \\left(\\bigcup_{a \\in A} P_{\\kappa, a} \\times Q_{\\kappa, a}\\right) \\subseteq \\bigsqcup_{\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}} \\mathbb{R}\\times [0,\\infty).\n    \\end{align*}\nPulling back the lexicographic order of Example~\\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market} through $\\pi$ yields a total order on $O_{\\sim}$.",
