      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Ordering the Market Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:partial-order-static-market}\n    Let us consider the setting of Example~\\ref{exmp:adm-static}. Now, define \n    \\begin{align*}\n        (p,q) \\preceq_{\\mathrm{buy}} (p^\\prime, q^\\prime) \\iff (p > p^\\prime) \\lor ((p = p^\\prime) \\land (q \\geq q^\\prime)), \n    \\end{align*}\n    which states that a buy order is ``better'' if the price per unit is greater, or if the prices are equal and more volume is provided. Similarly, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        (p,q) \\preceq_{\\mathrm{sell}} (p^\\prime, q^\\prime) \\iff (p < p^\\prime) \\lor ((p = p^\\prime) \\land (q \\geq q^\\prime)), \n    \\end{align*}\n    that is, a sell order is ``better'' if the price per unit is smaller or the price per unit is equal but more volume is provided. The orderings rank orders by priority, and in both cases the order on the left-hand side has priority over the one on the right-hand side. The resulting ordering on the disjoint union allows us to compare orders of the same type, but buy and sell orders are, once again, incomparable.",
      "status": "verified",
