      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Sided Parametrised Curves",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:sided-average-not-canonical}\n    Consider the increasing simple curves\n    \\begin{align*}\n        f:[0,2]&\\to\\mathbb{R},\n        &f(x)&=\n        \\begin{cases}\n            0 & 0\\leq x<1,\\\\\n            2 & 1\\leq x\\leq2,\n        \\end{cases}\\\\\n        g:[0,3]&\\to\\mathbb{R},\n        &g(x)&=\n        \\begin{cases}\n            0 & 0\\leq x<1,\\\\\n            1 & 1\\leq x<2,\\\\\n            3 & 2\\leq x\\leq3.\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    On $[0,1]$, the sided parametrising functions are $\\psi_f(u)=2u$ and $\\psi_g(u)=3u$. Hence the first component of the pointwise average\n    \\begin{align*}\n        z=\\frac{1}{2}\\Psi(f)+\\frac{1}{2}\\Psi(g)\n    \\end{align*}\n    is\n    \\begin{align*}\n        q(u)=\\frac{5}{2}u.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The second component has four levels and changes at\n    \\begin{align*}\n        u=\\frac{1}{3},\\qquad u=\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad u=\\frac{2}{3}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The pair $z$ belongs to $\\mathcal{A}$. Its decoded curve $h$ has four blocks on $[0,5/2]$, with endpoints\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\frac{5}{6},\\qquad \\frac{5}{4},\\qquad \\frac{5}{3},\\qquad \\frac{5}{2}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Because $h$ has four positive-side blocks, its canonical sided parametrisation assigns its first endpoint $5/6$ to parameter value $1/4$. Consequently,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\psi_h\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{5}{6},\n        \\qquad\n        q\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{5}{8}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus $\\Psi(h)\\neq z$. Since $z$ traces the graph of the uniquely decoded curve $h$, it cannot equal $\\Psi(k)$ for any other $k\\in\\mathpzc{P}$. Therefore $z\\notin\\Psi(\\mathpzc{P})$.\n\n    We must also rule out equality after passage to $L^2$ equivalence classes. Suppose, to the contrary, that $\\overline{\\Psi}(k)=[z]$ for some $k\\in\\mathpzc{P}$. The first components $\\psi_k$ and $q$ are continuous and equal almost everywhere, and are therefore equal everywhere. On $[0,1]$, the function $q$ is strictly increasing. Equality of the second components almost everywhere therefore implies that $k$ has the same four positive-length blocks and the same block endpoints as $h$. Its block-based sided parametriser must consequently satisfy\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\psi_k\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{5}{6}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    This contradicts $\\psi_k=q$ and $q(1/4)=5/8$. Hence $[z]\\notin\\overline{\\Psi}(\\mathpzc{P})$, so the Hilbert-valued image is not convex either.",
