      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:sort-discont}\\index{sorting!discontinuity}\n    Let $\\mathbb{R}^2$ carry the Euclidean metric and let $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R}^2)$ carry the induced tuple Hausdorff metric, and let $\\preceq$ be the lexicographic ordering on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ defined by  \n    \\begin{align*}\n        (x,y) \\preceq (x^\\prime, y^\\prime) \\iff x < x^\\prime \\lor ((x =x^\\prime) \\land (y \\leq y^\\prime)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    The order is total and, as a result, the sorting map is unique. For $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$, define the sequence \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}_n = i \\mapsto \\begin{cases}\n            (0, 1) & i =1\\\\ \n            \\left(\\frac{1}{n}, 0\\right) &i =2\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    and notice that $\\bm{x}_n \\in \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}^2, \\preceq)$ by construction. However, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\bm{x}_n = \\bm{x}= i \\mapsto \\begin{cases}\n            (0, 1) & i = 1 \\\\ \n            (0, 0) & i = 2,\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    but clearly $\\bm{x}\\notin\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}^2,\\preceq)$. Moreover, since $\\varsigma$ acts as the identity on $\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}^2, \\preceq)$, it follows that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x} =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\bm{x}_n =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\varsigma(\\bm{x}_n) \\neq \\varsigma(\\lim_{n\\to \\infty}\\bm{x}_n) = \\varsigma(\\bm{x}) =i \\mapsto\\begin{cases}\n            (0,0) &i = 1\\\\ \n            (0, 1) & i =2.\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}",
