      "section": "Ordering the Market Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{exmp:total-order-dynamic-market}\n    We continue from Example~\\ref{exmp:adm-dyn}, in which we considered the orders as elements of the set \n    \\begin{align*}\n        O = \\bigsqcup_{\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},~\\mathrm{sell}\\}} \\mathbb{R}\\times [0,\\infty) \\times [0,\\infty),  \n    \\end{align*}\n    where the components of each $((p,q,t), \\kappa) \\in O$ represent the price, $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$, quantity, $q \\in [0,\\infty)$, time of submission, $t \\in [0,\\infty)$, and direction $\\kappa \\in \\{\\mathrm{buy},\\mathrm{sell}\\}$. Now, define the orders \n    \\begin{align*}\n        &(p,q,t) \\preceq_{\\mathrm{buy}} (p^\\prime, q^\\prime, t^\\prime) \\iff\\\\ &(p > p^\\prime) \\lor ((p = p^\\prime) \\land (t < t^\\prime)) \\lor ((p = p^\\prime) \\land (t = t^\\prime) \\land (q \\geq q^\\prime)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which ranks a buy order $(p,q,t)$ ahead of $(p^\\prime, q^\\prime, t^\\prime)$ if it has a higher price; at equal prices, if it was submitted earlier; and at equal prices and times, if it is for a larger quantity. Similarly, we define \n    \\begin{align*}\n        &(p,q, t) \\preceq_{\\mathrm{sell}} (p^\\prime, q^\\prime,t^\\prime) \\iff \\\\\n        &(p < p^\\prime) \\lor ((p = p^\\prime) \\land (t < t^\\prime)) \\lor ((p = p^\\prime) \\land (t = t^\\prime) \\land (q \\geq q^\\prime)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    where sell orders are ranked increasingly according to price. That is, the ``best'' sell order is the one with the smallest price, but otherwise it is constructed analogously to the buy order. Endow $\\{\\mathrm{buy}, ~\\mathrm{sell}\\}$ with the order $\\leq$ of Example~\\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market}, for which\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\kappa \\leq \\kappa^\\prime \\iff \\kappa = \\kappa^\\prime \\lor (\\kappa = \\mathrm{buy} \\land \\kappa^\\prime = \\mathrm{sell}).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\leq, \\preceq_{\\mathrm{buy}}$, and  $\\preceq_{\\mathrm{sell}}$ are all total orders, we have that the lexicographic order defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (o, \\kappa) \\preceq (o^\\prime, \\kappa^\\prime) \\iff \\kappa < \\kappa^\\prime \\lor (\\kappa = \\kappa^\\prime \\land o \\preceq_\\kappa o^\\prime), \n    \\end{align*}\n    is total, where $<$ is the strict part of $\\leq$ and $(o,\\kappa),(o^\\prime, \\kappa^\\prime) \\in O$.",
