            "text": "We must show that $L^{i,j}_{n,m} \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n \\times \\mathbb{R}^m)$ and $G^{i,j}_{n,m} \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n \\times \\mathbb{R}^m)$. Analogously to the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable}, define the function $\\Lambda_{i,j} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\times \\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Lambda_{i,j}(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) = \\bm{y}(j) - \\bm{x}(i),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which for each $i \\in \\{1,\\dots, n\\}$ and $j \\in \\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ is a continuous function and hence Borel measurable. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        L_{n,m}^{i,j} = \\Lambda_{i,j}^{-1}([0,\\infty)) \\cap (I_n\\times D_m),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and likewise\n    \\begin{align*}\n        G_{n,m}^{i,j} = \\Lambda_{i,j}^{-1}((-\\infty,0)) \\cap (I_n \\times D_m).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Both sets are Borel measurable, since $(I_n \\times D_m) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n)\\otimes\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^m) =\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n \\times \\mathbb{R}^m)$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable}, and each $\\Lambda_{i,j}$ is continuous and hence Borel measurable. Because the intersection of two Borel measurable sets is Borel measurable, the claim follows, as desired.",
