        "text": "We establish the norm decomposition by expanding the squared distance around $\\hat{y}_S$. Fix an arbitrary $\\hat{y}\\in\\mathcal{H}$. Writing $y_i-\\hat{y}=(y_i-\\hat{y}_S)+(\\hat{y}_S-\\hat{y})$ for each $i\\in S$ and expanding the squared Hilbert norm yields\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\sum_{i\\in S}\\lVert y_i-\\hat{y}\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^2\n    =\\sum_{i\\in S}\\lVert y_i-\\hat{y}_S\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^2\n    +2\\left\\langle\\sum_{i\\in S}(y_i-\\hat{y}_S),\\,\\hat{y}_S-\\hat{y}\\right\\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}}\n    +|S|\\lVert\\hat{y}-\\hat{y}_S\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^2.\n\\end{align*}\nBy Definition~\\ref{defn:cell-impurity-split}, $\\sum_{i\\in S}(y_i-\\hat{y}_S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}y_i-|S|\\hat{y}_S=0$, so the inner-product cross term vanishes and the stated identity holds. Since $|S|>0$ and $\\lVert\\hat{y}-\\hat{y}_S\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^2\\geq 0$ with equality if and only if $\\hat{y}=\\hat{y}_S$, it follows that $\\hat{y}_S$ is the unique minimiser of $\\sum_{i\\in S}\\lVert y_i-\\hat{y}\\rVert_{\\mathcal{H}}^2$ over $\\hat{y}\\in\\mathcal{H}$."
