      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{lem:disintegration-lemma}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a complete and separable metric space, and let $(\\mathcal{N}(X), \\rho)$ be the associated Prokhorov space of finite integer-valued measures on $(X, \\mathcal{B}(X))$. Let $\\xi \\in \\mathcal{N}(X)$ be a non-zero measure with atomic decomposition \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi = \\sum_{k\\leq \\kappa} \\beta_k \\delta_{x_k}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Let \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\gamma = \\begin{cases}\n            1, & \\kappa = 1,\\\\\n            \\min\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\min_{\\substack{k, k^\\prime \\leq \\kappa \\\\ k \\neq k^\\prime}} d(x_k, x_{k^\\prime}), 1 \\right), & \\kappa \\geq 2,\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    and $0 < \\varepsilon < \\gamma$. Let $\\eta \\in \\mathcal{N}(X)$ be another measure with atomic decomposition \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\eta = \\sum_{l\\leq \\lambda} \\alpha_l \\delta_{y_l}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $\\rho(\\xi, \\eta) < \\varepsilon$, then $\\eta\\neq0$ by \\eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-3} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-point-process}, and the sets $L_k = \\{l\\in\\{1,\\dots,\\lambda\\} \\mid d(x_k,y_l)<\\varepsilon\\}$, $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,\\kappa\\}$, satisfy:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item for every $k \\in \\{1,\\dots,\\kappa\\}$, $\\beta_k = \\sum_{l \\in L_k} \\alpha_l$; \n        \\item for all distinct $k, k^\\prime \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\kappa\\}$, $L_k \\cap L_{k^\\prime} = \\emptyset$;\n        \\item for every $k \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\kappa\\}$, it holds that $\\max_{l \\in L_k} d(x_k, y_l) < \\varepsilon$; and \n        \\item the sets $(L_k)_{k\\leq \\kappa}$ cover the atoms of $\\eta$, i.e.\n        \\begin{align*}\n            \\bigcup_{k\\leq \\kappa} L_k = \\{1,\\dots,\\lambda\\}.\n        \\end{align*}\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 141,
        "references": [
          "lem:helpful-point-process",
          "eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2",
          "cor:measures-coincide"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/lem__disintegration-lemma__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, note that since $(X,d)$ is a metric space, the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra contains all singletons as well as all open balls $B_\\varepsilon(x) = \\{x^\\prime \\in X \\mid d(x,x^\\prime) < \\varepsilon\\}$. \n\n    We prove the properties simultaneously; let $k \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\kappa\\}$ be arbitrary, and note that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\{x_k\\} \\in \\mathcal{B}(X)\n    \\end{align*}\n    and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\{x_k\\}^\\varepsilon = B_\\varepsilon(x_k) \\in \\mathcal{B}(X).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Recall that $\\rho(\\xi, \\eta) < \\varepsilon < 1$ implies that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi(B) \\leq \\eta(B^\\varepsilon),\n    \\end{align*}\n     by Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-point-process}. In particular, the above yields the bound  \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi(\\{x_k\\}) = \\beta_k \\leq \\eta(B_\\varepsilon(x_k)),\n    \\end{align*} \n    for all $k \\leq \\kappa$. In addition, since $\\varepsilon < \\gamma$ it holds that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\eta(B_\\varepsilon(x_k)) \\leq \\xi((B_{\\varepsilon}(x_k))^{\\varepsilon}) \\leq \\xi(B_{2\\varepsilon}(x_k)) = \\beta_k, \n    \\end{align*}\n    where the first inequality is \\eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-point-process}, the second follows from $(B_{\\varepsilon}(x_k))^{\\varepsilon}\\subseteq B_{2\\varepsilon}(x_k)$ by the triangle inequality, and the equality holds because $2\\varepsilon<2\\gamma\\leq d(x_k,x_{k^\\prime})$ for every $k^\\prime\\neq k$;\n    and hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta_k = \\eta(B_\\varepsilon(x_k)). \n    \\end{align*}\n    Consequently, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta_k = \\sum_{l \\leq \\lambda}\\alpha_l \\delta_{y_l}(B_\\varepsilon(x_k)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and hence, we may define \n    \\begin{align*}\n        L_k = \\{l \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\lambda\\} \\mid \\delta_{y_l}(B_\\varepsilon(x_k)) = 1 \\}. \n    \\end{align*}\n    Note that by construction,   \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\delta_{y_l}(B_\\varepsilon(x_k)) = 1 \\iff d(y_l, x_k) < \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n    as desired. Next, let $k \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\kappa\\}$ and $l \\in L_k$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x_k, y_l) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $k^\\prime \\neq k$, then by the triangle inequality and the definition of $\\gamma$ we have \n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x_{k^\\prime}, y_l) \\geq d(x_k, x_{k^\\prime}) - d(x_k, y_l) > 2\\gamma - \\varepsilon > \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n    which implies $l \\notin L_{k^\\prime}$ for $k^\\prime \\neq k$.\n\n    It remains to show that the sets $L_k$ cover all atoms of $\\eta$. By the first conclusion and disjointness,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\sum_{k\\leq \\kappa}\\beta_k\n        = \\sum_{k\\leq \\kappa}\\sum_{l\\in L_k}\\alpha_l\n        = \\sum_{l\\in \\cup_{k\\leq \\kappa} L_k}\\alpha_l.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\rho(\\xi,\\eta)<\\varepsilon<1$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-point-process} gives $\\xi(X)=\\eta(X)$, equivalently $\\sum_{k\\leq\\kappa}\\beta_k=\\sum_{l\\leq\\lambda}\\alpha_l$ as in Corollary~\\ref{cor:measures-coincide}. All multiplicities $\\alpha_l$ are positive, so no index $l\\in\\{1,\\dots,\\lambda\\}$ can be missing from $\\cup_{k\\leq\\kappa}L_k$."
      },
