      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{lem:helpful-labelled}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and let $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$ be the set of labelled finite sets with metric $d_H^\\ast$ defined in Theorem~\\ref{thm:labelled-properties}. Then, for every $\\xi,\\eta \\in \\mathscr{L}(X)$\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H^\\ast(\\xi, \\eta) < 1 \\implies |\\xi| = |\\eta|.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, for every $r \\in (0,1]$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H^\\ast(\\xi, \\eta) < r \\implies \\forall (n,x) \\in \\xi\\ \\exists x^\\prime \\in X: (d(x,x^\\prime) < r) \\land (n, x^\\prime) \\in \\eta.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 813,
        "references": [
          "eq:bound-labelled-1",
          "eq:bound-labelled-2",
          "eq:labelled-conseq-1",
          "eq:labelled-conseq-2"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/lem__helpful-labelled__proof.svg",
        "text": "For the first property, note that if either $\\xi$ or $\\eta$ is the empty set, then $d_H^\\ast(\\xi, \\eta) < 1$ immediately implies $\\xi = \\eta = \\emptyset$ as it is otherwise infinitely far from other elements by definition. Therefore, it suffices to show the properties for $\\xi \\neq \\emptyset$ and $\\eta \\neq \\emptyset$. Now let $r \\in (0,1]$. Then\n    \\begin{align}\n        d_H^\\ast(\\xi, \\eta) < r\n        &\\implies \\max_{(n,x)\\in \\xi}\\min_{(n^\\prime,x^\\prime)\\in \\eta}\n        d^\\ast((n,x), (n^\\prime, x^\\prime)) \\notag\\\\\n        &\\quad =\\max_{(n,x)\\in \\xi}\\min_{(n^\\prime,x^\\prime)\\in \\eta}\n        \\left(d(x,x^\\prime) + |n-n^\\prime|\\right) < r.\n        \\label{eq:bound-labelled-1}\n    \\end{align}\n    Consequently, the inequality is equivalent to\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:labelled-conseq-1}\n        \\forall (n,x)\\in \\xi\\ \\exists(n^\\prime, x^\\prime) \\in \\eta : (d(x,x^\\prime) < r) \\land(n = n^\\prime),\n    \\end{align}\n    since $r \\leq 1$ and $|n - n^\\prime| < 1$ if and only if $n = n^\\prime$. Similarly, \n    \\begin{align}\n        \\max_{(n^\\prime,x^\\prime)\\in \\eta}\\min_{(n,x)\\in \\xi}\n        d^\\ast((n,x), (n^\\prime, x^\\prime)) \\notag\\\\\n        \\quad =\\max_{(n^\\prime,x^\\prime)\\in \\eta}\\min_{(n,x)\\in \\xi}\n        \\left(d(x,x^\\prime) + |n-n^\\prime|\\right) < r\n        \\label{eq:bound-labelled-2}\n    \\end{align}\n    if and only if\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:labelled-conseq-2}\n        \\forall (n^\\prime, x^\\prime)\\in \\eta\\ \\exists(n,x) \\in \\xi : (d(x,x^\\prime) < r) \\land(n = n^\\prime).\n    \\end{align}\n    Note that by definition, when $d_H^\\ast(\\xi,\\eta)<r$, then both \\eqref{eq:bound-labelled-1} and \\eqref{eq:bound-labelled-2} hold. For $r = 1$, \\eqref{eq:labelled-conseq-1} and \\eqref{eq:labelled-conseq-2} show that $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ use the same set of labels $n$. Since each label occurs at most once in an element of $\\mathscr{L}(X)$, each of $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ has as many elements as its label set, and hence $|\\xi| = |\\eta|$. Moreover, \\eqref{eq:labelled-conseq-1} is precisely the second property."
      },
