        "source": "A - appendices/analysis.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/lem__regular-paths-bounded-borel__proof.svg",
        "text": "We first show boundedness. Fix $x_0\\in X$ and suppose, for contradiction, that $d(x_0,f(t_k))\\to\\infty$ along a sequence $(t_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\subseteq[a,b]$. Since $[a,b]$ is compact, we may pass to a monotone subsequence converging to some $t\\in[a,b]$, and since $f(t)$ is a single point, we may further assume that the subsequence is strictly monotone. If it is increasing, the left limit $f(t-)$ exists, and if it is decreasing, the right limit $f(t+)$ exists: a c\\`adl\\`ag function has left limits and is right-continuous, a c\\`agl\\`ad function has right limits and is left-continuous, and a continuous function has both one-sided limits. In either case $d(x_0,f(t_k))$ converges to the finite number $d(x_0,f(t\\mp))$, a contradiction.\n\n    We now show Borel measurability. Let $f\\in D^+([a,b];X)$. For $m\\in\\mathbb{N}$ put $t_j=a+j(b-a)/m$ for $j\\in\\{0,\\dots,m\\}$, and define $f_m(t)=f(t_j)$ for $t\\in[t_{j-1},t_j)$ with $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, together with $f_m(b)=f(b)$. Each $f_m$ takes finitely many values, each on a Borel subset of $[a,b]$, and is therefore Borel measurable. For $t\\in[a,b)$ the node $t_j$ chosen at stage $m$ satisfies $t<t_j\\leq t+(b-a)/m$, so $t_j\\downarrow t$ and right-continuity gives $f_m(t)\\to f(t)$; at $t=b$ the sequence is constant. A pointwise limit of Borel measurable maps into a metric space is Borel measurable, since for an open set $U\\subseteq X$ and $U_k=\\{x\\in X\\mid d(x,X\\setminus U)>1/k\\}$ one has $\\{t\\mid f(t)\\in U\\}=\\bigcup_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\bigcup_{N\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\bigcap_{m\\geq N}\\{t\\mid f_m(t)\\in U_k\\}$. Hence $f$ is Borel measurable. For $f\\in D^-([a,b];X)$ the same argument applies with the cells $(t_{j-1},t_j]$, the values $f_m(t)=f(t_{j-1})$, $f_m(a)=f(a)$, and left-continuity, and a continuous $f$ is Borel measurable as a continuous map."
