      "statement": "\\label{lem:sorting-valid-first-choice-partition-covers}\n    Let $(X, \\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set, and let $\\varphi:\\mathscr{P} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ be a bijection. Define $(\\mathscr{U}_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{U}_n = \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(n)}} \\setminus \\bigcup_{\\substack{m \\in \\mathbb{N}\\\\ m < n}} \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(m)}},\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $\\pi_{(n)}$ is defined by $\\varphi^{-1}(n)$. Then, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{T}(X) = \\bigcup_{n\\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathscr{U}_n,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and the sets $\\mathscr{U}_n$ are pairwise disjoint.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1798,
        "references": [
          "cor:tuple-space-covered-by-sorting-valid-elements"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/lem__sorting-valid-first-choice-partition-covers__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that $\\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathscr{U}_n = \\mathscr{T}(X)$ and that $\\mathscr{U}_n \\cap \\mathscr{U}_k = \\emptyset$ for all $n, k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\neq k$.\n\n    First, since $\\mathscr{U}_n \\subseteq \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(n)}} \\subseteq \\mathscr{T}(X)$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, it holds that $\\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathscr{U}_n \\subseteq \\mathscr{T}(X)$. For the reverse inclusion, let $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ be arbitrary. By Corollary~\\ref{cor:tuple-space-covered-by-sorting-valid-elements}, the family $(\\mathscr{V}_\\pi)_{\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}}$ covers $\\mathscr{T}(X)$, and since $\\varphi : \\mathscr{P} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ is bijective, the set of indices\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_{\\bm{x}} = \\big\\{k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\;\\big|\\; \\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(k)}}\\big\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a non-empty subset of $\\mathbb{N}$. By the well-ordering principle on $\\mathbb{N}$, the set $I_{\\bm{x}}$ has a least element $n = \\min I_{\\bm{x}}$. By definition of $n$, we have $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(n)}}$ and $\\bm{x} \\notin \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(m)}}$ for every $m < n$. Consequently,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(n)}} \\setminus \\bigcup_{m < n} \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(m)}} = \\mathscr{U}_n \\subseteq \\bigcup_{k \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathscr{U}_k,\n    \\end{align*}\n    which proves that $\\mathscr{T}(X) = \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathscr{U}_n$.\n\n    Next, let $n, k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\neq k$, and assume without loss of generality that $m = k < n$. By construction, $\\mathscr{U}_k \\subseteq \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(k)}}$, whereas $\\mathscr{U}_n$ is disjoint from $\\bigcup_{m < n} \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(m)}}$ and hence disjoint from $\\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(k)}}$. It follows that $\\mathscr{U}_n \\cap \\mathscr{U}_k = \\emptyset$, as desired."
      },
