      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Aggregation and Executable Volume",
      "statement": "\\label{lem:state-functionals-permutation-invariant}\n    Let $O=\\bigsqcup_{\\kappa\\in\\{\\mathrm{buy},\\mathrm{sell}\\}}\\mathbb{R}\\times[0,\\infty)$, and let $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}:\\mathscr{T}(O)\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}:\\mathscr{T}(O)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be as in Corollaries~\\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable} and~\\ref{cor:state-midpoint-price-measurable}. Then, for every $\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{T}(O)$ and every bijection $\\rho$ of $\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{m})$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)=\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}),\n        \\qquad\n        \\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)=\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}).\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 813,
        "references": [
          "prop:executable-volume-uniform-price",
          "cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/4_market_clearing.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/lem__state-functionals-permutation-invariant__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for every $\\bm{m} \\in \\mathscr{T}(O)$ and every permutation $\\rho \\in \\mathscr{P}_{\\ell(\\bm{m})}$, it holds that $\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)=\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$ and $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)=\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$. For each $p\\in\\mathbb{R}$, let $S_{\\bm{m}}(p)$ denote the total quantity of the sell orders in $\\bm{m}$ with price at most $p$, and let $D_{\\bm{m}}(p)$ denote the total quantity of the buy orders in $\\bm{m}$ with price at least $p$. Since both quantities are finite sums over the entries of $\\bm{m}$, reindexing by the bijection $\\rho$ leaves them unchanged, so that $S_{\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho}(p)=S_{\\bm{m}}(p)$ and $D_{\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho}(p)=D_{\\bm{m}}(p)$ for all $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Applying Proposition~\\ref{prop:executable-volume-uniform-price} to the sorted pairs of Corollary~\\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable} yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)\n        = \\max_{p\\in\\mathbb{R}}\\min\\bigl(S_{\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho}(p),D_{\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho}(p)\\bigr)\n        = \\max_{p\\in\\mathbb{R}}\\min\\bigl(S_{\\bm{m}}(p),D_{\\bm{m}}(p)\\bigr)\n        = \\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which proves the first identity.\n\n    To prove the second identity, let $Q=\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})=\\mathscr{Q}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)$. If $Q=0$, then $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho) = 0 = \\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$ by definition. Assume therefore that $Q>0$, and write $\\iota=\\iota^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m})$. Every sell order with a price strictly below $\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota)$ precedes position $\\iota$ in the sorted sell tuple, so for any $p<\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota)$, the cumulative sell quantity $S_{\\bm{m}}(p)$ is at most the cumulative sell quantity strictly before position $\\iota$, which is strictly less than $Q$ by the minimality of $\\iota$. Conversely, at $p = \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota)$, we have $S_{\\bm{m}}(\\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota))\\geq\\widehat{\\bm{Q}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota)\\geq Q$. Consequently,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{s}}(\\iota^{\\bm{s}}(\\bm{m}))=\\min\\{p\\in\\mathbb{R}\\mid S_{\\bm{m}}(p)\\geq Q\\},\n        \\qquad\n        \\widehat{\\bm{p}}^{\\bm{b}}(\\iota^{\\bm{b}}(\\bm{m}))=\\max\\{p\\in\\mathbb{R}\\mid D_{\\bm{m}}(p)\\geq Q\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    where the second characterisation follows by a symmetric argument on the buy side. Because the right-hand sides depend on $\\bm{m}$ solely through $S_{\\bm{m}}$, $D_{\\bm{m}}$, and $Q$, each of which is invariant under $\\rho$, their arithmetic mean $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$ satisfies $\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m}\\circ\\rho)=\\mathscr{P}_{\\mathrm{state}}(\\bm{m})$, as desired."
      },
