        "text": "By the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, for each $n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$, the subset of $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})$ consisting of tuples of length $n$ is simultaneously closed and open, and it is homeomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ equipped with its usual topology; for $n=0$, it is the singleton $\\{\\bm{e}\\}$. Taking products, $L$ is closed and open in $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})^k$ and hence Borel, and its subspace topology is the usual topology of $\\mathbb{R}^{n_1}\\times\\dots\\times\\mathbb{R}^{n_k}$. Therefore, the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $L$ as a subspace of $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})^k$ coincides with $\\mathcal{B}(L)$, and, as $L$ is Borel, a subset of $L$ belongs to $\\mathcal{B}(L)$ if and only if it is Borel in $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})^k$. This proves both assertions."
