        "text": "Let $D\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{C}\\times\\mathcal{C}}$ be the pairwise Euclidean distance matrix with entries $D_{e,e'} = \\lVert\\bm{y}_e - \\bm{y}_{e'}\\rVert_2$, and let $\\bm{d}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the vector with entries $d_e = \\lVert\\bm{y}_e - \\bm{y}_q\\rVert_2$, so that $\\mathsf{ES}(\\pi;\\bm{y}_q) = \\bm{d}^{\\top}\\pi - \\frac{1}{2}\\pi^{\\top} D \\pi$. By Schoenberg's theorem on metric embeddings of Euclidean spaces~\\citep{gneiting2007strictly}, the Euclidean distance matrix $D$ is conditionally negative definite: for every vector $\\bm{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal{C}} v_e = 0$, we have $\\bm{v}^{\\top} D \\bm{v} \\le 0$. For any two distributions $\\pi,\\pi'\\in\\Delta(\\mathcal{C})$, their difference $\\bm{v}=\\pi-\\pi'$ satisfies $\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal{C}} v_e = 0$, and hence the quadratic form $-\\frac{1}{2}\\bm{v}^{\\top} D \\bm{v} \\ge 0$ is positive semi-definite along the tangent space of the simplex $\\Delta(\\mathcal{C})$. Consequently, $\\pi\\mapsto\\mathsf{ES}(\\pi;\\bm{y}_q)$ is convex on $\\Delta(\\mathcal{C})$."
