            "text": "Let $(q_f,p_f),(q_g,p_g)\\in\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\alpha,\\beta\\geq0$ be given, and set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        q=\\alpha q_f+\\beta q_g,\n        \\qquad\n        p=\\alpha p_f+\\beta p_g.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $q$ is continuous and increasing with $q(0)=0$, the functions $q$ and $p$ are affine on the cells of a common finite partition of $[-1,1]$, and $p$ is increasing. Right-continuity on $[-1,1)$ is preserved under non-negative linear combinations. Moreover, the terminal plateau of $q$ begins at the latest terminal-plateau start among the components with a strictly positive coefficient. At that point, components with an earlier start are already constant and the remaining components satisfy their left-limit condition, so $p$ satisfies condition~(4) of Definition~\\ref{defn:graph-tracing-pairs} as well. To verify condition~(3), suppose that $u,v\\in[-1,1]$ satisfy $u<v$ and $q(u)=q(v)$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        0\n        =q(v)-q(u)\n        =\\alpha\\bigl(q_f(v)-q_f(u)\\bigr)\n        +\\beta\\bigl(q_g(v)-q_g(u)\\bigr).\n    \\end{align*}\n    As in the proof of Proposition~\\ref{prop:linear-parametrisation-convex}, every component with a strictly positive coefficient is constant on $[u,v]$, and hence $p(u)=p(v)$. Consequently, $(q,p)\\in\\mathcal{A}$, which proves that $\\mathcal{A}$ is a convex cone.",
