      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 218,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Sided Parametrised Curves",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:admissible-parametrisations-cone}\n    Let $\\mathcal{A}\\subseteq\\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the family of graph-tracing pairs of Definition~\\ref{defn:graph-tracing-pairs}, and let $\\mathpzc{P}$ be the space of price curves. Then $\\mathcal{A}$ is a convex cone, and there is a well-defined \\emph{graph-wise decoder} $D:\\mathcal{A}\\to\\mathpzc{P}$ that assigns to each $(q,p)\\in\\mathcal{A}$ a unique curve $D(q,p)\\in\\mathpzc{P}$ on $\\mathrm{image}(q)$ via\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:graph-wise-decoder}\n        D(q,p)(x)\n        =p(u)\\qquad\\text{for any }u\\in[-1,1]\\text{ such that }q(u)=x.\n    \\end{align}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 367,
        "references": [
          "eq:graph-wise-decoder",
          "defn:graph-tracing-pairs",
          "prop:linear-parametrisation-convex"
        ],
        "source": "2 - day-ahead/2_clearing_operator_price_curves.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__admissible-parametrisations-cone__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show first that for all $(q_f,p_f),(q_g,p_g)\\in\\mathcal{A}$ and all $\\alpha,\\beta\\geq0$, the linear combination $\\alpha(q_f,p_f)+\\beta(q_g,p_g)$ belongs to $\\mathcal{A}$, and second that for every $(q,p)\\in\\mathcal{A}$, the formula \\eqref{eq:graph-wise-decoder} defines a unique element $D(q,p)\\in\\mathpzc{P}$ on $\\mathrm{image}(q)$.\n\n    Let $(q_f,p_f),(q_g,p_g)\\in\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\alpha,\\beta\\geq0$ be given, and set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        q=\\alpha q_f+\\beta q_g,\n        \\qquad\n        p=\\alpha p_f+\\beta p_g.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $q$ is continuous and increasing with $q(0)=0$, the functions $q$ and $p$ are affine on the cells of a common finite partition of $[-1,1]$, and $p$ is increasing. Right-continuity on $[-1,1)$ is preserved under non-negative linear combinations. Moreover, the terminal plateau of $q$ begins at the latest terminal-plateau start among the components with a strictly positive coefficient. At that point, components with an earlier start are already constant and the remaining components satisfy their left-limit condition, so $p$ satisfies condition~(4) of Definition~\\ref{defn:graph-tracing-pairs} as well. To verify condition~(3), suppose that $u,v\\in[-1,1]$ satisfy $u<v$ and $q(u)=q(v)$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        0\n        =q(v)-q(u)\n        =\\alpha\\bigl(q_f(v)-q_f(u)\\bigr)\n        +\\beta\\bigl(q_g(v)-q_g(u)\\bigr).\n    \\end{align*}\n    As in the proof of Proposition~\\ref{prop:linear-parametrisation-convex}, every component with a strictly positive coefficient is constant on $[u,v]$, and hence $p(u)=p(v)$. Consequently, $(q,p)\\in\\mathcal{A}$, which proves that $\\mathcal{A}$ is a convex cone.\n\n    For the second claim, let $(q,p)\\in\\mathcal{A}$ be given. Because $p$ takes the same value at every pair $u,v\\in[-1,1]$ satisfying $q(u)=q(v)$, the rule \\eqref{eq:graph-wise-decoder} is well-defined on $\\mathrm{image}(q)$. Since $q$ and $p$ are increasing, the decoded function $D(q,p)$ is increasing. Furthermore, $D(q,p)$ is affine on the image of every cell on which $q$ is not constant, so it is piecewise affine, and it is simple whenever $p$ is simple. It inherits the one-sided continuity convention chosen at the start of this section from condition~(4) on $p$, and its graph is exactly $\\mathrm{image}(u\\mapsto(q(u),p(u)))$, which establishes uniqueness, as desired."
      },
