      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 220,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Numerical Geometry and Diagnostics",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:app-numerical-inner-product-exact}\nFix $m=500$, set $h=1/m$, and let $u_j=j/m$ for $j\\in\\{-m,\\ldots,m\\}$. For each $z=(a,c)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1001}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{1000}$, let $F_z=(\\phi_a,\\pi_c)\\in L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the function pair whose first coordinate $\\phi_a$ interpolates linearly between $(u_{j-1},a_{j-1})$ and $(u_j,a_j)$ on each cell $K_j=[u_{j-1},u_j]$ and whose second coordinate $\\pi_c$ equals $c_j$ on $K_j$. Then, for all $z,z'\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1001}\\times\\mathbb{R}^{1000}$,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\langle z,z'\\rangle_{\\mathrm{num}}=\\langle F_z,F_{z'}\\rangle_{L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)}.\n\\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 96,
        "references": [
          "eq:numerical-inner-product"
        ],
        "source": "A - appendices/day-ahead-numerical-implementation.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__app-numerical-inner-product-exact__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that the integral $\\int_{-1}^{1}\\bigl(\\phi_a(u)\\phi_{a'}(u)+\\pi_c(u)\\pi_{c'}(u)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}u$ coincides with the sum in~\\eqref{eq:numerical-inner-product}.\n\nFix a cell $K_j=[u_{j-1},u_j]$ of width $h=u_j-u_{j-1}$. Using the local coordinate $s=(u-u_{j-1})/h\\in[0,1]$, the linear interpolants on $K_j$ take the form $\\phi_a(u)=(1-s)a_{j-1}+sa_j$ and $\\phi_{a'}(u)=(1-s)a'_{j-1}+sa'_j$. Changing variables to $s$ with $\\mathrm{d}u=h\\,\\mathrm{d}s$ and using the elementary integrals $\\int_0^1(1-s)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 s^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s=1/3$ and $\\int_0^1 s(1-s)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=1/6$, we obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_{K_j}\\phi_a(u)\\phi_{a'}(u)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\n&= h\\int_0^1\\bigl((1-s)a_{j-1}+sa_j\\bigr)\\bigl((1-s)a'_{j-1}+sa'_j\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&= \\frac{h}{6}\\bigl(2a_{j-1}a'_{j-1}+a_{j-1}a'_j+a_ja'_{j-1}+2a_ja'_j\\bigr).\n\\end{align*}\nSimilarly, because $\\pi_c$ and $\\pi_{c'}$ are constant and equal to $c_j$ and $c'_j$ on the interior of $K_j$, their product integrates to $\\int_{K_j}\\pi_c(u)\\pi_{c'}(u)\\,\\mathrm{d}u=h\\,c_jc'_j$. Summing both cell integrals over $j\\in\\{-m+1,\\ldots,m\\}$ yields~\\eqref{eq:numerical-inner-product}, as desired."
      },
