      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 222,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Battery Storage Optimisation",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:battery-sublinear-scaling}\nLet $\\mathcal{G}$, $\\mathcal{R}$, and $\\tau$ be as in Definition~\\ref{defn:battery-schedule-set}, let $\\lambda\\geq 0$, and let $\\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d = (\\widehat{P}_{d,h})_{h=1}^{24}$ be a $24$-tuple of non-decreasing functions on intervals $\\operatorname{domain}(\\widehat{P}_{d,h})\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}$ such that $0\\in\\operatorname{domain}(\\widehat{P}_{d,h})$ for each $h\\in\\{1,\\dots,24\\}$. For any scaling factor $\\alpha > 1$ and $q_{\\mathrm{start}},q_{\\mathrm{stop}}\\in\\mathcal{G}$, let $\\mathcal{A}_{\\alpha\\mathcal{G}}(\\alpha q_{\\mathrm{start}}, \\alpha q_{\\mathrm{stop}}; \\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d)$ denote the feasible schedule set obtained by replacing $\\mathcal{G}$ and $\\mathcal{R}$ with $\\alpha\\mathcal{G}$ and $\\alpha\\mathcal{R}$, and let $\\widehat{V}_{d,\\alpha}(\\alpha q_{\\mathrm{start}}, \\alpha q_{\\mathrm{stop}})$ denote the corresponding optimal value of \\eqref{eq:approximate-battery-problem}. If $\\mathcal{A}_{\\alpha\\mathcal{G}}(\\alpha q_{\\mathrm{start}}, \\alpha q_{\\mathrm{stop}}; \\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d)$ is non-empty, then $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mathcal{G}}(q_{\\mathrm{start}}, q_{\\mathrm{stop}}; \\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d)$ is also non-empty and\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\widehat{V}_{d,\\alpha}(\\alpha q_{\\mathrm{start}}, \\alpha q_{\\mathrm{stop}}) \\leq \\alpha\\,\\widehat{V}_d(q_{\\mathrm{start}}, q_{\\mathrm{stop}}).\n\\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 389,
        "references": [],
        "source": "2 - day-ahead/6_applications.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__battery-sublinear-scaling__proof.svg",
        "text": "We establish the bound by rescaling an arbitrary feasible element of $\\mathcal{A}_{\\alpha\\mathcal{G}}(\\alpha q_{\\mathrm{start}}, \\alpha q_{\\mathrm{stop}}; \\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d)$ and exploiting the monotonicity of $\\widehat{P}_{d,h}$. Let $\\Delta\\bm{q} = (\\Delta q_h)_{h=1}^{24} \\in \\mathcal{A}_{\\alpha\\mathcal{G}}(\\alpha q_{\\mathrm{start}}, \\alpha q_{\\mathrm{stop}}; \\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d)$, and define $\\Delta\\widetilde{\\bm{q}} = \\alpha^{-1}\\Delta\\bm{q}$. By construction, $\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h \\in \\mathcal{R}$ and the cumulative sums satisfy $\\widetilde{q}_h = q_{\\mathrm{start}} + \\sum_{r=1}^h \\Delta\\widetilde{q}_r = \\alpha^{-1}q_h \\in \\mathcal{G}$ for all $h$, with $\\widetilde{q}_{24} = q_{\\mathrm{stop}}$. Moreover, because $\\tau$ is positively homogeneous of degree one, $\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h) = \\alpha^{-1}\\tau(\\Delta q_h)$ lies between $0$ and $\\tau(\\Delta q_h)$, and hence belongs to the interval $\\operatorname{domain}(\\widehat{P}_{d,h})$. Thus $\\Delta\\widetilde{\\bm{q}} \\in \\mathcal{A}_{\\mathcal{G}}(q_{\\mathrm{start}}, q_{\\mathrm{stop}}; \\widehat{\\bm{P}}_d)$.\n\nNext, fix $h \\in \\{1,\\dots,24\\}$. Since $\\alpha > 1$ and $\\widehat{P}_{d,h}$ is non-decreasing, if $\\tau(\\Delta q_h) \\geq 0$, then $0 \\leq \\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h) \\leq \\tau(\\Delta q_h)$ implies $\\widehat{P}_{d,h}(\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h)) \\leq \\widehat{P}_{d,h}(\\tau(\\Delta q_h))$; multiplying by the non-positive quantity $-\\tau(\\Delta q_h) = -\\alpha\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h)$ reverses the inequality. Conversely, if $\\tau(\\Delta q_h) < 0$, then $\\tau(\\Delta q_h) \\leq \\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h) < 0$ implies $\\widehat{P}_{d,h}(\\tau(\\Delta q_h)) \\leq \\widehat{P}_{d,h}(\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h))$; multiplying by the positive quantity $-\\tau(\\Delta q_h) = -\\alpha\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h)$ preserves the inequality. In both cases, we obtain\n\\begin{align*}\n    -\\tau(\\Delta q_h)\\widehat{P}_{d,h}(\\tau(\\Delta q_h)) - \\lambda|\\Delta q_h|\n    \\leq \\alpha\\left[-\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h)\\widehat{P}_{d,h}(\\tau(\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h)) - \\lambda|\\Delta\\widetilde{q}_h|\\right].\n\\end{align*}\nSumming over $h=1,\\dots,24$ and taking the maximum over $\\Delta\\bm{q}$ yields the stated bound."
      },
