      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Relations and Functions",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:binary-product-space}\n    Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be sets. Then the map $(x_1,x_2)\\mapsto\\{(1,x_1),(2,x_2)\\}$ is a bijection from the Cartesian product of Definition~\\ref{defn:product-space} onto the Cartesian product of the family $(X_i)_{i\\in\\{1,2\\}}$ of Definition~\\ref{defn:arbitrary-product-space}; that is,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        X_1 \\times X_2 \\cong \\prod_{i=1}^2 X_i.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 234,
        "references": [
          "prop:graph-defines"
        ],
        "source": "A - appendices/sets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__binary-product-space__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that the map $\\Phi\\colon X_1\\times X_2\\to\\prod_{i=1}^2 X_i$ given by $\\Phi(x_1,x_2)=\\{(1,x_1),(2,x_2)\\}$ is well-defined and admits a two-sided inverse. For any $(x_1,x_2)\\in X_1\\times X_2$, the set $\\{(1,x_1),(2,x_2)\\}$ is a function on $\\{1,2\\}$ taking values $x_1\\in X_1$ at $1$ and $x_2\\in X_2$ at $2$, so $\\Phi(x_1,x_2)\\in\\prod_{i=1}^2X_i$. Define $\\Psi\\colon\\prod_{i=1}^2X_i\\to X_1\\times X_2$ by $\\Psi(z)=(z(1),z(2))$. Then $\\Psi(\\Phi(x_1,x_2))=(x_1,x_2)$ for every $(x_1,x_2)\\in X_1\\times X_2$, and for every $z\\in\\prod_{i=1}^2X_i$, Proposition~\\ref{prop:graph-defines} gives $\\Phi(\\Psi(z))=\\{(1,z(1)),(2,z(2))\\}=\\Gamma(z)=z$. Thus $\\Phi$ is a bijection, as desired."
      },
