      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Block Resampling",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:block-bootstrap-invariance}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $\\mathscr{C}(O)$ be the composite-action space over a complete and separable metric space $(O,d_O)$, and let $(\\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ be independent and identically distributed point processes in $\\mathcal{N}([0,1]\\times\\mathscr{C}(O))$ such that, for each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,K\\}$, $\\xi_k(\\{0\\}\\times\\mathscr{C}(O))=0$ almost surely and the block restrictions $\\xi_k^1,\\dots,\\xi_k^L$ of Definition~\\ref{defn:block-bootstrap} are mutually independent:\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{P}\\left(\\xi_k^1 \\in A_1, \\dots, \\xi_k^L \\in A_L\\right) = \\prod_{j = 1}^L \\mathbb{P}\\left(\\xi_k^j \\in A_j\\right)\n    \\end{align*}\n    for all $A_1, \\dots, A_L \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathcal{N}([0,1]\\times \\mathscr{C}(O)))$. Then, for any deterministic sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L\\in\\{1,\\dots,K\\}^L$, or any random sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ taking values in $\\{1,\\dots,K\\}^L$ independently of $(\\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$, the block-bootstrapped point process $\\xi^\\ast = \\sum_{j=1}^L \\xi_{k_j}^j$ satisfies $\\xi^\\ast \\stackrel{d}{=} \\xi_1$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 104,
        "references": [],
        "source": "3 - intraday/3_inference_for_point_processes.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__block-bootstrap-invariance__proof.svg",
        "text": "Suppose first that $(k_j)_{j=1}^L \\in \\{1,\\dots,K\\}^L$ is deterministic. Since the paths $(\\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ are mutually independent and the blocks $(\\xi_k^j)_{j=1}^L$ within each path $k$ are mutually independent by assumption, the entire array of blocks $(\\xi_k^j)_{1\\le k\\le K,\\,1\\le j\\le L}$ is a collection of mutually independent random elements in $\\mathcal{N}([0,1]\\times\\mathscr{C}(O))$. Because the interval index $j$ is distinct for each term in the sequence $(\\xi_{k_1}^1,\\dots,\\xi_{k_L}^L)$, these $L$ blocks are distinct entries of the array even when path indices $k_j$ repeat, and they are therefore mutually independent. Moreover, since $(\\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ are identically distributed, $\\xi_{k_j}^j$ has the same marginal distribution as $\\xi_1^j$ for every $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,L\\}$. Consequently, the random vector $(\\xi_{k_1}^1,\\dots,\\xi_{k_L}^L)$ has the same joint distribution on $\\mathcal{N}([0,1]\\times\\mathscr{C}(O))^L$ as $(\\xi_1^1,\\dots,\\xi_1^L)$.\n\n    Since $\\xi_1(\\{0\\}\\times\\mathscr{C}(O))=0$ almost surely and $[0,1]=\\{0\\}\\cup\\bigcup_{j=1}^L I_j$ is a disjoint union, we have $\\xi_1 = \\sum_{j=1}^L \\xi_1^j$ almost surely. Because addition of counting measures is Borel measurable on $\\mathcal{N}([0,1]\\times\\mathscr{C}(O))^L$, applying the summation map to both vectors yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi^\\ast = \\sum_{j=1}^L \\xi_{k_j}^j \\stackrel{d}{=} \\sum_{j=1}^L \\xi_1^j = \\xi_1.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Finally, if $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ is random and independent of $(\\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$, conditioning on the realisation of $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ and applying the deterministic result on each fibre shows that the conditional law of $\\xi^\\ast$ given $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ equals the unconditional law of $\\xi_1$, which completes the proof."
      },
