      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 228,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Distribution Forecasts",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:cdf-constraint-properties}\n    Let $\\mathcal{C}\\subseteq L^2([a,b])$ and $\\mathsf{S}:\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathfrak{D}_{[a,b]}$ be as in Definition~\\ref{defn:cdf-constraint-selection}. Then:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item the set $\\mathcal{C}$ is non-empty, closed, and convex in $L^2([a,b])$, and its metric projection $P_{\\mathcal{C}}:L^2([a,b])\\to\\mathcal{C}$ exists, is unique, and is $1$-Lipschitz;\n        \\item the conditional expectation satisfies $\\mathbb{E}[\\chi_t\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s]=[G_s(\\cdot,\\cdot)|_{[a,b]}]\\in\\mathcal{C}$ almost surely;\n        \\item the map $\\mathsf{S}$ is the inverse of the restriction map $F\\mapsto[F|_{[a,b]}]$ from $\\mathfrak{D}_{[a,b]}$ onto $\\mathcal{C}$ and is an isometry from $(\\mathcal{C},\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_{L^2([a,b])})$ onto $\\mathfrak{D}_{[a,b]}$ equipped with the Cram\\'er distance $(\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}(F-G)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x)^{1/2}$.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 86,
        "references": [],
        "source": "A - appendices/extended-research-directions.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__cdf-constraint-properties__proof.svg",
        "text": "We prove the three claims in order. For (i), since $[0]\\in\\mathcal{C}$, the set is non-empty; it is convex because convex combinations of non-decreasing functions with values in $[0,1]$ are again non-decreasing with values in $[0,1]$. For closedness, suppose $[f_n]\\in\\mathcal{C}$ and $[f_n]\\to[f]$ in $L^2([a,b])$. Choose bounded non-decreasing representatives of $[f_n]$ and a subsequence converging almost everywhere to a representative of $[f]$. On the full-measure convergence set, the limit is non-decreasing; its monotone extension to $[a,b]$, obtained from one-sided suprema and endpoint limits, agrees with that representative almost everywhere and remains in $[0,1]$. Hence $[f]\\in\\mathcal{C}$. Because $\\mathcal{C}$ is non-empty, closed, and convex in the Hilbert space $L^2([a,b])$, its metric projection $P_{\\mathcal{C}}:L^2([a,b])\\to\\mathcal{C}$ exists, is unique at the equivalence-class level, and is $1$-Lipschitz.\n\n    For (ii), with $G_s$ as above and $u\\in L^2([a,b])$, let $h_u(y)=\\int_a^b u(x)\\mathbbm{1}\\{y\\leq x\\}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Since $\\langle\\mathbb{E}[\\chi_t\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s],u\\rangle=\\mathbb{E}[\\langle\\chi_t,u\\rangle\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s]$ almost surely, Fubini's theorem for the kernel $\\kappa_s$ gives $\\langle\\mathbb{E}[\\chi_t\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s],u\\rangle=\\mathbb{E}[h_u(X_t)\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s]=\\int_a^b u(x)G_s(\\cdot,x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ almost surely. Applying this to a countable dense subset of $L^2([a,b])$ and discarding countably many null sets gives $\\mathbb{E}[\\chi_t\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s]=[G_s(\\cdot,\\cdot)|_{[a,b]}]$ almost surely. Since each $G_s(\\omega,\\cdot)$ is a distribution function, $\\mathbb{E}[\\chi_t\\mid\\mathcal{F}_s]\\in\\mathcal{C}$ almost surely.\n\n    For (iii), for any $[f]\\in\\mathcal{C}$, the selected extension $\\mathsf{S}([f])$ is non-decreasing and right-continuous, has limits $0$ and $1$ at $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$, and may assign masses at $a$ and $b$; it therefore lies in $\\mathfrak{D}_{[a,b]}$. By construction, $\\mathsf{S}$ is the inverse of the restriction map $F\\mapsto[F|_{[a,b]}]$ from $\\mathfrak{D}_{[a,b]}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$, so the $L^2([a,b])$ encoding of distribution functions supported on $[a,b]$ is injective. Since any two distribution functions $F,G\\in\\mathfrak{D}_{[a,b]}$ agree outside $[a,b)$, their Cram\\'er distance $(\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}(F-G)^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x)^{1/2}$ equals $\\lVert[F|_{[a,b]}]-[G|_{[a,b]}]\\rVert_{L^2([a,b])}$, which proves that $\\mathsf{S}$ is an isometry."
      },
