      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 230,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:components-partition-incomp}\nLet $(X,\\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set and let $\\bm{x}\\in\\mathscr{T}(X)$ with comparability components\n$\\mathcal{K}(\\bm{x})=\\{K_1,\\dots,K_\\kappa\\}$. Then:\n\\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n\\item The family $(K_k)_{k=1}^{\\kappa}$ is a partition of the graph of $\\bm{x}$, i.e.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Gamma(\\bm{x}) = \\bigcup_{k=1}^{\\kappa} K_k,\n\\end{align*}\nwith $K_k \\cap K_{k^\\prime} = \\emptyset$ for all $k\\neq k^\\prime$.\n\\item If $k\\neq k^\\prime$, then for all $(i,\\bm{x}(i))\\in K_k$ and $(j,\\bm{x}(j))\\in K_{k^\\prime}$ we have that $\\bm{x}(i)$ and $\\bm{x}(j)$ are incomparable in $X$.\n\\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1460,
        "references": [
          "prop:sim-equivalence",
          "defn:index-comparable",
          "defn:connectivity-relation"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__components-partition-incomp__proof.svg",
        "text": "If $\\bm{x} = \\bm{e}$, then $\\Gamma(\\bm{x}) = \\emptyset$ and $\\kappa = 0$, so both claims hold vacuously. Suppose therefore that $\\ell(\\bm{x}) > 0$.\n\n    First, we show (i). We must show that $\\bigcup_{k=1}^{\\kappa} K_k = \\Gamma(\\bm{x})$ and that $K_k \\cap K_{k^\\prime} = \\emptyset$ for all $k, k^\\prime \\in \\{1,\\dots,\\kappa\\}$ with $k \\neq k^\\prime$. By Proposition~\\ref{prop:sim-equivalence}, the connectivity relation $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim}$ is an equivalence relation on $\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$, so its equivalence classes $C_1,\\dots,C_\\kappa$ form a partition of $\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$. Consequently,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigcup_{k=1}^{\\kappa} K_k\n        = \\bigcup_{k=1}^{\\kappa} \\{(i,\\bm{x}(i)) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x}) \\mid i \\in C_k\\}\n        = \\left\\{(i,\\bm{x}(i)) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x}) \\;\\middle|\\; i \\in \\bigcup_{k=1}^{\\kappa} C_k\\right\\}\n        = \\Gamma(\\bm{x}).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, if $k \\neq k^\\prime$, then $C_k \\cap C_{k^\\prime} = \\emptyset$, and since the first coordinate of any element in $K_k$ lies in $C_k$, it follows that $K_k \\cap K_{k^\\prime} = \\emptyset$.\n\n    Next, we show (ii). Let $k, k^\\prime \\in \\{1,\\dots,\\kappa\\}$ with $k \\neq k^\\prime$, and let $(i,\\bm{x}(i)) \\in K_k$ and $(j,\\bm{x}(j)) \\in K_{k^\\prime}$ be arbitrary. We must show that $\\neg(\\bm{x}(i) \\preceq \\bm{x}(j))$ and $\\neg(\\bm{x}(j) \\preceq \\bm{x}(i))$. Suppose for contradiction that $\\bm{x}(i) \\preceq \\bm{x}(j)$ or $\\bm{x}(j) \\preceq \\bm{x}(i)$. By Definition~\\ref{defn:index-comparable}, this means that $i \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} j$, and hence $i \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} j$ by Definition~\\ref{defn:connectivity-relation}. Since $i \\in C_k$ and $j \\in C_{k^\\prime}$, it follows that $C_k = C_{k^\\prime}$, which contradicts $k \\neq k^\\prime$. Therefore, $\\bm{x}(i)$ and $\\bm{x}(j)$ are incomparable in $X$, as desired."
      },
