      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 236,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Structure of Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:disjoint-product}\n    Let $(X_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be a family of sets. Then \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha \\subseteq\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha\\Bigr) \\times A.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha = \\Bigl(\\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha\\Bigr) \\times A \\iff \\forall \\alpha, \\alpha^\\prime \\in A: X_\\alpha = X_{\\alpha^\\prime} .\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 153,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__disjoint-product__proof.svg",
        "text": "Notice that by definition \n    \\begin{align*}\n        (x,\\alpha) \\in X_{\\alpha} \\times \\{\\alpha\\} \\implies (x,\\alpha) \\in \\Bigl(\\bigcup_{\\alpha^\\prime \\in A} X_{\\alpha^\\prime}\\Bigr) \\times A,\n    \\end{align*}\n    since $x \\in X_\\alpha \\subseteq \\bigcup_{\\alpha^\\prime \\in A}X_{\\alpha^\\prime}$ and $\\alpha \\in A$. Therefore, the above shows that in general \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha = \\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\} \\subseteq \\Bigl(\\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha\\Bigr) \\times A.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $X_\\alpha = X_{\\alpha^\\prime}$ for all $\\alpha,\\alpha^\\prime \\in A$, then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Bigl(\\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha\\Bigr) \\times A\n        &= \\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} \\bigcup_{\\alpha^\\prime \\in A} X_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha^\\prime\\}\\\\\n        &= \\bigcup_{\\alpha^\\prime \\in A} X_{\\alpha^\\prime} \\times \\{\\alpha^\\prime\\}\n        = \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha^\\prime \\in A} X_{\\alpha^\\prime},\n    \\end{align*}\n    proving the reverse inclusion and hence equality.\n\n    Conversely, assume that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha = \\Bigl(\\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha\\Bigr) \\times A.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Fix $\\alpha,\\alpha^\\prime \\in A$ and let $x \\in X_\\alpha$. Then $(x,\\alpha^\\prime)$ belongs to the right-hand side and therefore, by the assumed equality, also to the left-hand side. Hence $(x,\\alpha^\\prime) \\in X_{\\alpha^\\prime} \\times \\{\\alpha^\\prime\\}$, which implies $x \\in X_{\\alpha^\\prime}$. Thus $X_\\alpha \\subseteq X_{\\alpha^\\prime}$. By symmetry, $X_{\\alpha^\\prime} \\subseteq X_\\alpha$, and therefore $X_\\alpha = X_{\\alpha^\\prime}$."
      },
