            "text": "Finally, we must show that $(x,\\alpha) \\preceq (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime)$ or $(x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime) \\preceq (x,\\alpha)$. To do so, notice that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        &((x,\\alpha) \\preceq (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime) )\\lor ((x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime) \\preceq (x,\\alpha))\\iff\\\\ &(\\alpha < \\alpha^\\prime) \\lor (\\alpha^\\prime < \\alpha) \\lor ((\\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime) \\land ((x \\preceq_\\alpha x^\\prime) \\lor (x^\\prime \\preceq_\\alpha x))),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and the three mutually exclusive cases are $\\alpha < \\alpha^\\prime$, $\\alpha^\\prime < \\alpha$, and $\\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime$. Since $\\leq$ is total on $A$, exactly one of them holds, and in the third case the disjunction holds because $\\preceq_\\alpha$ is total. For the antisymmetric relation $\\preceq$, this connexity property is equivalent to the totality condition~(iv) of Definition~\\ref{defn:ordered-sets}.",
