      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 238,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties}\n    Let $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be a family of metric spaces over an index set $A$. Let $(X,d)$ be the corresponding disjoint union metric space. Then:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item if $A$ is countable and $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ are all separable metric spaces, then $(X,d)$ is separable;\\label{property:disjoint-metric-1}\n        \\item if $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ are all complete metric spaces, then $(X,d)$ is complete.\\label{property:disjoint-metric-2}\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 401,
        "references": [
          "property:disjoint-metric-1"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__disjoint-union-metric-properties__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, we show \\eqref{property:disjoint-metric-1}. We must show that there is a countable and dense subset $U \\subseteq X$. Now, by assumption each $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)$ is separable. Then, by definition, there exists a countable and dense subset, say $U_\\alpha$, for which \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\forall x_\\alpha \\in X_\\alpha\\ \\forall \\varepsilon> 0\\ \\exists u_\\alpha \\in U_\\alpha : d_\\alpha(x_\\alpha, u_\\alpha) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    We claim that the set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        U = \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} U_\\alpha\n    \\end{align*}\n    is both countable and dense in $(X,d)$. First, note that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        U = \\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} (U_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\}).\n    \\end{align*}\n    For each $\\alpha \\in A$, the set $U_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\}$ is countable, as it naturally bijects with the countable set $U_\\alpha$. Moreover, $\\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} U_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\}$ is a countable union of countable sets and therefore countable. Hence, $U$ is indeed countable. \n\n    To show that $U$ is dense in $X$, we must show that for every $\\varepsilon > 0 $ and $x \\in X$ there exists a $u\\in U$ such that $d(x,u) < \\varepsilon$. To this end, let $\\varepsilon > 0$ and $x\\in X$ be given. Notice that $x = (x_\\alpha, \\alpha)$ for some $\\alpha \\in A$ and $x_{\\alpha} \\in X_\\alpha$. Since $U_\\alpha$ is dense in $X_\\alpha$, there exists an element $u_\\alpha \\in U_\\alpha$ such that $d_\\alpha(x_\\alpha, u_\\alpha) < \\varepsilon$. Define $u = (u_\\alpha, \\alpha)$; then $u \\in U$ and $d(x,u) = d_\\alpha(x_\\alpha,u_\\alpha) < \\varepsilon$, as desired. \n\n    Next, we must show that any Cauchy sequence in $(X,d)$ converges in $X$. To this end, recall that a sequence $(x_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}} \\subseteq X$ is said to be Cauchy if \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\forall \\varepsilon > 0\\ \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}: m,n\\geq N \\implies d(x_n, x_m) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Each $x \\in X$ has the form $x = (x_\\alpha, \\alpha)$, and so the Cauchy sequence $(x_n)_{n\\in \\mathbb{N}}$ admits the representation \n    \\begin{align*}\n        x_n = (x_{\\alpha_n, n}, \\alpha_n).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, since the sequence is Cauchy, choose $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $d(x_n,x_m)<1$ for all $n,m\\geq N$. This finite bound implies $\\alpha_n=\\alpha_m$ for all $n,m\\geq N$; set $\\alpha=\\alpha_N$. Now, for all tail indices, $d(x_n, x_m) = d_\\alpha (x_{\\alpha, n}, x_{\\alpha,m})$, implying that the sequence $(x_{\\alpha, n})_{n = N}^\\infty$ is Cauchy in $X_\\alpha$. By assumption, $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)$ is complete, and so the sequence $(x_{\\alpha, n})_{n = N}^\\infty$ has a limit, say, $x_\\alpha \\in X_\\alpha$. Then $x = (x_\\alpha, \\alpha) \\in X$ and clearly $(x_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ converges to $x$."
      },
