            "text": "We must show that the mapping $d:X\\times X \\to [0,\\infty]$ satisfies the three criteria for all $(x,\\alpha),(x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime),(x^\\ast, \\alpha^\\ast) \\in X = \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha$:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $d((x,\\alpha), (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime)) = 0$ if and only if $(x,\\alpha) = (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime)$.\\label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1}\n        \\item $d((x,\\alpha), (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime)) = d((x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime), (x,\\alpha))$. \\label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2}\n        \\item $d((x,\\alpha), (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime)) \\leq d((x,\\alpha), (x^\\ast, \\alpha^\\ast)) + d((x^\\ast, \\alpha^\\ast), (x^\\prime, \\alpha^\\prime))$. \\label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    The criteria \\eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1} and \\eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2} are trivial. For \\eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}, we use the usual extended-real convention that $r\\leq\\infty$ for every $r\\in[0,\\infty]$. If $\\alpha \\neq \\alpha^\\prime$, the right-hand side of the inequality is infinite, since $\\alpha^\\ast$ cannot be equal to both $\\alpha$ and $\\alpha^\\prime$. Hence, it remains to consider the case $\\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime$. Again, if $\\alpha^\\ast \\neq \\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime$ the claim is trivial, so we only need to consider $\\alpha^\\ast = \\alpha = \\alpha^\\prime$, but then it reduces to the standard triangle inequality for $d_\\alpha$, which, by assumption, is a metric.",
