            "text": "We first show that $\\mathscr{Q}((\\bm{Q}^s, \\bm{p}^s),\\allowbreak (\\bm{Q}^b, \\bm{p}^b)) \\leq\\allowbreak \\max_{p \\in \\mathbb{R}} \\min(S(p), D(p))$. If $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}^s, \\bm{p}^b) = \\emptyset$, then $\\mathscr{Q}((\\bm{Q}^s, \\bm{p}^s),\\allowbreak (\\bm{Q}^b, \\bm{p}^b)) = 0 \\leq\\allowbreak \\max_{p \\in \\mathbb{R}} \\min(S(p), D(p))$ because $S(p) \\geq 0$ and $D(p) \\geq 0$ for all $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose therefore that $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}^s, \\bm{p}^b) \\neq \\emptyset$, and let $(i, j) \\in \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}^s, \\bm{p}^b)$ be arbitrary. By \\eqref{eq:index-mapping}, we have $\\bm{p}^s(i) \\leq \\bm{p}^b(j)$, so we may choose a price $p \\in [\\bm{p}^s(i), \\bm{p}^b(j)]$. Since $\\bm{p}^s \\in \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq)$ is sorted in ascending order and $\\bm{p}^b \\in \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq)$ is sorted in descending order, every $r \\in \\{1, \\dots, i\\}$ satisfies $\\bm{p}^s(r) \\leq \\bm{p}^s(i) \\leq p$, and every $r \\in \\{1, \\dots, j\\}$ satisfies $\\bm{p}^b(r) \\geq \\bm{p}^b(j) \\geq p$. Because all order quantities are non-negative, it follows that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        S(p) \\geq \\sum_{r=1}^{i} \\bm{q}^s(r) = \\bm{Q}^s(i)\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        D(p) \\geq \\sum_{r=1}^{j} \\bm{q}^b(r) = \\bm{Q}^b(j),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\min(\\bm{Q}^s(i), \\bm{Q}^b(j)) \\leq \\min(S(p), D(p)) \\leq \\max_{p^\\prime \\in \\mathbb{R}} \\min(S(p^\\prime), D(p^\\prime)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Taking the maximum over $(i, j) \\in \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}^s, \\bm{p}^b)$ yields $\\mathscr{Q}((\\bm{Q}^s, \\bm{p}^s),\\allowbreak (\\bm{Q}^b, \\bm{p}^b)) \\leq\\allowbreak \\max_{p \\in \\mathbb{R}} \\min(S(p), D(p))$.",
