            "references": [],
            "text": "For the reverse inequality, fix $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$. If $\\min(S(p), D(p)) = 0$, then $\\min(S(p), D(p)) \\leq\\allowbreak \\mathscr{Q}((\\bm{Q}^s, \\bm{p}^s),\\allowbreak (\\bm{Q}^b, \\bm{p}^b))$ holds immediately. Suppose therefore that $\\min(S(p), D(p)) > 0$. Then both $\\{r \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p}^s) \\mid \\bm{p}^s(r) \\leq p\\}$ and $\\{r \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p}^b) \\mid\\allowbreak \\bm{p}^b(r) \\geq p\\}$ are non-empty finite sets; let $i$ and $j$ denote their respective maximum elements. Because $\\bm{p}^s$ is non-decreasing and $\\bm{p}^b$ is non-increasing, the index sets of qualifying orders are $\\{1, \\dots, i\\}$ and $\\{1, \\dots, j\\}$, which implies that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        S(p) = \\sum_{r=1}^{i} \\bm{q}^s(r) = \\bm{Q}^s(i)\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        D(p) = \\sum_{r=1}^{j} \\bm{q}^b(r) = \\bm{Q}^b(j).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, by construction we have $\\bm{p}^s(i) \\leq p \\leq \\bm{p}^b(j)$, so $(i, j) \\in \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}^s, \\bm{p}^b)$, and consequently\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\min\\bigl(S(p), D(p)\\bigr) = \\min\\bigl(\\bm{Q}^s(i), \\bm{Q}^b(j)\\bigr) \\leq \\mathscr{Q}\\bigl((\\bm{Q}^s, \\bm{p}^s), (\\bm{Q}^b, \\bm{p}^b)\\bigr).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Taking the maximum over $p \\in \\mathbb{R}$ establishes the reverse inequality, as desired.",
