      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 244,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Aggregation and Executable Volume",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:executable-volume-usc}\\index{executable volume!upper semicontinuity}\n    Let $\\mathfrak{C}(\\leq)$, $\\mathfrak{C}(\\geq)$, and $\\mathscr{Q}$ be as in Theorem~\\ref{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable}. Then:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $\\mathscr{Q}$ is upper semicontinuous.\n        \\item $\\mathscr{Q}$ is continuous at every $((\\bm{Q},\\bm{p}),(\\bm{Q}^\\prime,\\bm{p}^\\prime))$ with $\\bm{p}(i)\\neq\\bm{p}^\\prime(j)$ for all $(i,j)\\in\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p})\\times\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p}^\\prime)$.\n        \\item $\\mathscr{Q}$ is not continuous at $((\\bm{Q},\\bm{p}),(\\bm{Q}^\\prime,\\bm{p}^\\prime))$ if $\\bm{Q}$, $\\bm{p}$, $\\bm{Q}^\\prime$, and $\\bm{p}^\\prime$ have length one, $\\bm{Q}(1)=\\bm{Q}^\\prime(1)=1$, and $\\bm{p}(1)=\\bm{p}^\\prime(1)=0$.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 682,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/4_market_clearing.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__executable-volume-usc__proof.svg",
        "text": "To prove (i), we must show that for every $z=((\\bm{Q},\\bm{p}),(\\bm{Q}^\\prime,\\bm{p}^\\prime)) \\in \\mathfrak{C}(\\leq) \\times \\mathfrak{C}(\\geq)$ and every sequence $(z_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ in $\\mathfrak{C}(\\leq) \\times \\mathfrak{C}(\\geq)$ converging to $z$, it holds that $\\limsup_{k\\to\\infty} \\mathscr{Q}(z_k) \\leq \\mathscr{Q}(z)$. Let $z_k=((\\bm{Q}_k,\\bm{p}_k),(\\bm{Q}^\\prime_k,\\bm{p}^\\prime_k))$, $k\\in\\mathbb{N}$, converge to $z$. By the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, the fixed-length layers are clopen and carry the usual Euclidean topology, so there exists $K\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $k\\geq K$, the tuples $\\bm{p}_k$ and $\\bm{p}^\\prime_k$ have the constant lengths $n=\\ell(\\bm{p})$ and $m=\\ell(\\bm{p}^\\prime)$, and the coordinates of $\\bm{Q}_k$, $\\bm{p}_k$, $\\bm{Q}^\\prime_k$, and $\\bm{p}^\\prime_k$ converge pointwise to those of $\\bm{Q}$, $\\bm{p}$, $\\bm{Q}^\\prime$, and $\\bm{p}^\\prime$. If a pair $(i,j)\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ lies outside $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)$, then $\\bm{p}(i)>\\bm{p}^\\prime(j)$, and hence $\\bm{p}_k(i)>\\bm{p}^\\prime_k(j)$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Because $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ is finite, we may enlarge $K$ so that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}_k,\\bm{p}^\\prime_k)\\subseteq\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)\n        \\qquad\\text{for all } k\\geq K.\n    \\end{align*}\n    For each $k\\geq K$, define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        g_k = \\begin{cases}\n            0, & \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime) = \\emptyset,\\\\\n            \\max\\bigl\\{\\min(\\bm{Q}_k(i),\\bm{Q}^\\prime_k(j))\\bigm| (i,j)\\in\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)\\bigr\\}, & \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime) \\neq \\emptyset.\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since all cumulative quantities are non-negative, the inclusion $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}_k,\\bm{p}^\\prime_k)\\subseteq\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)$ implies that $\\mathscr{Q}(z_k)\\leq g_k$ for all $k \\geq K$. Moreover, because the maximum and minimum over a fixed finite index set are continuous, we have $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty} g_k = \\mathscr{Q}(z)$. Consequently,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\limsup_{k\\to\\infty}\\mathscr{Q}(z_k) \\leq \\lim_{k\\to\\infty} g_k = \\mathscr{Q}(z),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which proves (i).\n\n    To prove (ii), suppose in addition that $\\bm{p}(i)\\neq\\bm{p}^\\prime(j)$ for all $(i,j)\\in\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p})\\times\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p}^\\prime)$. Then every $(i,j)\\in\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)$ satisfies the strict inequality $\\bm{p}(i)<\\bm{p}^\\prime(j)$, and hence $\\bm{p}_k(i)<\\bm{p}^\\prime_k(j)$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Enlarging $K$ if necessary, we obtain $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}_k,\\bm{p}^\\prime_k) = \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)$ for all $k \\geq K$, which yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\lim_{k\\to\\infty} \\mathscr{Q}(z_k) = \\lim_{k\\to\\infty} g_k = \\mathscr{Q}(z),\n    \\end{align*}\n    establishing continuity at $z$.\n\n    Finally, to prove (iii), let $z=((\\bm{Q},\\bm{p}),(\\bm{Q}^\\prime,\\bm{p}^\\prime))$ be the point specified in (iii), and for each $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ define $z_k=((\\bm{Q},\\bm{p}_k),(\\bm{Q}^\\prime,\\bm{p}^\\prime))$, where $\\ell(\\bm{p}_k)=1$ and $\\bm{p}_k(1)=1/k$. Then $z_k \\to z$ as $k \\to \\infty$, but $\\bm{p}_k(1) = 1/k > 0 = \\bm{p}^\\prime(1)$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Thus $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}_k,\\bm{p}^\\prime)=\\emptyset$ and $\\mathscr{Q}(z_k)=0$ for all $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$, whereas $\\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p},\\bm{p}^\\prime)=\\{(1,1)\\}$ and $\\mathscr{Q}(z)=\\min(1,1)=1$, proving that $\\mathscr{Q}$ is discontinuous at $z$, as desired."
      },
