      "order": 245,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:finite-equivalence-classes}\n    Let $X$ be a set, and let $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ have length $n$ and distinct entries occurring $m_1,\\dots,m_\\kappa$ times (for the empty tuple, $\\kappa=0$ and the empty product is $1$). Then every element of $\\bm{x}^\\pi$ equals $\\bm{x}\\circ\\sigma$ for exactly $m_1!\\cdots m_\\kappa!$ permutations $\\sigma\\in\\mathscr{P}_n$. In particular,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        |\\bm{x}^\\pi| = \\frac{n!}{m_1!\\cdots m_\\kappa!} \\leq n!.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 330,
        "references": [
          "defn:permutative-equality"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__finite-equivalence-classes__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for every $\\bm{y} \\in \\bm{x}^\\pi$, the set of permutations\n    \\begin{align*}\n        S(\\bm{y}) = \\{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n \\mid \\bm{x} \\circ \\sigma = \\bm{y}\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    has cardinality $|S(\\bm{y})| = m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!$, and consequently that $|\\bm{x}^\\pi| = n! / (m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!)$. If $n = 0$, then $\\bm{x} = \\bm{e}$, $\\mathscr{P}_0$ is a singleton, $\\kappa = 0$, and the empty product equals $1$, so the claim holds immediately. Suppose therefore that $n > 0$, and let $a_1, \\dots, a_\\kappa \\in X$ denote the distinct entries of $\\bm{x}$. For each $j \\in \\{1, \\dots, \\kappa\\}$, define the level set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_j = \\{i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\} \\mid \\bm{x}(i) = a_j\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    which has cardinality $|I_j| = m_j$. A permutation $\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$ satisfies $\\bm{x} \\circ \\sigma = \\bm{x}$ if and only if $\\bm{x}(\\sigma(i)) = \\bm{x}(i)$ for every $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, which holds if and only if $\\sigma(I_j) = I_j$ for every $j \\in \\{1, \\dots, \\kappa\\}$. Since the sets $I_1, \\dots, I_\\kappa$ partition $\\{1, \\dots, n\\}$, choosing such a permutation $\\sigma$ is equivalent to independently choosing a bijection of each $I_j$ onto itself. Therefore,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        |S(\\bm{x})| = m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!.\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    Now let $\\bm{y} \\in \\bm{x}^\\pi$ be arbitrary. By Definition~\\ref{defn:permutative-equality}, there exists a permutation $\\sigma_0 \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$ such that $\\bm{y} = \\bm{x} \\circ \\sigma_0$. For any $\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x} \\circ \\sigma = \\bm{y} = \\bm{x} \\circ \\sigma_0 \\iff \\bm{x} \\circ (\\sigma \\circ \\sigma_0^{-1}) = \\bm{x} \\iff \\sigma \\circ \\sigma_0^{-1} \\in S(\\bm{x}).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Because the right-multiplication map $\\sigma \\mapsto \\sigma \\circ \\sigma_0^{-1}$ is a bijection from $\\mathscr{P}_n$ onto itself, it restricts to a bijection from $S(\\bm{y})$ onto $S(\\bm{x})$, whence $|S(\\bm{y})| = |S(\\bm{x})| = m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!$. Finally, since the sets $(S(\\bm{y}))_{\\bm{y} \\in \\bm{x}^\\pi}$ form a partition of $\\mathscr{P}_n$ into $|\\bm{x}^\\pi|$ subsets of equal cardinality $m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!$, we obtain\n    \\begin{align*}\n        n! = |\\mathscr{P}_n| = \\sum_{\\bm{y} \\in \\bm{x}^\\pi} |S(\\bm{y})| = |\\bm{x}^\\pi|\\, m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!,\n    \\end{align*}\n    which yields $|\\bm{x}^\\pi| = n! / (m_1! \\cdots m_\\kappa!) \\leq n!$, as desired."
      },
