            "text": "Let $\\beta > 0$ be the separation bound for $(X,d)$. Let $\\xi, \\eta \\in \\mathscr{F}(X)$ with $\\xi \\neq \\eta$. If either $\\xi$ or $\\eta$ is empty, then Remark~\\ref{rem:hausdorff-convention} gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\xi,\\eta)=\\infty \\geq \\beta,\n    \\end{align*}\n    so the claim is immediate. Therefore, it remains to consider the case where both $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ are non-empty. Since $\\xi \\neq \\eta$, without loss of generality there exists an $x_0 \\in \\xi \\setminus \\eta$. In particular, since $x_0 \\notin \\eta$, it holds that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\min_{x^\\prime \\in \\eta} d(x_0,x^\\prime) \\geq \\beta,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and so\n    \\begin{align*}  \n        d_H(\\xi, \\eta) &= \\max\\left(\\max_{x \\in \\xi}\\min_{x^\\prime \\in \\eta} d(x,x^\\prime), \\max_{x^\\prime \\in \\eta} \\min_{x\\in \\xi} d(x,x^\\prime) \\right) \\\\&\\geq \\max_{x \\in \\xi}\\min_{x^\\prime \\in \\eta} d(x,x^\\prime) \\geq \\beta,\n    \\end{align*}\n    as desired.",
