      "order": 248,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Finite Metric Products",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:finite-metric-products}\n    Let $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and let $(X_1,d_1),\\dots,(X_n,d_n)$ be pseudometric spaces. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime)\\mapsto\\max_{i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}}d_i(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{x}^\\prime(i)),\\qquad \\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime\\in\\prod_{i=1}^n X_i,\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a pseudometric on $\\prod_{i=1}^n X_i$, called the maximum metric\\index{product metric}, and it is a metric if every $d_i$ is. If every $d_i$ is a metric, then the maximum metric induces the product topology of the topologies induced by $d_1,\\dots,d_n$, and the product is separable if every factor is separable and complete if every factor is complete.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 361,
        "references": [
          "prop:topology-extended-pseudometric"
        ],
        "source": "A - appendices/analysis.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__finite-metric-products__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that the maximum map defines a pseudometric (and a metric when each $d_i$ is a metric) that induces the product topology and inherits separability and completeness from the factors. The maximum vanishes on the diagonal and is symmetric because every $d_i$ is, and the triangle inequality holds in each coordinate and therefore for the maximum; if every $d_i$ is a metric, vanishing of the maximum implies equality in every coordinate. Next, the ball of radius $\\varepsilon$ around $\\bm{x}$ is $\\prod_{i=1}^n B^{d_i}_\\varepsilon(\\bm{x}(i))$, which is open in the product topology. Conversely, if $\\bm{x}\\in\\prod_{i=1}^n O_i$ with each $O_i$ open in $X_i$, choose $\\varepsilon_i>0$ with $B^{d_i}_{\\varepsilon_i}(\\bm{x}(i))\\subseteq O_i$; the ball of radius $\\min_i\\varepsilon_i$ around $\\bm{x}$ then lies in $\\prod_{i=1}^n O_i$. By the centred-ball characterisation following Proposition~\\ref{prop:topology-extended-pseudometric}, the two topologies coincide.\n\n    Finally, if $D_i\\subseteq X_i$ is countable and dense for each $i$, then $\\prod_{i=1}^n D_i$ is countable and dense, since for $\\bm{x}$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ we may choose $y_i\\in D_i$ with $d_i(\\bm{x}(i),y_i)<\\varepsilon$ for every $i$. If $(\\bm{x}_m)_{m\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is Cauchy, then so is each coordinate sequence. If every factor is complete, their limits $x_i\\in X_i$ form $\\bm{x}=(x_1,\\dots,x_n)$, and $\\bm{x}_m\\to\\bm{x}$ in every coordinate and hence in the maximum metric, as desired."
      },
