        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__finite-separable__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that there exists a countable and dense subset of $\\mathscr{F}(X)$. We construct one explicitly. First, note that since $X$ is separable, there is a countable and dense set $U \\subseteq X$. Now, we claim that $\\mathscr{F}(U)$ is countable and dense in $\\mathscr{F}(X)$.\n\n    The countability follows immediately from Proposition~\\ref{prop:countable-finite-sets}. Therefore, it suffices to show that $\\mathscr{F}(U)$ is dense in $\\mathscr{F}(X)$. Let $\\xi \\in \\mathscr{F}(X)$ and assume without loss of generality that $\\xi \\neq \\emptyset$; if $\\xi = \\emptyset$ then $\\xi \\in \\mathscr{F}(U)$. We must show that for any $\\varepsilon >0$ there exists a $\\upsilon \\in \\mathscr{F}(U)$ such that \n\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\upsilon, \\xi) < \\varepsilon. \n    \\end{align*}\n\n    Since the underlying space $X$ is separable, it holds that\n\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:separable-finite-bound}\n        \\forall x \\in X\\ \\exists u_x \\in U: d(x,u_x) < \\varepsilon. \n    \\end{align}\n\n    In particular, for each $x \\in \\xi \\subseteq X$, we can find an element $u_x \\in U$ such that \\eqref{eq:separable-finite-bound} holds. Let $\\upsilon=\\{u_x\\mid x\\in \\xi\\}$. Then $\\upsilon$ is a finite subset of $U$, and by construction we have that \n\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\forall x \\in \\xi\\ \\exists u \\in \\upsilon: d(x,u) < \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    and analogously\n\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\forall u \\in \\upsilon\\ \\exists x \\in \\xi: d(x,u) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then both $\\max_{x\\in\\xi}\\min_{u \\in \\upsilon} d(x,u) < \\varepsilon$ and $\\max_{u\\in\\upsilon}\\min_{x \\in \\xi} d(x,u) < \\varepsilon$, and hence \n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\xi,\\upsilon) = \\max\\left(\\max_{x\\in\\xi}\\min_{u \\in \\upsilon} d(x,u), \\max_{u\\in\\upsilon}\\min_{x \\in \\xi} d(x,u)\\right) < \\varepsilon\n    \\end{align*}\n    as desired."
