            "references": [],
            "text": "We must show that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists an $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        n,m\\geq N \\implies d_H(\\xi_n, \\xi_m) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Now, without loss of generality, assume that $n \\leq m$. If $n=m$, the claim is immediate. If $n<m$, then one easily verifies that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\xi_n, \\xi_m) = \\max_{x \\in \\xi_{m}} \\min_{x^\\prime \\in \\xi_n} d(x,x^\\prime)\n    \\end{align*}\n    since $\\xi_n \\subseteq \\xi_m$. In this case, the maximum may be taken over the newly added points $(x_k)_{k=n+1}^m$. In particular, it holds that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\xi_n, \\xi_m) =\\max_{k\\in\\{n+1,\\dots,m\\}} \\min_{i\\leq n} d(x_k,x_i) \\leq \\max_{k \\in \\{n+1,\\dots,m\\}} d(x_k, x_n).\n    \\end{align*}\n    We now use the fact that $(x_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is a Cauchy sequence. For any given $\\varepsilon>0$ there exists an $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that whenever $n,m\\geq N$ it holds that $d(x_n, x_m) < \\varepsilon$. For $m > n \\geq N$ every $k \\in \\{n+1,\\dots,m\\}$ satisfies $d(x_k, x_n) < \\varepsilon$, and hence \n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\xi_n, \\xi_m) \\leq \\max_{k \\in \\{n+1,\\dots,m\\}} d(x_k, x_n) < \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n     as desired.",
