      "statement": "\\label{prop:function-kernel-quotient-injective-extension}\n    Let $X$ and $Y$ be non-empty sets and $\\varphi : X \\to Y$ a function. Then, $\\varphi$ induces a well-defined injective mapping $\\Phi : X_\\varphi \\to Y$ such that, for every $x\\in X$, $\\Phi(x_\\varphi) = \\varphi(x)$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 879,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__function-kernel-quotient-injective-extension__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, we show that $\\Phi$ is well-defined. If $[x]_{\\overset{\\varphi}{\\sim}} = [x']_{\\overset{\\varphi}{\\sim}}$, then $x \\overset{\\varphi}{\\sim} x'$, hence $\\varphi(x)=\\varphi(x')$. Therefore the value $\\Phi([x]_{\\overset{\\varphi}{\\sim}})$ does not depend on the chosen representative.\n\n    We now show injectivity. We must show that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Phi(x_\\varphi) = \\Phi(x^\\prime_\\varphi) \\implies x_\\varphi = x^\\prime_\\varphi.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Let $x_\\varphi, x^\\prime_\\varphi \\in X_\\varphi$ be arbitrary. By the definition of $\\Phi$, we have that $\\Phi(x_\\varphi) = \\Phi(x^\\prime_\\varphi)$ implies that $\\varphi(x) = \\varphi(x^\\prime)$, which implies that $x \\overset{\\varphi}{\\sim} x^\\prime$, and hence $x_\\varphi = x^\\prime_\\varphi$, as desired."
      },
