      "order": 258,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Monoids, Groups, and Completion",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:grothendieck-universal}\n    Let $(X,+)$ be a cancellative abelian monoid with identity element $e$, and let $G(X)$ and $\\iota$ be as in Definition~\\ref{defn:grothendieck-group}. Then\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $(G(X),+)$ is an abelian group with identity element $[(e,e)]$, and $-[(x,y)]=[(y,x)]$ for all $x,y\\in X$;\n        \\item $\\iota$ is an injective monoid homomorphism, and $[(x,y)]=\\iota(x)-\\iota(y)$ for all $x,y\\in X$;\n        \\item for every abelian group $(H,+)$ and every monoid homomorphism $\\psi:X\\to H$, there is a unique monoid homomorphism $\\bar{\\psi}:G(X)\\to H$ such that $\\bar{\\psi}\\circ\\iota=\\psi$, namely $\\bar{\\psi}([(x,y)])=\\psi(x)-\\psi(y)$.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 83,
        "references": [],
        "source": "A - appendices/algebra.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__grothendieck-universal__proof.svg",
        "text": "We establish properties (i), (ii), and (iii) in turn.\n\n    For (i), associativity and commutativity descend from componentwise addition on $X\\times X$, and $[(x,y)]+[(e,e)]=[(x,y)]$. If $(x,y)\\sim(x^\\prime,y^\\prime)$, then commutativity gives $y+x^\\prime=y^\\prime+x$, so $(y,x)\\sim(y^\\prime,x^\\prime)$, and the class $[(y,x)]$ does not depend on the representative of $[(x,y)]$. Moreover, $[(x,y)]+[(y,x)]=[(x+y,y+x)]=[(e,e)]$, since $(x+y)+e=e+(y+x)$ by the identity law and commutativity.\n\n    For (ii), $\\iota(x+x^\\prime)=[(x+x^\\prime,e+e)]=\\iota(x)+\\iota(x^\\prime)$, and $\\iota(e)=[(e,e)]$ is the identity element of $G(X)$. If $\\iota(x)=\\iota(x^\\prime)$, then $(x,e)\\sim(x^\\prime,e)$, that is, $x+e=x^\\prime+e$, and hence $x=x^\\prime$. Finally, by (i), $\\iota(x)-\\iota(y)=[(x,e)]+[(e,y)]=[(x,y)]$.\n\n    For (iii), if $(x,y)\\sim(x^\\prime,y^\\prime)$, then $\\psi(x)+\\psi(y^\\prime)=\\psi(x^\\prime)+\\psi(y)$, so $\\psi(x)-\\psi(y)=\\psi(x^\\prime)-\\psi(y^\\prime)$, and $\\bar{\\psi}$ is well-defined. It is additive because $\\psi$ is additive, $\\bar{\\psi}([(e,e)])=\\psi(e)-\\psi(e)=0$, and $\\bar{\\psi}(\\iota(x))=\\psi(x)-\\psi(e)=\\psi(x)$. If $\\chi:G(X)\\to H$ is any monoid homomorphism with $\\chi\\circ\\iota=\\psi$, then $\\chi$ preserves inverses, and (ii) gives $\\chi([(x,y)])=\\chi(\\iota(x))-\\chi(\\iota(y))=\\psi(x)-\\psi(y)$, so $\\chi=\\bar{\\psi}$, as desired."
      },
