            "text": "Next, we prove \\eqref{property:tie-break-unique}. We must show that for every $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$, there exists a unique sorted tuple $\\bm{y} \\in \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq_I)$ of the form $\\bm{y} = \\bm{x} \\circ \\pi$ with $\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}_{\\ell(\\bm{x})}$. If $\\bm{x} = \\bm{e}$, the unique sorted rearrangement is $\\bm{e}$. Suppose therefore that $n = \\ell(\\bm{x}) > 0$. By \\eqref{property:tie-break-order} and the injectivity of $H$ on the entries of $\\bm{x}$, the image $\\{\\bm{x}(1), \\dots, \\bm{x}(n)\\}$ consists of $n$ distinct elements that are strictly totally ordered by $\\prec_I$. Consequently, any permutation $\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$ for which $\\bm{x} \\circ \\pi \\in \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq_I)$ must satisfy\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}(\\pi(1)) \\prec_I \\bm{x}(\\pi(2)) \\prec_I \\dots \\prec_I \\bm{x}(\\pi(n)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which uniquely determines $\\pi(r)$ for every $r \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$. Therefore, $\\bm{x}$ admits a unique sorted rearrangement, and every sorting map $\\varsigma : \\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq_I)$ returns it, as desired.",
