      "section": "Primitive Actions",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:identifier-tie-breaking}\n    Let $Y$ be a set, let $(I,\\leq)$ be a countable, totally ordered set, let $X=Y\\times I$ with $\\mathrm{id}(y,i)=i$, and let $\\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)=\\{\\bm{x}\\in\\mathscr{T}(X)\\mid\\mathrm{id}\\circ\\bm{x}\\text{ is injective}\\}$. Let $(K,<)$ be a strictly totally ordered set, let $h:Y\\to K$ be a map, and let $H:X\\to K\\times I$ be given by $H(y,i)=(h(y),i)$. Let $\\sqsubset$ be the strict lexicographic order on $K\\times I$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (a,i)\\sqsubset(b,j)\\iff a<b\\ \\lor\\ \\bigl(a=b\\land i<j\\bigr),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and define the relation $\\preceq_I$ on $X$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        x\\preceq_I x^\\prime\\iff x=x^\\prime\\ \\lor\\ H(x)\\sqsubset H(x^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $(X,\\preceq_I)$ is a partially ordered set, and the restriction of $\\preceq_I$ to the entries of any $\\bm{x}\\in\\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$ is total, and\\label{property:tie-break-order}\n        \\item each $\\bm{x}\\in\\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$ has exactly one sorted rearrangement, so all sorting maps $\\varsigma:\\mathscr{T}(X)\\to\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq_I)$ agree on $\\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$.\\label{property:tie-break-unique}\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
