        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__identifier-tie-breaking__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, we prove \\eqref{property:tie-break-order}. We must show that $\\preceq_I$ is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive on $X$, and that for every $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$ and all $k, l \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$, either $\\bm{x}(k) \\preceq_I \\bm{x}(l)$ or $\\bm{x}(l) \\preceq_I \\bm{x}(k)$.\n\n    For reflexivity, note that $x = x$ implies $x \\preceq_I x$ for every $x \\in X$ by definition. For antisymmetry, suppose that $x \\preceq_I x^\\prime$ and $x^\\prime \\preceq_I x$ for some $x, x^\\prime \\in X$. If $x \\neq x^\\prime$, then the definition of $\\preceq_I$ requires both $H(x) \\sqsubset H(x^\\prime)$ and $H(x^\\prime) \\sqsubset H(x)$, which is impossible because the strict lexicographic order $\\sqsubset$ on $K \\times I$ is asymmetric. Hence $x = x^\\prime$. For transitivity, suppose that $x \\preceq_I x^\\prime$ and $x^\\prime \\preceq_I x^{\\prime\\prime}$. If $x = x^\\prime$ or $x^\\prime = x^{\\prime\\prime}$, then $x \\preceq_I x^{\\prime\\prime}$ holds immediately; otherwise, $H(x) \\sqsubset H(x^\\prime)$ and $H(x^\\prime) \\sqsubset H(x^{\\prime\\prime})$, and the transitivity of $\\sqsubset$ yields $H(x) \\sqsubset H(x^{\\prime\\prime})$, whence $x \\preceq_I x^{\\prime\\prime}$. Thus $(X, \\preceq_I)$ is a partially ordered set.\n\n    Now let $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$ and let $k, l \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$. If $k = l$, then $\\bm{x}(k) = \\bm{x}(l)$ and hence $\\bm{x}(k) \\preceq_I \\bm{x}(l)$. If $k \\neq l$, then injectivity of $\\mathrm{id} \\circ \\bm{x}$ implies that $\\mathrm{id}(\\bm{x}(k)) \\neq \\mathrm{id}(\\bm{x}(l))$, and consequently\n    \\begin{align*}\n        H(\\bm{x}(k)) = \\bigl(h(\\varpi(\\bm{x}(k))), \\mathrm{id}(\\bm{x}(k))\\bigr) \\neq \\bigl(h(\\varpi(\\bm{x}(l))), \\mathrm{id}(\\bm{x}(l))\\bigr) = H(\\bm{x}(l)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Because $\\sqsubset$ is a strict total order on $K \\times I$, either $H(\\bm{x}(k)) \\sqsubset H(\\bm{x}(l))$ or $H(\\bm{x}(l)) \\sqsubset H(\\bm{x}(k))$, which implies that either $\\bm{x}(k) \\preceq_I \\bm{x}(l)$ or $\\bm{x}(l) \\preceq_I \\bm{x}(k)$.\n\n    Next, we prove \\eqref{property:tie-break-unique}. We must show that for every $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}_{\\neq}(X)$, there exists a unique sorted tuple $\\bm{y} \\in \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq_I)$ of the form $\\bm{y} = \\bm{x} \\circ \\pi$ with $\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}_{\\ell(\\bm{x})}$. If $\\bm{x} = \\bm{e}$, the unique sorted rearrangement is $\\bm{e}$. Suppose therefore that $n = \\ell(\\bm{x}) > 0$. By \\eqref{property:tie-break-order} and the injectivity of $H$ on the entries of $\\bm{x}$, the image $\\{\\bm{x}(1), \\dots, \\bm{x}(n)\\}$ consists of $n$ distinct elements that are strictly totally ordered by $\\prec_I$. Consequently, any permutation $\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$ for which $\\bm{x} \\circ \\pi \\in \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq_I)$ must satisfy\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}(\\pi(1)) \\prec_I \\bm{x}(\\pi(2)) \\prec_I \\dots \\prec_I \\bm{x}(\\pi(n)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which uniquely determines $\\pi(r)$ for every $r \\in \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$. Therefore, $\\bm{x}$ admits a unique sorted rearrangement, and every sorting map $\\varsigma : \\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq_I)$ returns it, as desired."
