      "statement": "\\label{prop:induced-sorting}\n Let $(X, \\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set, and let $\\varphi:\\mathscr{P} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ be a bijection. Then, the function $\\varsigma:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ defined by \n \\begin{align*}\n     \\varsigma(\\bm{x}) = \\begin{cases}\n         \\varsigma_{\\pi_{(1)}}(\\bm{x}) & \\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{U}_1 \\\\\n         ~~~~\\vdots ~~~~ &~~~~\\vdots \\\\ \n         \\varsigma_{\\pi_{(n)}}(\\bm{x}) & \\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{U}_n \\\\ \n         ~~~~\\vdots ~~~~ &~~~~\\vdots \\\\ \n     \\end{cases}\n \\end{align*}\n    is a sorting map, where $\\pi_{(n)} = \\varphi^{-1}(n)$, $\\varsigma_{\\pi_{(i)}} : \\mathscr{U}_i \\to \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq)$ are defined by $\\varsigma_{\\pi_{(i)}}(\\bm{x}) = \\bm{x}[\\pi_{(i)}]$, and $\\mathscr{U}_i = \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(i)}} \\setminus \\bigcup_{j < i} \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi_{(j)}}$.",
