      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:inverse-permuted-tuple-measure-continuous}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a complete separable metric space, $(\\mathscr{T}^\\pi(X), \\mathscr{d}_\\pi)$ be the associated permuted-tuple matching space, and $(\\mathcal{N}(X), \\rho)$ be the space of integer-valued measures endowed with the Prokhorov distance. Then the mapping $(\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1} : \\mathcal{N}(X)\\to\\mathscr{T}^\\pi(X)$ is continuous.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 630,
        "references": [
          "lem:helpful-point-process",
          "thm:sufficient-decomposition",
          "lem:disintegration-lemma",
          "defn:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-distance"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__inverse-permuted-tuple-measure-continuous__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ and $\\xi_0 \\in \\mathcal{N}(X)$ there exists a $\\theta > 0$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\rho(\\xi, \\xi_0) < \\theta \\implies \\mathscr{d}_\\pi((\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi), (\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi_0)) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $\\xi_0 = 0$, choose $0<\\theta < \\min\\{\\varepsilon,1\\}$. If $\\rho(\\xi,\\xi_0)<\\theta$, then Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-point-process} gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi(X)=\\xi_0(X)=0,\n    \\end{align*}\n    so $\\xi=0$ and the conclusion is immediate. Therefore, assume that $\\xi_0 \\neq 0$.\n\n    By Theorem~\\ref{thm:sufficient-decomposition}, $\\xi_0$ admits the decomposition\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi_0 = \\sum_{k \\leq \\kappa} \\beta_k \\delta_{x_k},\n    \\end{align*}\n    with $(\\beta_k)_{k \\leq \\kappa} \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ and distinct $(x_k)_{k \\leq \\kappa} \\subseteq X$. Define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        S_0 = 0, \\qquad S_k = \\sum_{r=1}^k \\beta_r \\quad \\text{for } k \\in \\{1,\\dots,\\kappa\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and let $\\bm{x}_0 \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ be the tuple defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}_0(i) = x_k \\quad \\text{whenever } S_{k-1} < i \\leq S_k.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}_0)=\\xi_0$, so $(\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi_0)=\\bm{x}_0^\\pi$.\n\n    Let $\\gamma$ be as in Lemma~\\ref{lem:disintegration-lemma} for the atoms of $\\xi_0$, that is,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\gamma = \\begin{cases}\n            1, & \\kappa = 1,\\\\\n            \\min\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\min_{\\substack{k,j \\leq \\kappa \\\\ k \\neq j}} d(x_k, x_j), 1\\right), & \\kappa \\geq 2,\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    and choose $0<\\theta < \\min\\{\\varepsilon,\\gamma\\}$. Let $\\xi \\in \\mathcal{N}(X)$ satisfy $\\rho(\\xi,\\xi_0)<\\theta$. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:sufficient-decomposition}, $\\xi$ admits the decomposition\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi = \\sum_{l \\leq \\lambda} \\alpha_l \\delta_{y_l},\n    \\end{align*}\n    with $(\\alpha_l)_{l \\leq \\lambda} \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ and distinct $(y_l)_{l \\leq \\lambda} \\subseteq X$.\n\n    Since $\\rho(\\xi,\\xi_0)<\\theta<1$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-point-process} gives $\\xi(X)=\\xi_0(X)>0$, so $\\xi\\neq0$, and by Lemma~\\ref{lem:disintegration-lemma}, applied with $\\varepsilon=\\theta$, there exist pairwise disjoint sets $L_k \\subseteq \\{1,\\dots,\\lambda\\}$, $k \\in \\{1,\\dots,\\kappa\\}$, covering $\\{1,\\dots,\\lambda\\}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta_k = \\sum_{l\\in L_k} \\alpha_l\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        d(x_k,y_l) < \\theta \\quad \\forall l \\in L_k.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Choose an enumeration $L_k = \\{l_{k,1},\\dots,l_{k,m_k}\\}$ for each $k$, and define partial sums\n    \\begin{align*}\n        M_{k,0} = 0, \\qquad M_{k,r} = \\sum_{s=1}^r \\alpha_{l_{k,s}} \\quad \\text{for } r \\in \\{1,\\dots,m_k\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Define a tuple $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ by declaring that, on the $k$-th block $S_{k-1} < i \\leq S_k$, one has\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}(i) = y_{l_{k,r}} \\quad \\text{whenever } S_{k-1}+M_{k,r-1} < i \\leq S_{k-1}+M_{k,r}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\sum_{l\\in L_k} \\alpha_l = \\beta_k$, this indeed fills the entire $k$-th block. Because the sets $L_k$ cover $\\{1,\\dots,\\lambda\\}$, every atom of $\\xi$ is listed with its multiplicity, so $\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})=\\xi$, so $(\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi)=\\bm{x}^\\pi$.\n\n    Finally, for every index $i$ in the $k$-th block we have $\\bm{x}_0(i)=x_k$ and $\\bm{x}(i)=y_l$ for some $l\\in L_k$, hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{x}_0(i)) < \\theta.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}_0) < \\theta < \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and consequently\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_\\pi((\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi),(\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi_0))\n        = \\mathscr{d}_\\pi(\\bm{x}^\\pi,\\bm{x}_0^\\pi)\n        \\leq \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}_0)\n        < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The inequality uses the identity matching in Definition~\\ref{defn:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-distance} and $\\bar{d}\\leq d$. This proves continuity."
      },
