      "statement": "\\label{prop:kernel-space-hilbert-iff-image-closed}\n    Let $X$ be a non-empty set, $(\\mathcal{H}, \\langle \\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle)$ a Hilbert space, and $\\varphi: X \\to \\mathcal{H}$ a weakly linear operator. Then, $(X_\\varphi, \\llangle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rrangle)$ is a Hilbert space if and only if $\\varphi(X)$ is closed in $(\\mathcal{H}, \\langle \\cdot,\\cdot \\rangle)$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 941,
        "references": [
          "prop:function-kernel-quotient-injective-extension"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__kernel-space-hilbert-iff-image-closed__proof.svg",
        "text": "By Proposition~\\ref{prop:function-kernel-quotient-injective-extension}, the map $\\Phi:X_\\varphi \\to \\varphi(X)$ is bijective, and by the definition of $\\llangle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rrangle$ it is an isometry between $(X_\\varphi,\\llangle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rrangle)$ and the subspace $\\varphi(X)$ of $\\mathcal{H}$. Hence $(X_\\varphi,\\llangle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rrangle)$ is complete if and only if $\\varphi(X)$ is complete. A subspace of a Hilbert space is complete if and only if it is closed. Therefore the kernel space is a Hilbert space if and only if $\\varphi(X)$ is closed."
      },
