      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 270,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Linearly Parametrised Curves",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:linear-parametrisation-convex}\n    Let $\\Lambda : \\mathpzc{P} \\to \\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1]; \\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the pointwise linear parametrisation operator of Definition~\\ref{defn:linear-parametrisation-operator}. Then $\\Lambda$ is weakly convex.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 223,
        "references": [
          "defn:weakly-convex-operator"
        ],
        "source": "2 - day-ahead/2_clearing_operator_price_curves.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__linear-parametrisation-convex__proof.svg",
        "text": "By Definition~\\ref{defn:weakly-convex-operator}, we must show that for all $f,g\\in\\mathpzc{P}$ and all $\\alpha,\\beta\\geq0$, there exists a price curve $h\\in\\mathpzc{P}$ such that $\\alpha\\Lambda(f)+\\beta\\Lambda(g)=\\Lambda(h)$. Fix $f,g\\in\\mathpzc{P}$ and $\\alpha,\\beta\\geq0$. The first and second coordinate functions of $\\alpha\\Lambda(f)+\\beta\\Lambda(g)$ are given by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        q&=\\alpha\\lambda_f+\\beta\\lambda_g,\\\\\n        p&=\\alpha f\\circ\\lambda_f+\\beta g\\circ\\lambda_g.\n    \\end{align*}\n    On $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1]$, respectively, the first component satisfies\n    \\begin{align*}\n        q(u)=-(\\alpha l_f+\\beta l_g)u,\n        \\qquad\n        q(u)=(\\alpha r_f+\\beta r_g)u.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence $q$ is the linear parametrisation of the interval\n    \\begin{align*}\n        [\\alpha l_f+\\beta l_g,\\,\\alpha r_f+\\beta r_g].\n    \\end{align*}\n    Next, suppose that $u,v\\in[-1,1]$ satisfy $u<v$ and $q(u)=q(v)$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        0\n        &=q(v)-q(u)\\\\\n        &=\\alpha\\bigl(\\lambda_f(v)-\\lambda_f(u)\\bigr)\n          +\\beta\\bigl(\\lambda_g(v)-\\lambda_g(u)\\bigr).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Because $\\lambda_f$ and $\\lambda_g$ are increasing, both differences on the right-hand side are non-negative. Hence each difference with a strictly positive coefficient vanishes, so $f\\circ\\lambda_f$ (respectively $g\\circ\\lambda_g$) is constant on $[u,v]$ whenever $\\alpha>0$ (respectively $\\beta>0$). It follows that $p(u)=p(v)$.\n\n    Consequently, the rule\n    \\begin{align*}\n        h(x)=p(u)\n        \\qquad\\text{for any }u\\in[-1,1]\\text{ such that }q(u)=x\n    \\end{align*}\n    defines a well-defined, increasing, piecewise-affine function $h\\in\\mathpzc{P}$ on $\\mathrm{image}(q)$, which is simple whenever $f$ and $g$ are simple, since on each half of $[-1,1]$ the map $q$ is linear and $p$ is piecewise affine in $u$. Moreover, by construction, $q=\\lambda_h$ and $p=h\\circ\\lambda_h$, which yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha\\Lambda(f)+\\beta\\Lambda(g)=\\Lambda(h).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus $\\Lambda$ is weakly convex, as desired."
      },
