      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 272,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Execution Mandate",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:mandate-martingale}\nLet $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $K\\in\\mathbb{N}_+$ and $\\mathsf{TV}>0$, let $(\\xi_j)_{j=1}^{K}$ be independent and identically distributed standard normal random variables on $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$, and define $(w_k)_{k=0}^{K}$ and $(\\mathcal{G}_k)_{k=0}^{K}$ as in Definition~\\ref{defn:se-mandate}. Then $\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\lvert w_k-w_{k-1}\\rvert=\\mathsf{TV}$ almost surely, and $(w_k)_{k=0}^{K}$ is a bounded $(\\mathcal{G}_k)_{k=0}^{K}$-martingale.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 28,
        "references": [],
        "source": "3 - intraday/5_applications.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__mandate-martingale__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that $\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\lvert w_k-w_{k-1}\\rvert=\\mathsf{TV}$ almost surely and that for every $k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,K-1\\}$, the random variable $w_k$ is $\\mathcal{G}_k$-measurable and integrable, and that\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\mathbb{E}[w_{k+1}-w_k\\mid\\mathcal{G}_k]=0\\qquad\\text{almost surely.}\n\\end{align*}\nFirst, note that $S(\\bm{\\xi})=\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\lvert\\xi_j\\rvert>0$ almost surely because the $\\xi_j$ are continuous random variables. Summing the absolute increments gives $\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\lvert w_k-w_{k-1}\\rvert=\\mathsf{TV}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\lvert\\xi_k\\rvert/S(\\bm{\\xi})=\\mathsf{TV}$ almost surely. Moreover, for every $k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,K\\}$, the triangle inequality gives\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\lvert w_k\\rvert\\le\\mathsf{TV}\\,\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^{k}\\lvert\\xi_j\\rvert}{\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\lvert\\xi_j\\rvert}\\le\\mathsf{TV}\\qquad\\text{almost surely,}\n\\end{align*}\nso $w_k$ is bounded and hence integrable, and $\\lvert w_{k+1}-w_k\\rvert\\le\\mathsf{TV}$. By definition of $\\mathcal{G}_k$, each $w_k$ is $\\mathcal{G}_k$-measurable.\n\nTo establish the martingale identity, it suffices by definition of conditional expectation to show that for every bounded Borel-measurable function $h\\colon\\mathbb{R}^{k+1}\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\mathbb{E}\\bigl[(w_{k+1}-w_k)\\,h(w_0,\\ldots,w_k)\\bigr]=0.\n\\end{align*}\nTo this end, fix $k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,K-1\\}$ and define the sign-flipping map $T_{k+1}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^K\\to\\mathbb{R}^K$ on the underlying Gaussian vector $\\bm{\\xi}=(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_K)$ by\n\\begin{align*}\n    T_{k+1}(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_k,\\xi_{k+1},\\xi_{k+2},\\ldots,\\xi_K)\n    = (\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_k,-\\xi_{k+1},\\xi_{k+2},\\ldots,\\xi_K).\n\\end{align*}\nBecause the components of $\\bm{\\xi}$ are independent and $\\xi_{k+1}\\stackrel{d}{=}-\\xi_{k+1}$, the transformed vector $\\tilde{\\bm{\\xi}}=T_{k+1}(\\bm{\\xi})$ has the same joint distribution as $\\bm{\\xi}$. Moreover, since $\\lvert-\\xi_{k+1}\\rvert=\\lvert\\xi_{k+1}\\rvert$, the denominator $S(\\bm{\\xi})=\\sum_{j=1}^{K}\\lvert\\xi_j\\rvert$ satisfies $S(\\tilde{\\bm{\\xi}})=S(\\bm{\\xi})$.\n\nFor every $r\\in\\{0,\\ldots,k\\}$, the numerator $\\sum_{j=1}^{r}\\xi_j$ does not involve $\\xi_{k+1}$, so $w_r(\\tilde{\\bm{\\xi}})=w_r(\\bm{\\xi})$. By contrast, the $(k+1)$-th increment satisfies\n\\begin{align*}\n    (w_{k+1}-w_k)(\\tilde{\\bm{\\xi}})=\\mathsf{TV}\\,\\frac{-\\xi_{k+1}}{S(\\bm{\\xi})}=-(w_{k+1}-w_k)(\\bm{\\xi}).\n\\end{align*}\nConsequently, equality in law between $\\bm{\\xi}$ and $\\tilde{\\bm{\\xi}}$ yields\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\mathbb{E}\\bigl[(w_{k+1}-w_k)\\,h(w_0,\\ldots,w_k)\\bigr]\n    = \\mathbb{E}\\bigl[-(w_{k+1}-w_k)\\,h(w_0,\\ldots,w_k)\\bigr],\n\\end{align*}\nwhich implies $\\mathbb{E}\\bigl[(w_{k+1}-w_k)\\,h(w_0,\\ldots,w_k)\\bigr]=0$, as desired."
      },
