      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:measurable-partition-sorting}\n    Let $(X, \\preceq, d)$ be a metric space endowed with a Borel partial order. Let $\\varphi:\\mathscr{P}\\to \\mathbb{N}$ be a bijection. Then, the sets \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{U}_n = \\mathscr{V}_{\\varphi^{-1}(n)} \\setminus\\bigcup_{\\substack{m \\in \\mathbb{N}:\\\\ m < n }} \\mathscr{V}_{\\varphi^{-1}(m)}\n    \\end{align*}\n    form a measurable partition of $\\mathscr{T}(X)$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1850,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__measurable-partition-sorting__proof.svg",
        "text": "Note that by construction we have \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathscr{U}_n = \\bigcup_{\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}} \\mathscr{V}_\\pi = \\mathscr{T}(X),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and $\\mathscr{U}_n \\cap \\mathscr{U}_m = \\emptyset$ for all $n,m\\in \\mathbb{N}: n \\neq m$. Therefore, it suffices to show that each $\\mathscr{U}_n$ is measurable. To this end, note that it is enough to show that each $\\mathscr{V}_\\pi$ for $\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}$ is measurable, since the union over $m<n$ in the definition of $\\mathscr{U}_n$ is finite. \n\n    Notice that, by definition, we have \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{V}_\\pi = \\{\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X) \\mid \\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\pi) \\text{ and } \\bm{x} \\circ \\pi \\in \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq)\\} \\subseteq X^{\\ell(\\pi)}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Let $N = \\ell(\\pi)$ and note that by the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, it suffices to show that $\\mathscr{V}_\\pi$ is measurable in $X^N$ endowed with the maximum product metric and the associated Borel $\\sigma$-algebra. Since $X$ is metric, the diagonal is closed, and hence the strict order relation $\\prec=\\preceq\\setminus\\Delta_X$ is Borel whenever $\\preceq$ is Borel. Thus the set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        A_{i,j} =\\{\\bm{x}:\\{1,\\dots,N\\} \\to X \\mid \\bm{x}(\\pi(j)) \\prec \\bm{x}(\\pi(i))\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is Borel, for each $i,j \\in \\{1,\\dots,N\\}$, being the preimage of the Borel set $\\prec\\, \\subseteq X \\times X$ under the continuous coordinate map $\\bm{x} \\mapsto (\\bm{x}(\\pi(j)),\\bm{x}(\\pi(i)))$ from $X^N$ to $X\\times X$. Consequently, the complement, $A_{i,j}^{\\mathtt{C}}$, is measurable, and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{V}_{\\pi} &= \\{ \\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X) \\mid N=\\ell(\\bm{x}) \\text{ and } \\bm{x}\\circ\\pi \\in \\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)\\} \\\\\n        &= \\{\\bm{x} \\in X^{N} \\mid \\forall i,j \\in \\{1,\\dots, N\\}: i \\leq j \\implies \\neg(\\bm{x}(\\pi(j)) \\prec \\bm{x}(\\pi(i)))\\}\\\\\n        &=\\bigcap_{\\substack{i,j\\in \\{1,\\dots,N\\}\\\\i \\leq j}} \\{\\bm{x} \\in X^{N} \\mid \\neg(\\bm{x}(\\pi(j)) \\prec \\bm{x}(\\pi(i)))\\}\n        \\\\\n        &=\\bigcap_{\\substack{i,j \\in \\{1,\\dots,N\\}: \\\\ i \\leq j}} A_{i,j}^{\\mathtt{C}}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is therefore a finite intersection of measurable sets and hence measurable."
      },
