      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 278,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Sorting",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:minimal-sorting-measurable}\n    For $\\preceq \\in \\{\\leq, \\geq\\}$, where $\\{\\leq, \\geq\\}$ are the usual order relations on $\\mathbb{R}$, the $\\preceq$-minimal permutation map is a Borel measurable map between $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})$ and the set of finite permutations, $\\mathscr{P}$, where both are endowed with the tuple Hausdorff distance induced by the Euclidean distance.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 364,
        "references": [
          "prop:permutations-countable",
          "lem:tuple-space-measurable-2"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/4_market_clearing.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__minimal-sorting-measurable__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for any $B\\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{P})$, it holds that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\pi^\\preceq)^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})). \n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\mathscr{P}$ is countable (Proposition~\\ref{prop:permutations-countable}), we first notice that $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{P})$ is equal to the power set, $\\wp(\\mathscr{P})$, as each singleton $\\{\\pi\\}\\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{P})$ and each subset $B \\subseteq \\mathscr{P}$ is countable and may therefore be written as\n    \\begin{align*}\n        B = \\bigcup_{\\pi \\in B} \\{\\pi\\}. \n    \\end{align*}\n    As $\\sigma$-algebras are closed under countable unions, it suffices to prove that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\pi^\\preceq)^{-1}(\\{\\pi\\}) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})),\n    \\end{align*}\n    for any $\\pi \\in \\mathscr{P}$. Let $\\mathscr{W}_\\pi$ be the set defined by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{W}_\\pi = (\\pi^\\preceq)^{-1}(\\{\\pi\\}) =\\{\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R}) \\mid \\pi^\\preceq(\\bm{x}) = \\pi\\}. \n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\ell(\\pi)$ is fixed, one has\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{W}_\\pi = \\{\\bm{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{\\ell(\\pi)} \\mid \\pi^\\preceq(\\bm{x}) = \\pi\\} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^{\\ell(\\pi)}. \n    \\end{align*}\n    Any set that is Borel in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ will also be Borel in $\\mathscr{T}(\\mathbb{R})$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:tuple-space-measurable-2}. To this end, let $n = \\ell(\\pi)$ and define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        D_{k-1}^\\pi =\\{\\pi(j) \\mid j \\in \\{k, \\dots, n\\}\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and, by extension, the sets \n    \\begin{align*}\n        A^\\pi_k =\\bigcap_{j \\in D_{k-1}^\\pi} \\{\\bm{x}\\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid \\bm{x}(\\pi(k))\\preceq\\bm{x}(j)\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Each $A_k^\\pi$ is measurable because, for every fixed $j$, the set $\\{\\bm{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n\\mid\\bm{x}(\\pi(k))\\preceq\\bm{x}(j)\\}$ is Borel. Moreover, define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        B^{\\pi}_k = \\bigcap_{\\substack{j \\in D^\\pi_{k-1}\\\\j < \\pi(k)}} \\{\\bm{x}\\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid \\bm{x}(\\pi(k)) \\neq \\bm{x}(j)\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    which is measurable as the diagonal is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and finite intersections of measurable sets are measurable. In addition, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{W}_\\pi =\\bigcap_{k = 1}^n (A^\\pi_k \\cap B^\\pi_k).\n    \\end{align*}\n    To see why, note that $A^\\pi_k$ contains the tuples for which $\\pi(k)$ attains the $k^{\\mathrm{th}}$ selected minimum. The set $B^\\pi_k$, in turn, enforces the tie-breaking rule by preferring the smallest original index. To see this, if $j \\in D_{k-1}^\\pi = \\{\\pi(k), \\dots, \\pi(n)\\}$, then we may write $j = \\pi(m)$ for some $m \\geq k$; the condition $j < \\pi(k)$ forces $m > k$. Consequently, we may write \n    \\begin{align*}\n        B_k^\\pi = \\bigcap_{\\substack{m \\in \\{k+1, \\dots, n\\}\\\\ \\pi(m) < \\pi(k)}}\\{\\bm{x}\\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid \\bm{x}(\\pi(k))\\neq \\bm{x}(\\pi(m))  \\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    where the set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\{\\bm{x}\\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid \\bm{x}(\\pi(k))\\neq \\bm{x}(\\pi(m))\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    consists of the tuples in which these two entries differ. If there were an $m \\in \\{k+1, \\dots, n\\}$ such that $\\bm{x}(\\pi(k)) = \\bm{x}(\\pi(m))$ but $\\pi(m) < \\pi(k)$, then $\\pi^\\preceq(\\bm{x})$ would have selected $\\pi(m)$ before $\\pi(k)$, a contradiction. The sets $A_k^\\pi$ and $B_k^\\pi$ are Borel for each $k \\in \\{1,\\dots, n\\}$. Thus $\\mathscr{W}_\\pi$ is a finite intersection of Borel sets and hence Borel."
      },
