        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__minimum-sorting-map-is-sorting-map__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, note that $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{e}) = \\bm{e}$, which is sorted by definition. Let $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ with $\\ell(\\bm{x}) > 0$, and write\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathcal{K}(\\bm{x}) = \\{K_1,\\dots,K_\\kappa\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    where the components are ordered by first appearance. Since each $K_r$ is a labelled finite set, the tuple $\\varsigma(K_r)$ is well-defined by Definition~\\ref{defn:sorting-finite-sets}, and so is the concatenation\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{x}) = \\bigoplus_{r=1}^{\\kappa} \\varsigma(K_r).\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    Each block $\\varsigma(K_r)$ is sorted by construction. Moreover, if $r < s$, then every element of $K_r$ is incomparable with every element of $K_s$ by Proposition~\\ref{prop:components-partition-incomp}. Hence no element from the later block can be strictly smaller than an element from the earlier block. Therefore, the concatenation of the sorted blocks is again sorted, so $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{x}) \\in \\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$.\n\n    It remains to verify the permutation property. For each component $K_r$, the tuple $\\pi(K_r)$ produced in Definition~\\ref{defn:sorting-finite-sets} lists exactly the indices appearing in $K_r$, in the order used by $\\varsigma(K_r)$. In particular, by Lemma~\\ref{lem:labelled-finite-set-natural-projection-injective}, $\\pi(K_r)$ is a bijection from $\\{1,\\dots,|K_r|\\}$ onto the index set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_r = \\Pi_{\\mathbb{N}}(K_r).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since the family $\\mathcal{K}(\\bm{x})$ is a partition of $\\Gamma(\\bm{x})$, the sets $I_1,\\dots,I_\\kappa$ form a partition of $\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$. Consequently, the concatenation\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\sigma = \\bigoplus_{r = 1}^{\\kappa} \\pi(K_r)\n    \\end{align*}\n    has length $\\ell(\\bm{x})$ and image $\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$. Because the images of the blocks are pairwise disjoint, $\\sigma:\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}) \\to \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$ is bijective.\n\n    By construction, the tuple $\\bm{x}\\circ \\sigma$ first lists the entries from $K_1$ in the order prescribed by $\\varsigma(K_1)$, then the entries from $K_2$ in the order prescribed by $\\varsigma(K_2)$, and so on. Hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}\\circ\\sigma = \\bigoplus_{r=1}^{\\kappa} \\varsigma(K_r) = \\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}(\\bm{x}),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which proves that $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{min}}$ is a sorting map."
