      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 282,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Stream Matching",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:parser-two-level-exactness}\n    Under the assumptions and notation of Definition~\\ref{defn:parser-inner-outer-programs}, in the global integer program~\\eqref{eq:intraday-global-matching-objective} and in the subproblem~\\eqref{eq:intraday-inner-match-main}, the binary channel constraints $\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}} x_{i,j}\\le\\beta_j$ and $\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{A}} y_{u,j}\\le 1-\\beta_j$ for $\\beta_j\\in\\{0,1\\}$ are equivalent to the single assignment constraint $\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}} x_{i,j} + \\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{A}} y_{u,j} \\le 1$ for each $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$. Furthermore, the terminal value $V_{N,M}$ of the recursion~\\eqref{eq:intraday-outer-dp-main} equals the maximum objective value of the global integer program~\\eqref{eq:intraday-global-matching-objective}.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 377,
        "references": [
          "eq:intraday-global-matching-objective",
          "eq:intraday-outer-dp-main"
        ],
        "source": "3 - intraday/4_simulation_of_the_lob.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__parser-two-level-exactness__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, fix $j\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ and binary vectors $x_{\\cdot,j}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal{R}}$ and $y_{\\cdot,j}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\mathcal{A}}$. If there exists $\\beta_j\\in\\{0,1\\}$ such that $\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}} x_{i,j}\\le\\beta_j$ and $\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{A}} y_{u,j}\\le 1-\\beta_j$, then adding the two inequalities gives $\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}} x_{i,j}+\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{A}} y_{u,j}\\le 1$. Conversely, if $\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}} x_{i,j}+\\sum_{u\\in\\mathcal{A}} y_{u,j}\\le 1$, then at most one variable among $\\{x_{i,j}\\}_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}}\\cup\\{y_{u,j}\\}_{u\\in\\mathcal{A}}$ equals $1$; setting $\\beta_j = \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{R}} x_{i,j}\\in\\{0,1\\}$ satisfies both channel inequalities.\n\n    Second, consider any feasible solution $(x,y,\\beta,z)$ of the global problem~\\eqref{eq:intraday-global-matching-objective}. By the block-link constraints $x_{i,j}\\le z_{I_k,J_l}$ and $y_{u,j}\\le z_{I_k,J_l}$, nonzero assignments can occur only within block pairs $(I_k,J_l)$ for which $z_{I_k,J_l}=1$ and $\\lvert\\sigma_k-\\tilde{\\sigma}_l\\rvert\\le\\delta_t$. Because the blocks $(I_k)_{k=1}^N$ partition $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ and $(J_l)_{l=1}^M$ partition $\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$, the capacity constraints $\\sum_{j=1}^m x_{i,j}\\tilde{q}_j\\le\\Delta q_i$ and the assignment constraints decouple across distinct linked pairs $(I_k,J_l)$. Maximising over $(x,y,\\beta)$ for a fixed feasible link matrix $z$ therefore yields the objective value $\\sum_{(k,l):z_{I_k,J_l}=1}\\mathsf{Match}_{I_k,J_l}$; a link with $\\mathsf{Match}_{I_k,J_l}=0$ contributes nothing and may be reset to $z_{I_k,J_l}=0$ without violating any constraint, so we may assume that every selected link carries a strictly positive assignment. Finally, the degree constraints $\\sum_{k=1}^N z_{I_k,J_l}\\le 1$ and $\\sum_{l=1}^M z_{I_k,J_l}\\le 1$ together with the non-crossing constraint $z_{I_k,J_l}+z_{I_{k'},J_{l'}}\\le 1$ ($k<k'$, $l>l'$) characterise precisely the set of strictly increasing sequences of block pairs $1\\le k_1<\\cdots<k_r\\le N$ and $1\\le l_1<\\cdots<l_r\\le M$. By standard dynamic-programming induction on $(k,l)$, the maximum of $\\sum_{s=1}^r \\mathsf{Match}_{I_{k_s},J_{l_s}}$ over all such strictly increasing pair sequences on $\\{1,\\dots,k\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots,l\\}$ satisfies the recursion~\\eqref{eq:intraday-outer-dp-main}, and hence $V_{N,M}$ equals the global maximum of~\\eqref{eq:intraday-global-matching-objective}."
      },
