      "order": 286,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:permuted-tuple-measure-map-bijective}\n    Let $X$ be a standard Borel space, let $\\mathscr{T}(X)$ be the associated tuple space, and let $\\mathcal{N}(X)$ be the space of finite integer-valued measures on $X$. Then, the mapping $\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi:\\mathscr{T}^\\pi(X) \\to \\mathcal{N}(X)$ defined by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{m}^\\pi(\\bm{x}^\\pi) = \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})\n    \\end{align*}\n    is bijective.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 571,
        "references": [
          "prop:tuple-measure-surjective"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__permuted-tuple-measure-map-bijective__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, note that $\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi$ is well-defined, as every $\\bm{x}^\\prime \\in \\bm{x}^\\pi$ satisfies $\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}^\\prime) = \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})$. By Proposition~\\ref{prop:tuple-measure-surjective}, the mapping is surjective. It therefore remains to prove injectivity.\n\n    Let $\\bm{x}^\\pi, \\bm{y}^\\pi \\in \\mathscr{T}^\\pi(X)$ satisfy\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{m}^\\pi(\\bm{x}^\\pi)=\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi(\\bm{y}^\\pi).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Choose representatives $\\bm{x}\\in \\bm{x}^\\pi$ and $\\bm{y}\\in \\bm{y}^\\pi$. Then $\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})=\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{y})$. In particular,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})(X) = \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{y})(X) = \\ell(\\bm{y}).\n    \\end{align*}\n    For each $a \\in X$, define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_a = \\{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}) \\mid \\bm{x}(i)=a\\}, \\qquad J_a = \\{j \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{y}) \\mid \\bm{y}(j)=a\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Equality of the induced measures implies $|I_a| = |J_a|$ for every $a \\in X$ (evaluate both measures on $\\{a\\}$, which is measurable in a standard Borel space). Since only finitely many of these sets are non-empty, we may choose bijections\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\sigma_a : I_a \\to J_a\n    \\end{align*}\n    for each such $a \\in X$. The union of these bijections defines a permutation\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\sigma:\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}) \\to \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{y}),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and by construction $\\bm{y}(\\sigma(i))=\\bm{x}(i)$ for every $i$. Hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x} = \\bm{y}\\circ \\sigma,\n    \\end{align*}\n    so $\\bm{x} \\overset{\\pi}{\\sim} \\bm{y}$ and therefore $\\bm{x}^\\pi = \\bm{y}^\\pi$. Thus $\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi$ is injective."
      },
