            "references": [],
            "text": "We must show that for any $(\\bm{x}_0, \\pi_0) \\in \\mathscr{T}(X) \\times \\mathscr{P}$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a $\\delta > 0$ such that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\max(\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0), \\mathscr{d}_H^{\\mathbb{N}}(\\pi, \\pi_0)) < \\delta \\implies \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}[\\pi], \\bm{x}_0[\\pi_0]) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $\\ell(\\bm{x}_0)=0$, a neighbourhood of radius less than $1$ fixes $\\bm{x}=\\bm{e}$, so both outputs equal $\\bm{e}$. We may therefore assume $\\ell(\\bm{x}_0)>0$. By the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} and the uniform discreteness of $(\\mathscr{P},\\mathscr{d}_H^{\\mathbb{N}})$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        &\\max\\bigl(\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0),\n        \\mathscr{d}_H^{\\mathbb{N}}(\\pi, \\pi_0)\\bigr) < 1\n        \\\\\n        &\\implies\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            \\ell(\\bm{x})&=\\ell(\\bm{x}_0),\n            &\\qquad \\pi&=\\pi_0,\\\\\n            \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}_0)\n            &=\\max_{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}_0)}\n            d(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{x}_0(i)).\n        \\end{aligned}\n    \\end{align*}\n    Now suppose the bound above holds. If $\\ell(\\bm{x}_0) \\neq \\ell(\\pi_0)$, the result is trivial, since $\\bm{x}[\\pi] = \\bm{e} = \\bm{x}_0[\\pi_0]$. Therefore, assume that $\\ell(\\bm{x}_0) = \\ell(\\pi_0)$. Under the bound by $1$, this implies that $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\pi) =\\ell(\\bm{x}_0) = \\ell(\\pi_0)$. Hence, whenever $\\delta < \\min(1,\\varepsilon)$, it holds that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}[\\pi], \\bm{x}_0[\\pi_0]) = \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}[\\pi_0], \\bm{x}_0[\\pi_0]) = \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0) < \\delta < \\min(1,\\varepsilon) \\leq \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n    as desired.",
