            "text": "Second, to formalise the dependence of the excess supply on the order prices, let $N_p=n+n^\\prime+2$ and define the combined price-extraction map $\\bm{\\pi}:\\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\\prime}\\to\\mathcal{P}^{N_p}$ by concatenating the lower price limit, the sorted sell prices, the sorted buy prices, and the upper price limit:\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\bm{\\pi}(\\bm{s},\\bm{b})=(-500)\\oplus\\bm{P}_{\\mathrm{price}}\\bigl(\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{sell}}(\\bm{s})\\bigr)\\oplus\\bm{P}_{\\mathrm{price}}\\bigl(\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{buy}}(\\bm{b})\\bigr)\\oplus(\\overline{p}).\n\\end{align*}\nSince $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{sell}}$ and $\\varsigma_{\\mathrm{buy}}$ are continuous on each cell $\\mathscr{S}_{\\pi_s,\\pi_b}$, and coordinate projections and concatenation are continuous by Proposition~\\ref{prop:tuple-projection-continuous} and Theorem~\\ref{thm:analytic-properties-actions}, the map $\\bm{\\pi}$ is continuous on $\\mathscr{S}_{\\pi_s,\\pi_b}$. For every pair of indices $u,v\\in\\{1,\\dots,N_p\\}$, define the elementary comparison sets\n\\begin{align*}\n    E_{u,v}&=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\\prime}\\bigm|\\bm{\\pi}(\\bm{m})(u)=\\bm{\\pi}(\\bm{m})(v)\\bigr\\},\\\\\n    L_{u,v}&=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\\prime}\\bigm|\\bm{\\pi}(\\bm{m})(u)<\\bm{\\pi}(\\bm{m})(v)\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nBecause the diagonal is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the strict order relation is open in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, each $E_{u,v}$ and each $L_{u,v}$ has a Borel intersection with $\\mathscr{S}_{\\pi_s,\\pi_b}$. Any total preorder on the finite index set $\\{1,\\dots,N_p\\}$ is specified by an ordered partition $\\mathcal{C}=(C_1,\\dots,C_r)$ of $\\{1,\\dots,N_p\\}$ into non-empty equivalence classes. For each such ordered partition $\\mathcal{C}$, define the cell\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\mathscr{W}(\\mathcal{C})=\\left(\\bigcap_{k=1}^r\\bigcap_{u,v\\in C_k}E_{u,v}\\right)\\cap\\left(\\bigcap_{1\\leq k<l\\leq r}\\bigcap_{u\\in C_k,\\,v\\in C_l}L_{u,v}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\nAs $\\mathcal{C}$ ranges over the finitely many ordered partitions of $\\{1,\\dots,N_p\\}$, the sets $\\mathscr{S}_{\\pi_s,\\pi_b}\\cap\\mathscr{W}(\\mathcal{C})$ form a finite Borel refinement of $\\mathscr{S}_{\\pi_s,\\pi_b}$. On each non-empty cell of this refinement, the number $r$ of distinct prices is constant, and choosing the minimum index $u_k=\\min C_k$ in each equivalence class yields the strictly increasing distinct price coordinates\n\\begin{align*}\n    x_k(\\bm{m})=\\bm{\\pi}(\\bm{m})(u_k),\\qquad k\\in\\{1,\\dots,r\\},\n\\end{align*}\nwhich satisfy $-500=x_1(\\bm{m})<x_2(\\bm{m})<\\dots<x_r(\\bm{m})=\\overline{p}$ and depend continuously on $\\bm{m}$. Furthermore, on each such cell, the position of $x_k(\\bm{m})$ relative to the sorted sell and buy prices is fixed, so the one-sided limits at $x_k(\\bm{m})$ of the offered and bid volumes are given by fixed expressions, namely $0$, a running maximum of the sorted volumes, or the linear interpolation at $x_k(\\bm{m})$ between two consecutive list entries whose price difference is strictly positive on the cell. Consequently, the maps $\\bm{m}\\mapsto E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k(\\bm{m})-)$ and $\\bm{m}\\mapsto E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k(\\bm{m})+)$ are continuous on the cell for every $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,r\\}$.",
