            "references": [],
            "text": "Third, we refine each cell according to the jump structure of $E^{\\bm{m}}$ and the location of zero quantity. For each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,r\\}$, define the Borel set\n\\begin{align*}\n    J_k=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{L}_{n,n^\\prime}\\bigm| E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k(\\bm{m})-)=E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k(\\bm{m})+)\\bigr\\},\n\\end{align*}\nand for each subset $K\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,r\\}$, set\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\mathscr{J}(K)=\\left(\\bigcap_{k\\in K}J_k\\right)\\cap\\left(\\bigcap_{k\\in\\{1,\\dots,r\\}\\setminus K}J_k^{\\mathrm{c}}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\nIntersecting the previous cells with $\\mathscr{J}(K)$ fixes the number $N=2r-\\lvert K\\rvert$ of distinct vertices of the completed graph of $E^{\\bm{m}}$. Interchanging the price and excess-supply coordinates then yields a continuous vertex path $\\bm{m}\\mapsto\\bigl((q_1(\\bm{m}),p_1(\\bm{m})),\\allowbreak\\dots,\\allowbreak(q_N(\\bm{m}),p_N(\\bm{m}))\\bigr)$ of $P_{\\bm{m}}$, running from $(-b_*,x_1)$ to $(s_*,\\overline{p})$ with $q_1(\\bm{m})\\leq\\dots\\leq q_N(\\bm{m})$. To extract the crossing price and the one-sided limits at zero, define for each $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$ the sign sets\n\\begin{align*}\n    Z_i^-=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\bigm| q_i(\\bm{m})<0\\bigr\\},\\qquad\n    Z_i^0=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\bigm| q_i(\\bm{m})=0\\bigr\\},\\qquad\n    Z_i^+=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\bigm| q_i(\\bm{m})>0\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nFor every sign assignment $\\bm{\\sigma}=(\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_N)\\in\\{-,0,+\\}^N$, the intersection $\\mathscr{Z}(\\bm{\\sigma})=\\bigcap_{i=1}^N Z_i^{\\sigma_i}$ is Borel measurable. On each non-empty cell intersected with $\\mathscr{Z}(\\bm{\\sigma})$, the index sets $I_-=\\{i\\mid\\sigma_i=-\\}$, $I_0=\\{i\\mid\\sigma_i=0\\}$, and $I_+=\\{i\\mid\\sigma_i=+\\}$ are fixed. If $I_0\\neq\\emptyset$ with $i_{\\min}=\\min I_0$ and $i_{\\max}=\\max I_0$, then $P_{\\bm{m}}(0)=\\frac{1}{2}(p_{i_{\\min}}(\\bm{m})+p_{i_{\\max}}(\\bm{m}))$, $P_{\\bm{m}}(0-)=p_{i_{\\min}}(\\bm{m})$, and $P_{\\bm{m}}(0+)=p_{i_{\\max}}(\\bm{m})$. If $I_0=\\emptyset$, then there is a unique index $i_*\\in I_-$ such that $i_*+1\\in I_+$, and $P_{\\bm{m}}(0)=P_{\\bm{m}}(0-)=P_{\\bm{m}}(0+)$ coincides with the linear interpolation of $p_{i_*}(\\bm{m})$ and $p_{i_*+1}(\\bm{m})$ at $0$, whose denominator $q_{i_*+1}(\\bm{m})-q_{i_*}(\\bm{m})$ is strictly positive on the cell. In either case, $P_{\\bm{m}}(0)$, $P_{\\bm{m}}(0-)$, and $P_{\\bm{m}}(0+)$ depend continuously on $\\bm{m}$.",
