            "text": "Fourth, we partition each resulting cell to fix the breakpoints of $P_{\\bm{m}}$ on the positive and negative sides. We write out the positive side; the negative side is partitioned identically after replacing $q$ with $-q$. On each cell of the current refinement, the positive-side vertex path $(q_1^+(\\bm{m}),p_1^+(\\bm{m})),\\dots,(q_{n_+}^+(\\bm{m}),p_{n_+}^+(\\bm{m}))$, starting at $(0,P_{\\bm{m}}(0+))$ and continuing through the vertices with $i\\in I_+$, has a fixed length $n_+$ and depends continuously on $\\bm{m}$. For each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n_+-1\\}$, define the equality sets\n\\begin{align*}\n    Q_k^+=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\bigm| q_k^+(\\bm{m})=q_{k+1}^+(\\bm{m})\\bigr\\},\\qquad\n    P_k^+=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\bigm| p_k^+(\\bm{m})=p_{k+1}^+(\\bm{m})\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nIntersecting the current cell with each combination of $Q_k^+,(Q_k^+)^{\\mathrm{c}}$ and $P_k^+,(P_k^+)^{\\mathrm{c}}$ over $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n_+-1\\}$ yields a finite Borel refinement on which the indices of repeated vertices, vertical segments, and segments of positive width are constant. After omitting repeated vertices, let $(\\tilde{q}_1^+(\\bm{m}),\\tilde{p}_1^+(\\bm{m})),\\allowbreak\\dots,\\allowbreak(\\tilde{q}_{\\tilde{n}_+}^+(\\bm{m}),\\tilde{p}_{\\tilde{n}_+}^+(\\bm{m}))$ denote the reduced positive-side vertex path. For each interior index $k\\in\\{2,\\dots,\\tilde{n}_+-1\\}$ that joins two segments of positive width, define the collinearity set\n\\begin{align*}\n    A_k^+=\\bigl\\{\\bm{m}\\bigm|\\bigl(\\tilde{q}_k^+(\\bm{m})-\\tilde{q}_{k-1}^+(\\bm{m})\\bigr)\\bigl(\\tilde{p}_{k+1}^+(\\bm{m})-\\tilde{p}_k^+(\\bm{m})\\bigr)\\\\\n    =\\bigl(\\tilde{p}_k^+(\\bm{m})-\\tilde{p}_{k-1}^+(\\bm{m})\\bigr)\\bigl(\\tilde{q}_{k+1}^+(\\bm{m})-\\tilde{q}_k^+(\\bm{m})\\bigr)\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{align*}\nBecause $\\tilde{q}_k^+$ and $\\tilde{p}_k^+$ are continuous on the current cell, each $A_k^+$ is closed in the cell and hence Borel measurable. Intersecting with $\\bigl(\\bigcap_{k\\in B}A_k^+\\bigr)\\cap\\bigl(\\bigcap_{k\\notin B}(A_k^+)^{\\mathrm{c}}\\bigr)$ for each subset $B$ of such interior indices fixes the positive-side breakpoints, the number $U$ of pieces of positive width on the positive side, and the endpoints of every piece as fixed continuous vertex coordinates of the path. Performing the analogous finite intersection on the negative side fixes the number $L$ of negative-side pieces of positive width and their continuous endpoints.",
