            "references": [],
            "text": "To prove the second claim, let $\\bm{M}$ be a measurable random state in $\\mathscr{L}$ with $\\mathbb{E}[D(\\bm{M})^2]<\\infty$, and set $M_p=\\max(500,\\overline{p})$. Since every point of $\\Psi(P_{\\bm{M}})(u)$ lies on the completed graph of $P_{\\bm{M}}$, we have $\\lvert\\psi_{P_{\\bm{M}}}(u)\\rvert\\leq D(\\bm{M})$ and $\\lvert P_{\\bm{M}}(\\psi_{P_{\\bm{M}}}(u))\\rvert\\leq M_p$ for all $u\\in[-1,1]$. Integrating over $u\\in[-1,1]$ yields\n\\begin{align*}\n    \\lVert Z(\\bm{M})\\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)}^2\n    =\\int_{-1}^1\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lvert\\psi_{P_{\\bm{M}}}(u)\\bigr\\rvert^2+\\bigl\\lvert P_{\\bm{M}}\\bigl(\\psi_{P_{\\bm{M}}}(u)\\bigr)\\bigr\\rvert^2\\Bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\n    \\leq 2D(\\bm{M})^2+2M_p^2.\n\\end{align*}\nTaking expectations gives $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert Z(\\bm{M})\\rVert_{L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)}^2]\\leq 2\\mathbb{E}[D(\\bm{M})^2]+2M_p^2<\\infty$. Because $L^2([-1,1];\\mathbb{R}^2)$ is separable, Borel measurability of $Z(\\bm{M})$ implies strong measurability by Pettis's measurability theorem, so $Z(\\bm{M})$ is a square-integrable Hilbert-valued random element, as desired.",
