      "section": "Price-Impact Curves",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:price-impact-curve-properties}\n    Let $\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{M}_+$ be a positively matched market state with total sell volume $s_*$, total buy volume $b_*$, available-depth interval $D_{\\bm{m}}=[-b_*,s_*]$, and price-impact curve $P_{\\bm{m}}:D_{\\bm{m}}\\to\\mathcal{P}$. Then $P_{\\bm{m}}$ has the following properties.\n\\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n    \\item $\\operatorname{domain}(P_{\\bm{m}})=D_{\\bm{m}}=[-b_*,s_*]$.\\label{claim:compact-dom}\n    \\item The function $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is piecewise affine over finitely many disjoint intervals, some of which may be singletons.\\label{claim:pw-affine}\n    \\item The function $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is increasing (that is, non-decreasing). \\label{claim:increasing}\n    \\item $0 \\in \\mathrm{domain}(P_{\\bm{m}})$. \\label{claim:zero-is-contained}\n\\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 114,
        "references": [
          "claim:compact-dom",
          "claim:zero-is-contained",
          "claim:increasing",
          "claim:pw-affine"
        ],
        "source": "2 - day-ahead/2_clearing_operator_price_curves.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__price-impact-curve-properties__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for every $\\bm{m}\\in\\mathscr{M}_+$, the level set $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ is a non-empty closed interval for each $q\\in D_{\\bm{m}}$, so that $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is well-defined, and that $P_{\\bm{m}}$ satisfies properties \\eqref{claim:compact-dom}--\\eqref{claim:zero-is-contained}. First, note that because $b_*,s_*\\geq0$, the definition $D_{\\bm{m}}=[-b_*,s_*]$ immediately yields \\eqref{claim:compact-dom} and \\eqref{claim:zero-is-contained} once $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is shown to be well-defined on $D_{\\bm{m}}$.\n\nTo show that $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ is a non-empty closed interval for every $q\\in D_{\\bm{m}}$, let $x_0<x_1<\\dots<x_K$ be the distinct elements of $\\{-500,\\overline{p}\\}$ together with the order prices of $\\bm{m}$, so that $x_0=-500$ and $x_K=\\overline{p}$. No order price lies in $(x_{k-1},x_k)$, so the volumes offered and bid, and hence $E^{\\bm{m}}$, are affine there. The completed graph of $E^{\\bm{m}}$ is therefore the union of the segments from $(x_{k-1},E^{\\bm{m}}(x_{k-1}+))$ to $(x_k,E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k-))$, for $k=1,\\dots,K$, and the vertical segments $\\{x_k\\}\\times[E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k-),E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k+)]$, for $k=0,\\dots,K$, where $E^{\\bm{m}}(x_0-)=-b_*$ and $E^{\\bm{m}}(x_K+)=s_*$. Taken in their order along the graph, consecutive segments share an endpoint, and their ranges of levels are closed intervals that cover $[-b_*,s_*]$ and overlap only at their endpoints. Consequently, $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ is non-empty for every $q\\in D_{\\bm{m}}$, and it is closed because the completed graph is a finite union of closed segments.\n\nNext, since $E^{\\bm{m}}$ is non-decreasing, $p<p^\\prime$ implies $E^{\\bm{m}}(p+)\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p^\\prime-)$. If $p<p^{\\prime\\prime}<p^\\prime$ with $p,p^\\prime\\in J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$, then\n\\begin{align*}\n    E^{\\bm{m}}(p^{\\prime\\prime}-)\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p^\\prime-)\\leq q\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p+)\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p^{\\prime\\prime}+),\n\\end{align*}\nso $p^{\\prime\\prime}\\in J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$. Thus $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ is a non-empty closed interval, and $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is well-defined on $D_{\\bm{m}}$.\n\nTo establish \\eqref{claim:increasing}, let $q,q^\\prime\\in D_{\\bm{m}}$ satisfy $q<q^\\prime$, and let $p\\in J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ and $p^\\prime\\in J_{\\bm{m}}(q^\\prime)$. Then $p\\leq p^\\prime$, because $p^\\prime<p$ would yield $q^\\prime\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p^\\prime+)\\leq E^{\\bm{m}}(p-)\\leq q$, a contradiction. Taking the maximum over $p\\in J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ and the minimum over $p^\\prime\\in J_{\\bm{m}}(q^\\prime)$ gives\n\\begin{align*}\n    P_{\\bm{m}}(q)\\leq\\max J_{\\bm{m}}(q)\\leq\\min J_{\\bm{m}}(q^\\prime)\\leq P_{\\bm{m}}(q^\\prime),\n\\end{align*}\nwhich proves that $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is increasing.\n\nFinally, to establish \\eqref{claim:pw-affine}, we remove from $D_{\\bm{m}}$ the finitely many levels $E^{\\bm{m}}(x_k\\pm)$. Every remaining level $q$ lies in the interior of the range of levels of exactly one segment, and no other segment reaches $q$, since the endpoints of these ranges have been removed. If the segment is the $k$th non-vertical one, then $E^{\\bm{m}}$ is affine with positive slope on $(x_{k-1},x_k)$, the set $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)$ consists of the single price at which $E^{\\bm{m}}$ takes the value $q$, and $P_{\\bm{m}}$ agrees with the affine inverse of $E^{\\bm{m}}$ on the interior of the range. If it is the vertical segment at $x_k$, then $J_{\\bm{m}}(q)=\\{x_k\\}$, and $P_{\\bm{m}}$ equals $x_k$ on the interior of the range. Hence $D_{\\bm{m}}$ is the disjoint union of finitely many open intervals, on each of which $P_{\\bm{m}}$ is affine, and finitely many singletons, which establishes \\eqref{claim:pw-affine}, as desired."
      },
