            "text": "We must show that $\\sim$ is an equivalence relation, that $d_\\sim$ is independent of the chosen representatives, and that $(X_\\sim,d_\\sim)$ satisfies the metric axioms. First, reflexivity and symmetry of $\\sim$ follow immediately from the pseudometric axioms~(\\ref{eq:pseudometric-1}) and~(\\ref{eq:pseudometric-2}), while $x\\sim y$ and $y\\sim z$ imply $0\\leq d(x,z)\\leq d(x,y)+d(y,z)=0$, which proves transitivity. Next, if $x\\sim y$ and $x^\\ast\\sim y^\\ast$, then the triangle inequality gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x,x^\\ast)\\leq d(x,y)+d(y,y^\\ast)+d(y^\\ast,x^\\ast)=d(y,y^\\ast),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and exchanging the two representative pairs gives the reverse inequality $d(y,y^\\ast)\\leq d(x,x^\\ast)$. Hence $d(x,x^\\ast)=d(y,y^\\ast)$, so $d_\\sim$ is well-defined. Finally, symmetry and the triangle inequality descend directly from $d$, and $d_\\sim([x],[x^\\ast])=0$ implies $x\\sim x^\\ast$ and hence $[x]=[x^\\ast]$ by definition of the quotient, as desired.",
