      "section": "Measurable Spaces and Measures",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:push-forward}\\index{push-forward measure}\\index{law}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $(S,\\mathcal{S})$ be a measurable space, and let $f:\\Omega \\to S$ be $\\mathcal{F}/\\mathcal{S}$-measurable. Then the map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mu \\circ f^{-1}: \\mathcal{S} \\to [0,\\infty]\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a measure on $(S,\\mathcal{S})$. It is called the push-forward measure induced by $f$. If $\\mu$ is a probability measure, then $\\mu \\circ f^{-1}$ is called the law or distribution of $f$.",
