            "text": "Let $\\bm{m} \\in \\mathscr{S}(O, \\preceq)$ be given, and for each $\\kappa\\in\\{\\mathrm{buy},\\mathrm{sell}\\}$, define the side index set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_\\kappa(\\bm{m})=\\{i\\in\\operatorname{domain}(\\bm{m}) \\mid \\bm{m}(i) \\in \\left([-9999,9999]\\times (0,\\infty) \\times [0,1]\\right) \\times \\{\\kappa\\}\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and let $n=\\ell(\\bm{\\Pi}_{\\mathrm{buy}}\\bm{m})$. If a sell order occurred at an index $i$ before a buy order at an index $j$, then $i<j$ and $\\bm{m}(j)\\prec\\bm{m}(i)$ by \\eqref{eq:price-time-priority}, contradicting Definition~\\ref{defn:sorted-tuples}. Hence,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_{\\mathrm{buy}}(\\bm{m})=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\n        \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n        I_{\\mathrm{sell}}(\\bm{m})=\\{n+1,\\ldots,\\ell(\\bm{m})\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Fix $\\kappa\\in\\{\\mathrm{buy},\\mathrm{sell}\\}$, and let $\\prec^\\kappa$ denote the strict part of $\\preceq^\\kappa$. Let $r,r^\\prime\\in\\operatorname{domain}(\\bm{\\Pi}_\\kappa\\bm{m})$ satisfy $r<r^\\prime$. Since $\\bm{\\Pi}_\\kappa\\bm{m}=\\bm{P}_{\\mathrm{element}}(\\bm{m}[I_\\kappa(\\bm{m})])$ by Definition~\\ref{defn:separation-operators}, reindexing gives indices $i<j$ in $I_\\kappa(\\bm{m})$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\bm{\\Pi}_\\kappa\\bm{m})(r)=o_i,\\qquad\n        (\\bm{\\Pi}_\\kappa\\bm{m})(r^\\prime)=o_j,\n        \\qquad\n        \\bm{m}(i)=(o_i,\\kappa),\\qquad\n        \\bm{m}(j)=(o_j,\\kappa).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\bm{m}$ is sorted, $\\neg(\\bm{m}(j)\\prec\\bm{m}(i))$. By \\eqref{eq:price-time-priority}, this gives $\\neg(o_j\\prec^\\kappa o_i)$. Consequently, no later entry of $\\bm{\\Pi}_\\kappa\\bm{m}$ strictly precedes an earlier entry, which establishes that $\\bm{\\Pi}_\\kappa\\bm{m} \\in \\mathscr{S}([-9999,9999]\\times (0,\\infty) \\times [0,1], \\preceq^\\kappa)$ by Definition~\\ref{defn:sorted-tuples}, as claimed.",
