      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 298,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:sim-equivalence}\nLet $(X,\\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set and let $\\bm{x}\\in\\mathscr{T}(X)$. Then $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim}$ is an equivalence relation on $\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1423,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__sim-equivalence__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, we show that $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim}$ is reflexive. For any $i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})$, we have $(i,i) \\in \\Delta_{\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})}$, and since $\\Delta_{\\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})} = \\{(i,i) \\mid i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})\\}$ is a subset of the reflexive transitive closure, it follows that $i \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} i$.\n\n    Next, we show that $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim}$ is symmetric. Suppose $i \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} j$. Then, there exists a sequence of indices $i = i_1, i_2, \\dots, i_n = j$ such that $i_k \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} i_{k+1}$ for all $k < n$. Since $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow}$ is symmetric, we have $i_{k+1} \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} i_k$ for all $k < n$. Therefore, the sequence $j = i_n, i_{n-1}, \\dots, i_1 = i$ shows that $j \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} i$.\n\n    Finally, we show that $\\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim}$ is transitive. Suppose $i \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} j$ and $j \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} k$. Then, there exist sequences of indices $i = i_1, i_2, \\dots, i_m = j$ and $j = j_1, j_2, \\dots, j_p = k$ such that $i_r \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} i_{r+1}$ for all $r < m$ and $j_l \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\leftrightsquigarrow} j_{l+1}$ for all $l < p$. Concatenating these sequences gives us a sequence of indices from $i$ to $k$, showing that $i \\overset{\\bm{x}}{\\sim} k$."
      },
