      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Metric Spaces",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:topology-extended-pseudometric}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a pseudometric space. For each $x \\in X$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$, define the open ball\n    \\begin{align*}\n        B_\\varepsilon(x) = \\{x^\\prime \\in X \\mid d(x,x^\\prime) < \\varepsilon\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then the family\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathcal{B}_d = \\{B_\\varepsilon(x) \\mid x \\in X,\\ \\varepsilon > 0\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a topological base. We call $\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathcal{B}_d}$ the topology induced by $d$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 183,
        "references": [],
        "source": "A - appendices/analysis.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__topology-extended-pseudometric__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that $\\mathcal{B}_d$ satisfies the two defining conditions (i) and (ii) of a topological base. For each $x \\in X$ and any $\\varepsilon > 0$, we have $d(x,x)=0<\\varepsilon$ and hence $x \\in B_\\varepsilon(x)$, so the first base condition holds.\n\n    For the second condition, let $x \\in B_{\\varepsilon^\\prime}(x^\\prime) \\cap B_{\\varepsilon^\\ast}(x^\\ast)$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x^\\prime,x) < \\varepsilon^\\prime\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        d(x^\\ast,x) < \\varepsilon^\\ast.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        r = \\frac{1}{2}\\min\\{\\varepsilon^\\prime - d(x^\\prime,x),\\ \\varepsilon^\\ast - d(x^\\ast,x)\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is strictly positive. If $y \\in B_r(x)$, then the triangle inequality gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x^\\prime,y) \\leq d(x^\\prime,x) + d(x,y) < d(x^\\prime,x) + r \\leq \\varepsilon^\\prime,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and similarly $d(x^\\ast,y) < \\varepsilon^\\ast$. Therefore\n    \\begin{align*}\n        B_r(x) \\subseteq B_{\\varepsilon^\\prime}(x^\\prime) \\cap B_{\\varepsilon^\\ast}(x^\\ast),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which proves the second base condition."
      },
