        "line": 945,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__totally-ordered-tuple-sorted-iff-monotone__proof.svg",
        "text": "Suppose first that $\\bm{x}$ is sorted. Then, by definition,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        i \\leq j \\implies \\neg(\\bm{x}(j) \\prec \\bm{x}(i)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since the order is total, for any $a,b \\in X$ we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\neg(b \\prec a) \\iff a \\preceq b.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        i \\leq j \\implies \\bm{x}(i) \\preceq \\bm{x}(j).\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    Conversely, if\n    \\begin{align*}\n        i \\leq j \\implies \\bm{x}(i) \\preceq \\bm{x}(j),\n    \\end{align*}\n    then certainly\n    \\begin{align*}\n        i \\leq j \\implies \\neg(\\bm{x}(j) \\prec \\bm{x}(i)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    so $\\bm{x}$ is sorted."
