      "order": 306,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:transitive-closure-finite}\n    Let $X$ be a non-empty finite set and let $R \\subseteq X \\times X$ be a relation on $X$. Then, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\overline{R} = \\Delta_X \\cup \\bigcup_{n=1}^{|X| - 1} R^n.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1401,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__transitive-closure-finite__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that $\\Delta_X \\cup \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} R^n = \\Delta_X \\cup \\bigcup_{n=1}^{|X|-1} R^n$, where the union on the right-hand side is understood to be empty when $|X| = 1$. Since the right-hand side is manifestly a subset of the left-hand side, it suffices to show that for every $(x, z) \\in \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} R^n$, either $x = z$ or there exists an integer $m \\in \\{1, \\dots, |X|-1\\}$ such that $(x, z) \\in R^m$.\n\n    Let $(x, z) \\in \\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} R^n$ be given, and let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ be the least positive integer such that $(x, z) \\in R^m$. If $x = z$, then $(x, z) \\in \\Delta_X$, and the claim holds. Suppose therefore that $x \\neq z$. By the chain characterisation of $R^m$, there exists a sequence of elements $x = x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_{m+1} = z$ in $X$ such that $(x_r, x_{r+1}) \\in R$ for all $r \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$.\n\n    Suppose for contradiction that $m \\ge |X|$. Then the sequence $x_1, \\dots, x_{m+1}$ contains $m + 1 > |X|$ terms in $X$. By the pigeonhole principle, there exist indices $i, j \\in \\{1, \\dots, m+1\\}$ with $i < j$ such that $x_i = x_j$. Since $x_1 = x \\neq z = x_{m+1}$, we cannot have both $i = 1$ and $j = m+1$. Consequently, omitting the intermediate terms $x_{i+1}, \\dots, x_j$ yields a shorter chain\n    \\begin{align*}\n        x = x_1, \\dots, x_i, x_{j+1}, \\dots, x_{m+1} = z\n    \\end{align*}\n    of length $m^\\prime = m - (j - i) \\in \\{1, \\dots, m-1\\}$ in which every consecutive pair belongs to $R$. Hence $(x, z) \\in R^{m^\\prime}$, contradicting the minimality of $m$. Therefore $m \\le |X| - 1$, as desired."
      },
