      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 308,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:tuple-induced-measure-continuous}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the associated tuple Hausdorff space, and $(\\mathcal{N}(X), \\rho)$ be the space of finite integer-valued measures endowed with the Prokhorov distance. Then the mapping $\\mathfrak{m} :\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathcal{N}(X)$ defined by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}) = B \\mapsto \\sum_{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})} \\delta_{\\bm{x}(i)}(B),\n    \\end{align*}\n    is continuous.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 484,
        "references": [
          "thm:tuple-space-properties"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__tuple-induced-measure-continuous__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ and $\\bm{x}_0$ there exists a $\\theta > 0 $ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0) < \\theta \\implies \\rho(\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}), \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}_0)) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\nChoose $0<\\theta < \\min\\{\\varepsilon,1\\}$. First, recall that by Theorem~\\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties} we have that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0) < \\theta < 1\n    \\end{align*}\n    implies that $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}_0)$. If $\\bm{x}_0$ is the empty tuple, then $\\bm{x}$ is also the empty tuple, and therefore\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\rho(\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}),\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}_0)) = 0 < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Assume therefore that $\\bm{x}_0 \\neq \\bm{e}$. Then, we additionally have \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}_0) = \\max_{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}_0)} d(\\bm{x}(i), \\bm{x}_0(i)) < \\theta < 1.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Consequently, for any Borel set $B$ and every index $i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}_0)$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}_0(i)\\in B \\implies \\bm{x}(i)\\in B^\\theta.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}_0)(B) \\leq \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})(B^\\theta).\n    \\end{align*}\n    By symmetry,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})(B) \\leq \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}_0)(B^\\theta).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence, by the definition of the Prokhorov metric,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\rho(\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}), \\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x}_0)) \\leq \\theta < \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n    as desired."
      },
