      "order": 309,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:tuple-measure-surjective}\n    Let $X$ be a standard Borel space, let $\\mathscr{T}(X)$ be the associated tuple space, and let $\\mathcal{N}(X)$ be the space of finite integer-valued measures on $X$. Then, the mapping $\\mathfrak{m}:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathcal{N}(X)$ is surjective.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 523,
        "references": [
          "thm:sufficient-decomposition"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__tuple-measure-surjective__proof.svg",
        "text": "Let $\\xi\\in\\mathcal{N}(X)$. If $\\xi=0$, then the empty tuple $\\bm{e}$ satisfies $\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{e})=\\xi$.\n    Hence assume $\\xi\\neq 0$.\n\n    By Theorem~\\ref{thm:sufficient-decomposition} any measure $\\xi \\in \\mathcal{N}(X)$ admits the decomposition \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi =\\sum_{k \\leq \\kappa } \\beta_k\\delta_{x_k},\n    \\end{align*}\n    with $(\\beta_k)_{k \\leq \\kappa}\\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ and $(x_k)_{k\\leq \\kappa} \\subseteq X$. Note that the representation is unique up to a permutation of $\\{k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid k \\leq \\kappa\\}$; fix an arbitrary enumeration and define the tuple \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}(i) = \\begin{cases}\n            x_1 & i \\in \\{1,\\dots, \\beta_1\\} \\\\\n            x_2 & i \\in \\{ \\beta_1 + 1, \\dots, \\beta_1 +\\beta_2\\}\\\\ \n            ~\\vdots &~~~\\vdots \\\\\n            x_\\kappa & i \\in \\{\\sum_{k = 1}^{\\kappa-1} \\beta_k+1, \\dots, \\sum_{k \\leq \\kappa}\\beta_k\\},\n        \\end{cases}\n    \\end{align*}\n    which by construction satisfies, for every measurable $B$, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi(B) =\\sum_{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x})} \\delta_{\\bm{x}(i)}(B) =\\mathfrak{m}(\\bm{x})(B),\n    \\end{align*}\n    as desired."
      },
