      "order": 310,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Primitive Actions",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:tuple-monoid}\n    Let $(X, \\preceq)$ be a totally ordered set. Then the restriction of $+$ from Definition~\\ref{defn:sorted-tuple-add} to $\\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq)\\times \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq)$ makes $(\\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq), +)$ into a commutative monoid with the cancellation\\index{cancellation} property.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 44,
        "references": [
          "property:identity",
          "property:associative",
          "prop:totally-ordered-tuple-sorted-iff-monotone",
          "property:commutative",
          "property:right-cancel",
          "property:left-cancel"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/3_market_actions.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__tuple-monoid__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that, for all $\\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime, \\bm{x}^\\ast \\in \\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq)$, the operation satisfies\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $(\\bm{x} + \\bm{x}^\\prime) + \\bm{x}^\\ast = \\bm{x} + (\\bm{x}^\\prime + \\bm{x}^\\ast)$ (associativity). \\label{property:associative}\n        \\item $\\bm{x} + \\bm{e} = \\bm{x}$ (identity element).  \\label{property:identity}\n        \\item $\\bm{x} + \\bm{x}^\\prime = \\bm{x}^\\prime + \\bm{x}$ (commutativity). \\label{property:commutative}\n        \\item $\\bm{x} + \\bm{x}^\\prime = \\bm{x}^\\ast + \\bm{x}^\\prime \\text{ implies } \\bm{x} = \\bm{x}^\\ast$ (right cancellation).  \\label{property:right-cancel}\n        \\item $\\bm{x}^\\prime + \\bm{x} = \\bm{x}^\\prime + \\bm{x}^\\ast \\text{ implies } \\bm{x} = \\bm{x}^\\ast$ (left cancellation).  \\label{property:left-cancel}\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    The identity property \\eqref{property:identity} is immediate. Since $\\bm{x}\\oplus \\bm{e} = \\bm{x}$ and $\\bm{x}$ is already sorted, idempotency of the sorting map on $\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ yields $\\bm{x} + \\bm{e} = \\bm{x}$.\n\n    For each $a \\in X$ and each tuple $\\bm{z}\\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ define the multiplicity\n    \\begin{align*}\n        N_a(\\bm{z}) = |\\{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{z}) \\mid \\bm{z}(i) = a\\}|.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Sorting does not change multiplicities, and concatenation adds them, i.e., for every $a \\in X$ and $\\bm{x},\\bm{y} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        N_a(\\bm{x}+\\bm{y}) = N_a(\\bm{x}) + N_a(\\bm{y}).\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    For \\eqref{property:associative}, notice that both $(\\bm{x} + \\bm{x}^\\prime) + \\bm{x}^\\ast=\\varsigma(\\varsigma(\\bm{x} \\oplus \\bm{x}^\\prime) \\oplus \\bm{x}^\\ast)$ and $\\bm{x} + (\\bm{x}^\\prime + \\bm{x}^\\ast)=\\varsigma(\\bm{x} \\oplus \\varsigma(\\bm{x}^\\prime \\oplus \\bm{x}^\\ast))$ are sorted tuples containing every $a\\in X$ exactly $N_a(\\bm{x})+N_a(\\bm{x}^\\prime)+N_a(\\bm{x}^\\ast)$ times. Under a total order, a sorted tuple is non-decreasing (Proposition~\\ref{prop:totally-ordered-tuple-sorted-iff-monotone}), so its $i^{\\mathrm{th}}$ entry is the smallest of its entries $a$ with $\\sum_{b\\preceq a}N_b\\geq i$; hence two sorted tuples with equal multiplicities coincide, and both expressions are the same tuple. The same argument proves \\eqref{property:commutative}.\n\n    From \\eqref{property:commutative} it suffices to show \\eqref{property:right-cancel}, as \\eqref{property:left-cancel} then follows immediately. If $\\bm{x} + \\bm{x}^\\prime = \\bm{x}^\\ast + \\bm{x}^\\prime$, then for every $a \\in X$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        N_a(\\bm{x}) + N_a(\\bm{x}^\\prime)\n        = N_a(\\bm{x}+\\bm{x}^\\prime)\n        = N_a(\\bm{x}^\\ast+\\bm{x}^\\prime)\n        = N_a(\\bm{x}^\\ast) + N_a(\\bm{x}^\\prime),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and hence $N_a(\\bm{x}) = N_a(\\bm{x}^\\ast)$. Since both $\\bm{x}$ and $\\bm{x}^\\ast$ are sorted in the same total order, equality of all multiplicities implies $\\bm{x} = \\bm{x}^\\ast$, as shown in the proof of \\eqref{property:associative}. This proves right cancellation."
      },
