      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuple Selections and Projections",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:tuple-projection-continuous}\n     Let $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha\\in A}$ be a finite non-empty family of metric spaces, $X = \\prod_{\\alpha\\in A} X_\\alpha$ and let $d: X \\times X \\to [0,\\infty]$ be the maximum product metric on $X$. Let $(\\mathscr{T}(X_\\alpha), \\mathscr{d}_{H,\\alpha})_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be the associated tuple Hausdorff spaces, and let $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple Hausdorff space associated with $(X,d)$. Then\n     \\begin{enumerate}\n         \\item the projection $\\bm{P}_\\alpha :\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(X_\\alpha)$ is continuous for every $\\alpha \\in A$.\n         \\item the tuple decomposition $\\bm{P}:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\prod_{\\alpha \\in A}\\mathscr{T}(X_\\alpha)$ is continuous whenever $\\prod_{\\alpha \\in A}\\mathscr{T}(X_\\alpha)$ is endowed with the product topology.\n         \\item the mapping $\\bm{P}$ is injective and hence invertible on its image.\n     \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 135,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/4_market_clearing.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__tuple-projection-continuous__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, we must show that for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ and $\\bm{x}_0 \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ there exists a $\\delta >0$ such that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}_0) < \\delta \\implies \\mathscr{d}_{H,\\alpha}(\\bm{P}_\\alpha\\bm{x}, \\bm{P}_\\alpha\\bm{x}_0) < \\varepsilon,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and since the graph map $(i,x)\\mapsto(i,P_\\alpha x)$ is $1$-Lipschitz for the maximum product metric, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_{H,\\alpha}(\\bm{P}_\\alpha\\bm{x}, \\bm{P}_\\alpha\\bm{x}_0) \\leq \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}_0),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and so for a given $\\varepsilon > 0$ one can choose $\\delta = \\varepsilon$. Note that the second assertion follows trivially from the first, as one uses the maximum metric to generate the product topology.\n\n    It remains to show that $\\bm{P}$ is injective. Let $\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}^\\prime \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ and assume that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{P}\\bm{x} = \\bm{P}\\bm{x}^\\prime.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $\\bm{P}_\\alpha \\bm{x} = \\bm{P}_\\alpha \\bm{x}^\\prime$ for every $\\alpha \\in A$. Since $A$ is non-empty, fix $\\alpha_0 \\in A$. The equality $\\bm{P}_{\\alpha_0}\\bm{x} = \\bm{P}_{\\alpha_0}\\bm{x}^\\prime$ implies that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}) = \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{P}_{\\alpha_0}\\bm{x}) = \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{P}_{\\alpha_0}\\bm{x}^\\prime) = \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Now let $i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}) = \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}^\\prime)$. Since $\\bm{P}_\\alpha \\bm{x}(i) = \\bm{P}_\\alpha \\bm{x}^\\prime(i)$ for every $\\alpha \\in A$, we have \n    \\begin{align*}\n        P_\\alpha(\\bm{x}(i)) = P_\\alpha(\\bm{x}^\\prime(i)) \\quad \\forall \\alpha \\in A.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence $\\bm{x}(i)=\\bm{x}^\\prime(i)$ in the Cartesian product $X=\\prod_{\\alpha \\in A} X_\\alpha$. As this holds for every index $i$, it follows that $\\bm{x}=\\bm{x}^\\prime$."
      },
