            "text": "First, let $(x_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ be a Cauchy sequence. Suppose that $\\beta>0$ is a separation bound, so that $d(x,x^\\prime) < \\beta \\iff x = x^\\prime$. In particular, note that if one takes $\\varepsilon < \\beta$, then by the Cauchy property, there exists an $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\forall n ,m \\geq N: d(x_n,x_m) < \\varepsilon < \\beta,\n    \\end{align*}\n    but since $d(x_n,x_m) < \\beta$ implies that $x_n = x_m$, it follows that $x_n \\equiv x_N$ for all $n \\geq N$, which constitutes the limit. Hence, any Cauchy sequence is eventually constant, and so $(X,d)$ is complete.",
