      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 313,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Fields and Vector Spaces",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:vector-space-extension}\\index{vector space!extension}\n    Let $(V, +, \\cdot)$ be a vector space over a field $\\mathbb{K}$. Let $X$ be a set, and define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        F(X; V) = \\{f \\mid f:X \\to V\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Define $+:F(X; V) \\times F(X; V) \\to F(X; V)$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (f + g)(x) = f(x) + g(x),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and define $\\cdot:\\mathbb{K}\\times F(X; V) \\to F(X; V)$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (a\\cdot f)(x) = a\\cdot f(x).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $(F(X; V), +, \\cdot)$ is a vector space over $\\mathbb{K}$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 138,
        "references": [
          "defn:real-vector-space",
          "prop:empty-domain-function"
        ],
        "source": "A - appendices/algebra.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/prop__vector-space-extension__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that $(F(X;V),+,\\cdot)$ is an abelian group under pointwise addition and satisfies the four vector-space axioms (i)--(iv) of Definition~\\ref{defn:real-vector-space}. All axioms are inherited pointwise from $V$. Specifically, pointwise addition is associative and commutative because addition in $V$ is associative and commutative, the zero function $0(x)=0_V$ is the additive identity, the pointwise inverse is given by $(-f)(x)=-f(x)$, and the scalar-multiplication axioms (i)--(iv) follow by evaluating the corresponding identities in $V$ at each $x \\in X$.\n    In the degenerate case where $X=\\emptyset$, Proposition~\\ref{prop:empty-domain-function} gives the unique empty-domain function, so $F(X;V)=\\{\\emptyset\\}$ and the pointwise operations make it the zero vector space, as desired."
      },
