      "section": "Weak Stationarity and Score Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{prop:weak-stationarity}\n    Let $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, $(\\mathcal{H}, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot\\rangle)$ be a real separable Hilbert space with an orthonormal basis $(e_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$, and $X = (X_t)_{t \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of $\\mathcal{H}$-valued random elements with $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X_t\\rVert^2] < \\infty$ for all $t \\in \\mathbb{N}$. Then $X$ is weakly stationary if and only if the score sequence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\theta_t^\\infty)_{t \\in \\mathbb{N}} = ((\\theta_{t,n})_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}})_{t \\in \\mathbb{N}} = ((\\langle X_t, e_n\\rangle)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}})_{t \\in \\mathbb{N}}\n    \\end{align*}\n    has coordinate means and cross-covariances depending only on the lag. That is, for all $n,m \\in \\mathbb{N}$, the score process $(\\theta_{t,n})_{t \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ has mean $\\mu_n$ independent of $t$, and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[(\\theta_{t+h,n} - \\mu_n)(\\theta_{t,m} - \\mu_m)] = \\sigma_{n,m}(h)\n    \\end{align*}\n    depends only on $h \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$.",
