      "order": 317,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Relations and Functions",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:bijection-not-homeomorphism}\n    A bijection gives only set-theoretic equivalence. To preserve a topology, metric, order, measure, or algebraic operation, we prove the relevant preservation property or explicitly transport the structure along the bijection. Likewise, we cannot infer that a family is pairwise disjoint merely from the existence of some bijection between its ordinary union and the tagged disjoint union (Definition~\\ref{defn:disjoint-union}). Pairwise disjointness does, however, make the tag-forgetting map $(x,\\alpha)\\mapsto x$ bijective; we use this canonical map in Chapter~\\ref{chap:tuple-market-states}.",
