      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Metric Spaces",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:extended-metric-convention}\\index{extended metric}\n    Following \\cite{burago2001course}, we allow a metric $d$ throughout to satisfy $d(x,y)=\\infty$ for some pairs $(x,y)$. When we prefer to work with an ordinary bounded metric, we may replace $d$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bar{d}(x,y) = \\min\\{d(x,y),1\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The effect of this truncation is simple:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item Only the numerical values of large point-to-point distances change. In particular, distances that were infinite become equal to $1$, and supremum distances become automatically bounded. The corresponding Hausdorff distance\\index{Hausdorff distance}s (Definition~\\ref{defn:Hausdorff-distance}; see also below) between non-empty sets become bounded, while the convention $d_H(A,\\emptyset)=d_H(\\emptyset,A)=\\infty$ for $A\\neq\\emptyset$ remains unchanged.\n        \\item For every $\\varepsilon \\in (0,1)$, the $d$-ball and the $\\bar{d}$-ball of radius $\\varepsilon$ coincide, so $d$ and $\\bar{d}$ induce the same topology and therefore the same Borel $\\sigma$-algebra.\n        \\item Convergence, continuity, Cauchy sequence\\index{Cauchy sequence}s, completeness, separability, and hence every notion built from these, such as Polishness, do not change either.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    For example, a $d$-Cauchy sequence is eventually contained in a ball of radius less than $1$, where $d$ and $\\bar{d}$ agree on all tail comparisons; the same observation transfers limits and completeness in both directions. Thus, whenever a statement is most naturally phrased for ordinary bounded metrics\\index{metrics}, one may pass to $\\bar{d}$ without changing any of the analytic or topological content relevant here.\n\n    The finite-distance component of $x\\in X$ is the set $\\{y\\in X\\mid d(x,y)<\\infty\\}$. By the triangle inequality, these components partition $X$, and $d$ is finite on each of them. Each component is open, since it contains every point at distance less than $1$ from one of its points, and closed, since its complement is a union of components. From some index on, the terms of a Cauchy sequence have pairwise distances below $1$, so its tail lies in a single component, and a completeness proof reduces to the ordinary argument within that component. The same reduction applies to any partition of $X$ into pieces at mutual distance at least $1$, and the completeness proofs for disjoint unions (Proposition~\\ref{prop:disjoint-union-metric-properties}), labelled finite sets (Theorem~\\ref{thm:labelled-properties}), tuples (Theorem~\\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}), and permuted tuples (Theorem~\\ref{thm:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-metric-complete-separable}) follow this pattern.",
