      "section": "Monoids, Groups, and Completion",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:group-completion-scope}\n    In Chapter~\\ref{chap:hilbert-market-representations}, we keep several constructions distinct. The kernel quotient in Definition~\\ref{defn:function-kernel-quotient-space} handles non-injectivity by identifying objects with the same encoded value. Before applying the construction of Definition~\\ref{defn:grothendieck-group}, we verify that the relevant transported object is closed under its proposed addition and is a cancellative abelian monoid. Failure of closure under that addition is not repaired by quotienting. If cancellation fails, the relation of Proposition~\\ref{prop:grothendieck-relation} must be replaced by the general Grothendieck congruence, $(x,y)\\approx(x^\\prime,y^\\prime)$ if and only if $x+y^\\prime+k=x^\\prime+y+k$ for some $k\\in X$, or by an explicitly defined quotient that enforces cancellation, and the resulting canonical map need not be injective. Theorem~\\ref{thm:grothendieck-vector-space-from-convex-cone} introduces formal differences, and Corollary~\\ref{thm:grothendieck-vectorization-is-span} identifies the resulting vector space with $\\mathrm{span}~\\varphi(X)$ rather than with the whole ambient space. Definition~\\ref{defn:completed-kernel-space} and Corollary~\\ref{cor:grothendieck-vectorization-inner-product-space} use metric completion to add limits of Cauchy sequences; this is distinct from group completion. None of these constructions makes a formal inverse a feasible market cancellation, trade, execution, or state transition.",
