      "order": 323,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Primitive Actions",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:identifier-coordinates}\n    Identifiers do not change when entries are removed. Unlike its index-based counterpart, identifier-based cancellation therefore composes, in the sense that for all $J,J^\\prime\\in\\mathscr{F}(I)$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\bm{x}\\ominus_I J)\\ominus_I J^\\prime=\\bm{x}\\ominus_I(J\\cup J^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    In the metric setting of Example~\\ref{exmp:identifier-coordinates}, the identifier carries no metric information beyond equality. Under the pseudometric $\\bar{d}((y,i),(y^\\prime,j))=d_Y(y,y^\\prime)$, elements that differ only in their identifiers lie at distance $0$, and identifying such elements recovers $(Y,d_Y)$ through $\\varpi$; the maps that disregard identifiers are precisely those that factor through $\\varpi$. The metric $d$ instead separates distinct identifiers by $1$, which makes $\\mathrm{id}$ continuous. Had we indexed a disjoint union $\\bigsqcup_{i\\in I}Y$ (Definition~\\ref{defn:disjoint-union}) by the identifiers instead, elements with distinct identifiers would lie at distance $\\infty$ (Definition~\\ref{defn:disjoint-union-metrisation}) and be incomparable under the induced partial order (Proposition~\\ref{prop:disjoint-partial-order}), so that no sorting could rank them against each other. In the product, identifiers can instead break ties (Proposition~\\ref{prop:identifier-tie-breaking}).",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
