      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 325,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "$L^p$Lp Spaces",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:lp-point-evaluation}\n    We obtain an operation on $L^p$ from representatives only when the operation is invariant under almost-everywhere equality. For Lebesgue measure, or any diffuse measure, on an interval, point evaluation fails this criterion, since two representatives of the same class may differ at a prescribed singleton. We therefore cannot assign a generic $L^p$ class a well-defined point value, canonical graph, or graph inverse. To use such operations, we fix (a) a selected regular representative; (b) a continuous subclass on which the natural map into $L^p$ is injective; (c) a c\\`adl\\`ag (respectively c\\`agl\\`ad) representative together with its terminal (respectively initial) value, which is lost by passage to $L^p$; or (d) a finite-dimensional parametrisation with a well-defined inverse.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
