      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 328,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:point-process-measurability-equivalence}\n    If $(X,d)$ is complete and separable, the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra on $(\\mathcal{N}(X),\\rho)$ is the evaluation $\\sigma$-algebra\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\sigma\\{\\mu\\mapsto\\mu(B)\\mid B\\in\\mathcal{B}(X)\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Consequently, a map $\\xi:\\Omega\\to\\mathcal{N}(X)$ is a Borel random element if and only if $\\omega\\mapsto\\xi(\\omega)(B)$ is measurable for every $B\\in\\mathcal{B}(X)$. We use these equivalent random-element and evaluation-wise formulations of a finite point process interchangeably; see \\citet{daley2003introduction} and \\citet{kallenberg2017random}.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
