      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Path Spaces on Compact Real Intervals",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:pointwise-versus-lp}\n    We treat the elements of $C([a,b];X)$ and $D^\\pm([a,b];X)$ below as pointwise functions, so their values $f(t)$ and graphs $\\Gamma(f)$ are well-defined. By contrast, $L^p$ (Appendix~\\ref{app:measure-theory}) consists of equivalence classes modulo almost-everywhere equality; a generic $L^p$ element has no representative-independent value at a fixed point and no canonical graph. Passing from a path space to $L^p$ therefore retains only the equivalence class of a path; operations that depend on point values, such as evaluation or the graph, are lost.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
