      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:sorting-permutations-not-unique}\n    The proof verifies that the sorting map is unique. However, there may still be multiple permutations that sort the tuple in the event that there are repeated elements. For instance, if $\\bm{x} = (x,x)$ for some $x \\in X$, then any permutation of the two entries will sort the tuple, as they are identical. Nevertheless, the resulting sorted tuple is unique, as it is the same regardless of the permutation applied.",
