      "order": 334,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{rem:unordered-configuration-space}\n    As a set, $\\mathscr{F}(X)$ is the union over $n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$ of the unordered configuration spaces of $n$ distinct points. The topologies differ, however. In a configuration space the number of points is locally constant, whereas under the Hausdorff distance distinct points may merge in the limit; for instance, $d_H(\\{0,1/k\\},\\{0\\})=1/k\\to 0$ in $\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{R})$. With the Hausdorff distance, $\\mathscr{F}(X)\\setminus\\{\\emptyset\\}$ is the union of the finite subset spaces of \\citet{borsuk1931symmetric}, who called them symmetric products; these differ from the symmetric products $X^n/S_n$ of algebraic topology, which retain multiplicities.",
      "status": "verified",
