      "order": 339,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "Note that the measurability results below require only that the order relation belong to the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra $\\mathcal{B}(X\\times X)$ of the product topology, and therefore require neither completeness nor separability of $X$. Separability becomes important when measurable maps into $X\\times X$ are constructed componentwise from maps into each coordinate. For instance, if $\\xi$ and $\\eta$ are $X$-valued random elements on a measurable space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F})$, then the paired map $\\omega \\mapsto (\\xi(\\omega), \\eta(\\omega))$ is $(\\mathcal{F}, \\mathcal{B}(X)\\otimes\\mathcal{B}(X))$-measurable; consequently, the comparison set $\\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid \\xi(\\omega) \\preceq \\eta(\\omega)\\}$ is guaranteed to be an event in $\\mathcal{F}$ whenever $\\mathcal{B}(X\\times X)=\\mathcal{B}(X)\\otimes\\mathcal{B}(X)$, which holds when $X$ is separable, whereas without separability even the diagonal $\\Delta_X$ may fail to lie in $\\mathcal{B}(X)\\otimes\\mathcal{B}(X)$.",
