      "section": "Ordering the Market Orders",
      "statement": "We consider the non-strict case in Proposition~\\ref{prop:disjoint-total-order}. However, one can proceed analogously with the strict case $\\prec$; the two differ only by the diagonal $\\Delta_X = \\{(x,x) \\mid x \\in X\\}$. That is,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\prec ~=~ \\preceq\\setminus\\Delta_X,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\preceq ~= ~ \\prec\\cup ~\\Delta_X,\n    \\end{align*}\n    where one has to recall that a relation is, strictly speaking, a subset of $X \\times X$.",
