      "lean": [
        {
          "declaration": "pseudoEMetricTopology_hausdorff_iff",
          "declarationKind": "theorem",
          "explanation": "the topology of a pseudometric is Hausdorff exactly when zero distance separates points",
          "file": "Thesis/Appendices/Analysis/AnalysisBasic.lean",
          "fullName": "Thesis.Foundations.Preliminary.Analysis.pseudoEMetricTopology_hausdorff_iff",
          "informal": "Informal statement: the topology of a pseudometric is Hausdorff exactly when zero distance separates points. Lean strategy / thesis relation note: both directions follow the manuscript. If zero distance separates points, two distinct points have positive distance and the disjoint half-distance balls of half_distance_balls_disjoint are the required open neighbourhoods. Conversely, if the space is Hausdorff but d(x,y) = 0 with x ≠ y, pick disjoint open neighbourhoods U ∋ x, V ∋ y; the centred-ball characterisation of open sets gives a ball B(x,ε) ⊆ U, and d(x,y) = 0 < ε puts y in U, contradicting disjointness",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "line": 465,
          "module": "Thesis.Appendices.Analysis.AnalysisBasic",
          "name": "pseudoEMetricTopology_hausdorff_iff",
          "source": "Thesis/Appendices/Analysis/AnalysisBasic.lean",
          "strategy": "both directions follow the manuscript. If zero distance separates points, two distinct points have positive distance and the disjoint half-distance balls of half_distance_balls_disjoint are the required open neighbourhoods. Conversely, if the space is Hausdorff but d(x,y) = 0 with x ≠ y, pick disjoint open neighbourhoods U ∋ x, V ∋ y; the centred-ball characterisation of open sets gives a ball B(x,ε) ⊆ U, and d(x,y) = 0 < ε puts y in U, contradicting disjointness"
        }
      ],
      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 357,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Metric Spaces",
      "statement": "Let $(X,d)$ be a pseudometric space, and let $(X,\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathcal{B}_d})$ be the topological space induced by $d$. Then $(X,\\mathcal{T}_{\\mathcal{B}_d})$ is Hausdorff if and only if\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x,x^\\ast) = 0 \\implies x = x^\\ast\n        \\qquad \\text{for all } x,x^\\ast \\in X.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 261,
        "references": [
          "prop:topology-extended-pseudometric"
        ],
        "source": "A - appendices/analysis.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/source__A---appendices-analysis.tex__255__prop__19__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, suppose that $d(x,x^\\ast)=0$ implies $x=x^\\ast$. Let $x \\neq x^\\ast$. Then $d(x,x^\\ast)>0$. Set $\\varepsilon = \\frac{1}{2}\\min\\{d(x,x^\\ast),1\\}$, which is a finite positive real number. We claim that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        B_\\varepsilon(x) \\cap B_\\varepsilon(x^\\ast) = \\emptyset.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Indeed, if $y$ belonged to the intersection, then the triangle inequality would give\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x,x^\\ast) \\leq d(x,y) + d(y,x^\\ast) < 2\\varepsilon = \\min\\{d(x,x^\\ast),1\\} \\leq d(x,x^\\ast),\n    \\end{align*}\n    which is impossible. Hence the induced topology is Hausdorff.\n\n    Conversely, suppose there exist distinct points $x,x^\\ast \\in X$ with $d(x,x^\\ast)=0$. Let $O$ be any open neighbourhood of $x$. Since $O$ is open, the centred-ball characterisation following Proposition~\\ref{prop:topology-extended-pseudometric} shows that there exists $\\varepsilon > 0$ such that $B_\\varepsilon(x) \\subseteq O$. But $d(x,x^\\ast)=0$ implies $x^\\ast \\in B_\\varepsilon(x)$, hence $x^\\ast \\in O$. By symmetry, every open neighbourhood of $x^\\ast$ also contains $x$. Therefore no two open neighbourhoods of $x$ and $x^\\ast$ can be disjoint, so the topology is not Hausdorff."
      },
