      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Path Spaces on Compact Real Intervals",
      "statement": "Let $(X,d)$ and $(Y,\\rho)$ be metric spaces. A function $f:X \\to Y$ is called continuous if for every $x_0 \\in X$ and every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $\\delta > 0$ such that, for every $x\\in X$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x,x_0) < \\delta \\implies \\rho(f(x),f(x_0)) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}",
      "status": "verified",
