        "text": "We must show that $\\Gamma(f)$ is compact, closed, and bounded in $([a,b]\\times X,\\rho)$. Define $\\Phi:[a,b] \\to [a,b] \\times X$ by $\\Phi(t) = (t,f(t))$. Continuity of $f$ implies that $\\Phi$ is continuous under $\\rho$. The interval $[a,b]$ is compact, so its continuous image $\\Phi([a,b]) = \\Gamma(f)$ is compact. Every metric space in the sense of Remark~\\ref{rem:extended-metric-convention} is Hausdorff, so the compact set $\\Gamma(f)$ is closed. The finite-distance components of $\\rho$ (Remark~\\ref{rem:extended-metric-convention}) are both open and closed, and $\\Gamma(f)$ is connected as the continuous image of the connected interval $[a,b]$; hence it lies in one such component. On that component, $\\rho$ is an ordinary metric, so compactness implies that $\\Gamma(f)$ is bounded, as desired."
