      "section": "Path Spaces on Compact Real Intervals",
      "statement": "{billingsley2013convergence}}]\n    Let $a,b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, and let $D([a,b];X)$ denote either $D^+([a,b];X)$ or $D^-([a,b];X)$. A sequence $(f_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\subseteq D([a,b];X)$ converges to $f \\in D([a,b];X)$ in $(D([a,b];X),d_\\circ)$ if and only if there exists a sequence $(\\lambda_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\subseteq \\Lambda([a,b])$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sup_{t \\in [a,b]} |\\lambda_n(t)-t| = 0\n    \\end{align*}\n    and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sup_{t \\in [a,b]} d(f_n(\\lambda_n(t)),f(t)) = 0.\n    \\end{align*}",
