      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Finite Measures and Random Measures",
      "statement": "Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$ and $(X,d)$ be as in Definition \\ref{defn:kallenberg-random-measure}, and let $\\xi:\\Omega\\times\\mathcal{B}(X)\\to[0,\\infty]$ be such that $\\xi(\\omega,\\cdot)$ is a finite Borel measure for every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$. Endow $\\mathcal{M}(X)$ with the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra induced by the Prokhorov metric. Then the following are equivalent:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item the map $\\xi:\\Omega \\times \\mathcal{B}(X) \\to [0,\\infty]$ is a random measure in the sense of Definition \\ref{defn:kallenberg-random-measure};\n        \\item the map $\\Xi:\\Omega \\to \\mathcal{M}(X)$ defined by\n        \\begin{align*}\n            \\Xi(\\omega)=\\xi(\\omega,\\cdot)\n        \\end{align*}\n        is measurable.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
