      "section": "Analytic Results for Primitive Actions",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:analytic-properties-actions}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, and let\n    \\begin{enumerate}[-]\n        \\item $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple space over $X$ endowed with the tuple Hausdorff distance, \n        \\item $(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}), d_H)$ be the set of finite subsets of the natural numbers, endowed with the Hausdorff distance induced by the Euclidean distance, and \n        \\item $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$ be the set of labelled finite sets over $X$, endowed with the index-augmented Hausdorff distance.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    If all product spaces are endowed with the product metrics, then \n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $\\oplus:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\times \\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(X)$ as defined in Definition~\\ref{defn:concat-tuple} is continuous\\index{continuity!primitive actions},\\label{property:concat-continuous}\n        \\item $\\ominus:\\mathscr{T}(X)\\times \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N})\\to\\mathscr{T}(X)$ as defined in Definition~\\ref{defn:cancel} is continuous, and \\label{property:cancel-continuous}\n        \\item $\\circledast:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\times \\mathscr{L}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(X)$ as defined in Definition~\\ref{defn:modification} is continuous. \\label{property:modification-continuous}\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 286,
        "references": [
          "property:concat-continuous",
          "thm:the-standard-machinery",
          "property:cancel-continuous",
          "prop:finite-hausdorff-uniformly-discrete",
          "property:modification-continuous",
          "lem:helpful-labelled"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/3_market_actions.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__analytic-properties-actions__proof.svg",
        "text": "Let $\\varepsilon > 0$ and fix $\\delta \\in (0,\\min(1,\\varepsilon))$.\n\n    For \\eqref{property:concat-continuous}, suppose that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\max(\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0), \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{y}, \\bm{y}_0)) < \\delta.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\delta < 1$, the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}) implies\n    \\begin{align*}\n        m \\overset{\\mathrm{def}}{=} \\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}_0)\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        n \\overset{\\mathrm{def}}{=} \\ell(\\bm{y}) = \\ell(\\bm{y}_0),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and, since $\\min(\\mathscr{d}_H,1)=\\min(d_\\infty,1)$ on each layer, where $d_\\infty$ is the maximum pointwise metric, the two distances coincide on each layer whenever either is below $1$. If $m+n = 0$, then both outputs are empty tuples and there is nothing to show. Otherwise,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}\\oplus\\bm{y}, \\bm{x}_0\\oplus\\bm{y}_0)\n        = \\max_{i \\in \\{1,\\dots,m+n\\}} d\\big((\\bm{x}\\oplus\\bm{y})(i),(\\bm{x}_0\\oplus\\bm{y}_0)(i)\\big)\n        < \\delta < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence $\\oplus$ is continuous.\n\n    For \\eqref{property:cancel-continuous}, suppose that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\max(\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0), d_H(\\eta,\\eta_0)) < \\delta.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Again, $\\delta < 1$ implies $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}_0)$ and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H(\\eta,\\eta_0) < 1 \\implies \\eta = \\eta_0\n    \\end{align*}\n    because $(\\mathbb{N},|\\cdot|)$ is uniformly discrete and hence so is $(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}),d_H)$ by Proposition~\\ref{prop:finite-hausdorff-uniformly-discrete}. Let\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I = \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}_0)\\setminus \\eta_0.\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $I = \\emptyset$, then both $\\bm{x}\\ominus\\eta$ and $\\bm{x}_0\\ominus\\eta_0$ are empty tuples, so the distance is $0$. Otherwise, if\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I = \\{i_1 < \\dots < i_k\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    then reindexing simply produces the tuples\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\bm{x}\\ominus\\eta &= r \\mapsto \\bm{x}(i_r),\\\\\n        \\bm{x}_0\\ominus\\eta_0 &= r \\mapsto \\bm{x}_0(i_r),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and therefore\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}\\ominus\\eta, \\bm{x}_0\\ominus\\eta_0)\n        &= \\max_{r\\in\\{1,\\dots,k\\}} d(\\bm{x}(i_r), \\bm{x}_0(i_r))\\\\\n        &\\leq \\max_{i \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{x}_0)} d(\\bm{x}(i), \\bm{x}_0(i))\\\\\n        &< \\delta < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence $\\ominus$ is continuous.\n\n    Finally, consider \\eqref{property:modification-continuous} and suppose that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\max(\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}_0), d_H^\\ast(\\xi,\\xi_0)) < \\delta.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\delta < 1$, the tuple part gives $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}_0)$, and as $d_H^\\ast(\\xi,\\xi_0)<1$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-labelled} shows that $\\xi$ and $\\xi_0$ have exactly the same set of indices. Denote this common finite index set by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        D = \\{n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\mid \\exists z\\in X:(n,z)\\in\\xi\\} = \\{n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\mid \\exists z\\in X:(n,z)\\in\\xi_0\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, for every $n \\in D$ there are unique elements $\\xi(n), \\xi_0(n) \\in X$ with\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (n,\\xi(n)) \\in \\xi\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        (n,\\xi_0(n)) \\in \\xi_0,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and they satisfy\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(\\xi(n),\\xi_0(n)) < \\delta.\n    \\end{align*}\n\nIf $\\ell(\\bm{x}_0)=0$, then both $\\bm{x}\\circledast\\xi$ and $\\bm{x}_0\\circledast\\xi_0$ are empty tuples, so their tuple Hausdorff distance is $0$. We may therefore assume that $\\ell(\\bm{x}_0)>0$.\n    Now let $m = \\ell(\\bm{x}_0) = \\ell(\\bm{x})$. For each $i \\in \\{1,\\dots,m\\}$ there are two cases. If $i \\notin D$, then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\bm{x}\\circledast \\xi)(i) = \\bm{x}(i)\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        (\\bm{x}_0\\circledast \\xi_0)(i) = \\bm{x}_0(i).\n    \\end{align*}\n    If $i \\in D$, then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\bm{x}\\circledast \\xi)(i) = \\xi(i)\n        \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n        (\\bm{x}_0\\circledast \\xi_0)(i) = \\xi_0(i).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence, for every $i \\in \\{1,\\dots,m\\}$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d\\big((\\bm{x}\\circledast \\xi)(i), (\\bm{x}_0\\circledast \\xi_0)(i)\\big) < \\delta.\n    \\end{align*}\n    By the \\hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, the output tuples lie on the same length layer, so\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}\\circledast \\xi, \\bm{x}_0\\circledast \\xi_0)\n        = \\max_{i\\in\\{1,\\dots,m\\}} d\\big((\\bm{x}\\circledast \\xi)(i), (\\bm{x}_0\\circledast \\xi_0)(i)\\big)\n        < \\delta < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore, $\\circledast$ is continuous."
      },
