      "order": 372,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Measurable Spaces and Measures",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:atomic}\n    Let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mu)$ be a $\\sigma$-finite measure space. Then there exist unique measures $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ on $(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F})$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mu = \\alpha + \\beta,\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $\\alpha$ is diffuse and $\\beta$ is purely atomic. If moreover $\\Omega$ is a Polish space and $\\mathcal{F} = \\mathcal{B}(\\Omega)$, then there is an index set $I\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$, possibly empty, such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta = \\sum_{n\\in I} a_n\\delta_{x_n},\n    \\end{align*}\n    with $a_n=\\beta(\\{x_n\\})>0$ and pairwise distinct points $x_n$, where every singleton $\\{x_n\\}$ is a point atom of $\\beta$. When $I=\\emptyset$, the sum denotes the zero measure.",
