      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Conditional Expectation and Regression",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:conditional-expect-Hilbert}\n    Let $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, and let $(\\mathcal{H}, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ be a real separable Hilbert space for which $(e_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is an orthonormal basis. Let $\\mathcal{G} \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}$ be a sub-$\\sigma$-algebra, and let $X:\\Omega \\to \\mathcal{H}$ be an $\\mathcal{F}$-measurable random element for which $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X\\rVert^2] < \\infty$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid \\mathcal{G}] = \\sum_{n\\in \\mathbb{N}} \\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n \\mid \\mathcal{G}] e_n,\\quad \\text{a.s.},\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $\\theta_n = \\langle X, e_n\\rangle$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 150,
        "references": [],
        "source": "2 - day-ahead/3_functional_data_analysis.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__conditional-expect-Hilbert__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that the partial sums of the series $\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n\\mid\\mathcal{G}]e_n$ converge in $\\mathcal{H}$ almost surely to $\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid\\mathcal{G}]$. For almost every $\\omega\\in\\Omega$, the orthonormal basis expansion gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        X(\\omega) = \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} \\theta_n(\\omega) e_n,\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $\\theta_n(\\omega)=\\langle X(\\omega),e_n\\rangle$. Define the partial sums\n    \\begin{align*}\n        S_N(\\omega) = \\sum_{n = 1}^N \\theta_n(\\omega) e_n,\\qquad N\\in\\mathbb{N}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    By the linearity of conditional expectation\\index{conditional expectation} and the property that $\\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n e_n\\mid\\mathcal{G}]=\\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n\\mid\\mathcal{G}]e_n$ for a fixed deterministic basis vector $e_n$, it follows that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[S_N \\mid \\mathcal{G}] = \\sum_{n=1}^N \\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n \\mid \\mathcal{G}] e_n\\qquad\\text{almost surely.}\n    \\end{align*}\n    Moreover, because $S_N$ is the orthogonal projection of $X$ onto $\\mathrm{span}\\{e_1,\\dots,e_N\\}$, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        S_N\\to X,\n        \\qquad\n        \\lVert S_N\\rVert\\leq\\lVert X\\rVert,\n        \\quad\\text{almost surely.}\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X\\rVert]<\\infty$ by the square-integrability hypothesis, the conditional dominated convergence theorem yields, almost surely,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid \\mathcal{G}] = \\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\mathbb{E}[S_N \\mid \\mathcal{G}] = \\lim_{N \\to \\infty} \\sum_{n = 1}^N \\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n \\mid \\mathcal{G}] e_n = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\mathbb{E}[\\theta_n \\mid \\mathcal{G}] e_n,\n    \\end{align*}\n    which establishes the coordinatewise representation of $\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid\\mathcal{G}]$, as desired."
      },
