            "text": "For the third criterion, we first note that $X_\\varphi$ is cancellative. Indeed, the convex cone structure on $X_\\varphi$ is obtained by transporting the cone structure of $\\varphi(X)\\subseteq V$ along the bijection\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Phi:X_\\varphi \\to \\varphi(X)\\subseteq V.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus, for all $a,c \\in X_\\varphi$, the transported addition satisfies\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Phi(a+c)=\\Phi(a)+\\Phi(c).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Now suppose that $a+c=b+c$ in $X_\\varphi$. Applying $\\Phi$ yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Phi(a+c)=\\Phi(b+c).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Using the compatibility of $\\Phi$ with the transported addition, we obtain\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Phi(a)+\\Phi(c)=\\Phi(b)+\\Phi(c).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $V$ is a vector space, addition in $V$ is cancellative, and hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Phi(a)=\\Phi(b).\n    \\end{align*}\n    By injectivity of $\\Phi$, it follows that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        a=b.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore $X_\\varphi$ is cancellative. Now suppose that $(x, z) \\sim (x^\\prime, z^\\prime)$ and $(x^\\prime, z^\\prime) \\sim (x^\\ast, z^\\ast)$. Then\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:non-prime-and-prime}\n        x + z^\\prime = x^\\prime + z,\n    \\end{align}\n    and\n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:prime-and-ast}\n        x^\\prime + z^\\ast = x^\\ast + z^\\prime.\n    \\end{align}\n    Using associativity and commutativity of addition in $X_\\varphi$, together with equations \\eqref{eq:non-prime-and-prime} and \\eqref{eq:prime-and-ast}, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (x+z^\\ast)+z^\\prime \n        &= (x+z^\\prime)+z^\\ast \\\\\n        &= (x^\\prime+z)+z^\\ast \\\\\n        &= z+(x^\\prime+z^\\ast) \\\\\n        &= z+(x^\\ast+z^\\prime) \\\\\n        &= (x^\\ast+z)+z^\\prime.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $X_\\varphi$ is cancellative, we may cancel the common term $z^\\prime$, obtaining\n    \\begin{align*}\n        x+z^\\ast = x^\\ast+z.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore $(x,z)\\sim (x^\\ast,z^\\ast)$, from which it follows that $\\sim$ is transitive. Hence $\\sim$ is an equivalence relation on $X_\\varphi \\times X_\\varphi$.",
