            "references": [],
            "text": "First, suppose that $\\alpha\\geq 0$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha[(x,z)] = [(\\alpha x,\\alpha z)]\n    \\end{align*}\n    and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha[(x^\\prime,z^\\prime)] = [(\\alpha x^\\prime,\\alpha z^\\prime)].\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus, it suffices to show that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\alpha x,\\alpha z)\\sim(\\alpha x^\\prime,\\alpha z^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    By definition of $\\sim$, this is equivalent to showing that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha x+\\alpha z^\\prime = \\alpha x^\\prime+\\alpha z.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Using compatibility of the transported cone structure with non-negative scalar multiplication, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha x+\\alpha z^\\prime &= \\alpha(x+z^\\prime) \\\\\n        &= \\alpha(x^\\prime+z) \\\\\n        &= \\alpha x^\\prime+\\alpha z.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\alpha x,\\alpha z) \\sim (\\alpha x^\\prime,\\alpha z^\\prime),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        [(\\alpha x,\\alpha z)] = [(\\alpha x^\\prime,\\alpha z^\\prime)].\n    \\end{align*}\n    So scalar multiplication is independent of representatives when $\\alpha\\geq 0$.",
