            "text": "Now suppose that $\\alpha<0$. Let\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta=|\\alpha|.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $\\beta>0$, and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha[(x,z)] = [(\\beta z,\\beta x)]\n    \\end{align*}\n    while\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha[(x^\\prime,z^\\prime)] = [(\\beta z^\\prime,\\beta x^\\prime)].\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus, it suffices to show that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\beta z,\\beta x)\\sim(\\beta z^\\prime,\\beta x^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    By definition of $\\sim$, this is equivalent to showing that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta z+\\beta x^\\prime\n        =\n        \\beta z^\\prime+\\beta x.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $(x,z)\\sim(x^\\prime,z^\\prime)$, we have\n    \\begin{align*}\n        x+z^\\prime=x^\\prime+z.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Multiplying by $\\beta$ and using compatibility with non-negative scalar multiplication gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta x+\\beta z^\\prime\n        &= \\beta(x+z^\\prime) \\\\\n        &= \\beta(x^\\prime+z) \\\\\n        &= \\beta x^\\prime+\\beta z.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Using commutativity of addition in $X_\\varphi$, this gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta z^\\prime+\\beta x\n        =\n        \\beta z+\\beta x^\\prime.\n    \\end{align*}\n    By symmetry of equality,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\beta z+\\beta x^\\prime\n        =\n        \\beta z^\\prime+\\beta x.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Therefore\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (\\beta z,\\beta x)\\sim(\\beta z^\\prime,\\beta x^\\prime).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\beta=|\\alpha|$, this means that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (|\\alpha|z,|\\alpha|x)\\sim(|\\alpha|z^\\prime,|\\alpha|x^\\prime),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        [(\\left|\\alpha\\right|z,\\left|\\alpha\\right|x)]\n        =\n        [(\\left|\\alpha\\right|z^\\prime,\\left|\\alpha\\right|x^\\prime)].\n    \\end{align*}\n    So scalar multiplication is independent of representatives when $\\alpha<0$.",
