            "text": "We now proceed to show that $(\\mathscr{V}(X, \\varphi), +, \\cdot)$ is a real vector space. To do so, we define the auxiliary map $T:\\mathscr{V}(X, \\varphi) \\to \\mathrm{span}~\\varphi(X)$ by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        T([(x,z)]) \\overset{\\mathrm{def}}{=} \\Phi(x) - \\Phi(z).\n    \\end{align*}\n    First, let us verify that $T$ is well-defined. Note that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        [(x,z)] &= \\{ (x^\\prime, z^\\prime) \\in X_\\varphi \\times X_\\varphi \\mid x + z^\\prime = z + x^\\prime\\}\\\\\n        &=\\{ (x^\\prime, z^\\prime) \\in X_\\varphi \\times X_\\varphi \\mid \\Phi(x) + \\Phi(z^\\prime) = \\Phi(z) + \\Phi(x^\\prime)\\}\\\\\n        &=\\{ (x^\\prime, z^\\prime) \\in X_\\varphi \\times X_\\varphi \\mid \\Phi(x) - \\Phi(z) = \\Phi(x^\\prime) - \\Phi(z^\\prime)\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Hence, $T$ is well-defined, as its value is constant on each equivalence class. Moreover, $\\Phi(x)-\\Phi(z)\\in \\mathrm{span}~\\varphi(X)$ by construction. We now claim that $T$ is a bijection. Injectivity follows from the property that if\n    \\begin{align*}\n        T([(x,z)]) = \\Phi(x) - \\Phi(z) = \\Phi(x^\\prime) - \\Phi(z^\\prime) = T([(x^\\prime, z^\\prime)]) \n    \\end{align*}\n    then $(x,z) \\sim (x^\\prime, z^\\prime)$ and so $[(x,z)] = [(x^\\prime,z^\\prime)]$. For surjectivity, let $v \\in \\mathrm{span}\\,\\varphi(X)$ and write\n    \\begin{align*}\n        v = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i \\varphi(u_i),\n    \\end{align*}\n    for $(u_i)_{i=1}^n \\subseteq X$ and $(\\alpha_i)_{i=1}^n \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. Define \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\alpha^+_i =\\max(\\alpha_i, 0), \\quad \\alpha^-_i =-\\min(\\alpha_i, 0),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and set \n    \\begin{align*}\n        v^+ = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha^+_i \\varphi(u_i), \\quad v^- = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha^-_i \\varphi(u_i).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\varphi(X)$ is a convex cone, and hence, by induction on $n$, closed under non-negative combinations of $n$ elements, and $\\Phi : X_\\varphi \\to \\varphi(X)$ is bijective, there exist $x,z \\in X_\\varphi$ such that $\\Phi(x)=v^+$ and $\\Phi(z)=v^-$. By construction,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        T([(x,z)]) = v^+ - v^- = v,\n    \\end{align*}\n    so $T$ is surjective.",
