            "text": "We must show that for every $B \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2))$, it holds that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    First, note that since $\\mathbb{N}^2$ is countable, it follows, by Proposition~\\ref{prop:countable-finite-sets}, that $\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$ is countable. Moreover, since $B \\subseteq \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$, $B$ must also be countable. Now, as \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(B) =\\bigcup_{\\eta \\in B}\\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(\\{\\eta\\})\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a countable union, it suffices to prove that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(\\{\\eta\\}) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq))\n    \\end{align*}\n    for each $\\eta \\in \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$.",
