      "order": 383,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Aggregation and Executable Volume",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:index-measurable}\n    The mapping $\\mathfrak{I}:\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq) \\to \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$ defined by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}(\\bm{p}^s, \\bm{p}^b) = \\{(i,j) \\in \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p}^s) \\times \\mathrm{domain}(\\bm{p}^b)\\mid \\bm{p}^s(i) \\leq \\bm{p}^b(j)\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is measurable whenever $\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq)$ is endowed with the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra induced by the product of the tuple Hausdorff distances, and $\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$ is endowed with its Borel $\\sigma$-algebra induced by the Hausdorff distance.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 582,
        "references": [
          "prop:countable-finite-sets",
          "lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable",
          "lem:tuple-space-measurable-2",
          "thm:measurable-partition",
          "lem:bounded-sets-measurable"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/4_market_clearing.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__index-measurable__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that for every $B \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2))$, it holds that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    First, note that since $\\mathbb{N}^2$ is countable, it follows, by Proposition~\\ref{prop:countable-finite-sets}, that $\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$ is countable. Moreover, since $B \\subseteq \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$, $B$ must also be countable. Now, as \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(B) =\\bigcup_{\\eta \\in B}\\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(\\{\\eta\\})\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a countable union, it suffices to prove that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(\\{\\eta\\}) \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq))\n    \\end{align*}\n    for each $\\eta \\in \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$.\n\n    Extending the sets of Lemma~\\ref{lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable} by the empty tuple, we set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        I_0 &=\\{\\bm{e}\\},\\\\\n        I_n &= \\{\\bm{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\mid \\bm{x}(1) \\leq \\dots \\leq \\bm{x}(n)\\}\\quad  \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and similarly, \n    \\begin{align*}\n        D_0 &=\\{\\bm{e}\\},\\\\\n        D_m &= \\{\\bm{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m \\mid \\bm{x}(1) \\geq \\dots \\geq \\bm{x}(m)\\}\\quad  \\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    The singleton sets $I_0=D_0=\\{\\bm{e}\\}$ are Borel measurable in the zero-dimensional components. By Lemma~\\ref{lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable}, $I_n$ and $D_m$ are measurable in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\\mathbb{R}^m$, endowed with their usual Euclidean Borel $\\sigma$-algebras, for all $n,m \\in \\mathbb{N}$. \n\n    Lemma~\\ref{lem:tuple-space-measurable-2} also makes every set $I_n$ and every set $D_m$, for $n,m\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$, measurable in the tuple space. Consequently,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\leq) \\times \\mathscr{S}(\\mathbb{R}, \\geq) &= \\left(\\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0} I_n\\right) \\times \\left(\\bigcup_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0} D_n\\right)\\\\\n        &=\\bigcup_{(n,m)\\in \\mathbb{N}^2_0} (I_n \\times D_m),\n    \\end{align*}\n    with $I_n \\times D_m$ being measurable in the product space. Therefore, by Theorem~\\ref{thm:measurable-partition}, it suffices to prove that for arbitrary $n,m \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$, the functions \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathfrak{I}\\restriction_{I_n \\times D_m}: I_n\\times D_m \\to \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)\n    \\end{align*}\n    are $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^n \\times \\mathbb{R}^m)/\\wp(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2))$-measurable. Fix $n,m\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$ and $\\eta\\in\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}^2)$. If\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\eta \\nsubseteq \\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots,m\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    then $\\mathfrak{I}\\restriction_{I_n\\times D_m}^{-1}(\\{\\eta\\})=\\emptyset$, which is measurable. Therefore, assume that $\\eta \\subseteq \\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$. For $(i,j) \\in \\{1,\\dots, n\\}\\times \\{1,\\dots, m\\}$, recall from Lemma~\\ref{lem:bounded-sets-measurable} the sets\n    \\begin{align*}\n        L^{i,j}_{n,m} = \\{(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) \\in I_n\\times D_m \\mid \\bm{x}(i) \\leq \\bm{y}(j)\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and \n    \\begin{align*}\n       G^{i,j}_{n,m} = \\{(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) \\in I_n\\times D_m \\mid \\bm{x}(i) > \\bm{y}(j)\\},\n    \\end{align*}\n    and observe that both are measurable sets by Lemma~\\ref{lem:bounded-sets-measurable}. One then verifies, including the case $\\eta=\\emptyset$, that\n    \\begin{align}\n        \\mathfrak{I}\\restriction_{I_n \\times D_m}^{-1}(\\{\\eta\\}) =  \\bigcap_{(i,j) \\in \\eta} L^{i,j}_{n,m} \\cap \\bigcap_{\\substack{i \\in \\{1,\\dots, n\\} \\\\ j\\in \\{1,\\dots,m\\}\\\\ (i,j) \\notin \\eta}} G^{i,j}_{n,m},\n    \\end{align}\n    which is a finite intersection of measurable sets. Consequently, the result follows from Lemma~\\ref{lem:tuple-space-measurable-2}, applied with $k=2$, and Theorem~\\ref{thm:measurable-partition}."
      },
