      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:labelled-properties}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and endow $\\mathbb{N}$ with the Euclidean distance. On $\\mathbb{N}\\times X$, define $d^\\ast:(\\mathbb{N}\\times X) \\times (\\mathbb{N}\\times X) \\to [0, \\infty]$ by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        d^\\ast((n,x),(n^\\prime, x^\\prime)) = d(x,x^\\prime) + |n-n^\\prime|.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Let $\\mathscr{L}(X) \\subseteq \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}\\times X)$ be endowed with the Hausdorff distance $d_H^\\ast : \\mathscr{L}(X) \\times \\mathscr{L}(X) \\to [0,\\infty]$ induced by $d^\\ast$; since $d^\\ast$ is a metric on $\\mathbb{N}\\times X$, Theorem~\\ref{thm:Hausdorff-finite-sets} makes $d_H^\\ast$ a metric on $\\mathscr{L}(X)$. It holds that\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item if $(X,d)$ is separable, then so is $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$.\\label{property:labelled-finite-separable}\n        \\item if $(X,d)$ is complete, then so is $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$.\\label{property:labelled-finite-complete}\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 845,
        "references": [
          "property:labelled-finite-separable",
          "prop:finite-separable",
          "lem:helpful-labelled"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__labelled-properties__proof.svg",
        "text": "For \\eqref{property:labelled-finite-separable}, note that if $(X,d)$ is separable, then $(\\mathbb{N} \\times X, d^\\ast)$ is separable, as it is a product of two separable spaces. By Proposition~\\ref{prop:finite-separable}, $(\\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}\\times X), d_H^\\ast)$ is separable; since $\\mathscr{L}(X) \\subseteq \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}\\times X)$, the subspace $\\mathscr{L}(X)$ is also separable, because every subspace of a separable metric space is separable.\n\n    Now let $(\\xi_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}} \\subseteq \\mathscr{L}(X)$ be a Cauchy sequence. We must show that there exists an element $\\xi \\in \\mathscr{L}(X)$ such that $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty} d_H^\\ast(\\xi_k, \\xi) = 0$. We proceed constructively; first, let $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ be such that $d_H^\\ast(\\xi_k,\\xi_m) < 1$ for all $k,m\\geq N$, which exists because the sequence is Cauchy. Now, define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Pi:\\mathscr{L}(X)&\\to \\mathscr{F}(\\mathbb{N}) \\\\\n        \\xi &\\mapsto \\{n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mid \\exists x \\in X : (n,x) \\in \\xi\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    By Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-labelled}, there is a finite set $I \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ such that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        k \\geq N \\implies \\Pi(\\xi_k) = I.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Note that if $I$ is empty, then the claim is verified, as this immediately implies, by Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-labelled}, that $k \\geq N \\implies\\xi_k = \\emptyset$. Therefore, we assume without loss of generality that $I \\neq \\emptyset$. For each $i \\in I$, define \n    \\begin{align*}\n        x_k(i) = x,\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $x \\in X$ is the unique element such that $(i,x) \\in \\xi_k$. Notice that since the sequence $(\\xi_k)_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is Cauchy, for every $\\varepsilon>0$ we may choose $N_\\varepsilon\\geq N$ such that $d_H^\\ast(\\xi_k,\\xi_m)<\\min(\\varepsilon,1)$ for $k,m\\geq N_\\varepsilon$. Lemma~\\ref{lem:helpful-labelled}, applied with $r=\\min(\\varepsilon,1)$, then gives, since each label occurs at most once in $\\xi_m$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        d(x_k(i),x_m(i))<\\varepsilon,\\qquad i\\in I,\\ k,m\\geq N_\\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus $(x_k(i))_{k = N}^\\infty$ is a Cauchy sequence for each $i \\in I$. Since $X$ is assumed complete, each sequence $(x_k(i))_{k = N}^\\infty$ has a limit, say $x(i) \\in X$. We claim that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\xi = \\{(i,x(i)) \\mid i \\in I\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is the limit of the Cauchy sequence. For $k \\geq N$ one has \n    \\begin{align*}\n        d_H^\\ast(\\xi_k, \\xi) \\leq \\max_{i \\in I} d(x_k(i), x(i)),\n    \\end{align*}\n    since the same indexed point in $\\xi$ is an admissible Hausdorff match for the corresponding indexed point in $\\xi_k$, and conversely. The right-hand side corresponds to the maximum metric on a finite product of $X$. Therefore, one has that \n    \\begin{align}\n        \\lim_{k\\to\\infty} d_H^\\ast(\\xi_k, \\xi) = 0,\n    \\end{align}\n    since $x_k(i) \\to x(i)$ for each of the finitely many $i \\in I$, which proves the claim."
      },
