      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Orthonormal Coordinates, Moments, and Covariance",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:lln-clt-mean}\\index{law of large numbers}\\index{central limit theorem}\n    Let $(\\mathcal{H},\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle)$ be a real separable Hilbert space, and let $X:\\Omega \\to \\mathcal{H}$ be a random element with $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X\\rVert] < \\infty$ and mean $\\mu$. Moreover, let $(X_i)_{i \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ be independent copies of $X$. Then, as $n \\to \\infty$,\n    \\begin{align}\n        \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\overset{\\text{a.s.}}{\\to} \\mu.\n    \\end{align}\n    Further, if $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X\\rVert^2] < \\infty$, then as $n \\to \\infty$,\n    \\begin{align}\n        \\sqrt{n}\\left(\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i - \\mu\\right) \\overset{d}{\\to} \\xi,\n    \\end{align}\n    where $\\xi$ is an $\\mathcal{H}$-valued Gaussian random element with mean $0$ and covariance operator $\\Sigma=\\mathbb{E}[(X - \\mu) \\otimes (X - \\mu)]$; that is, $\\langle\\xi,u\\rangle$ is normally distributed with mean $0$ and variance $\\langle\\Sigma u,u\\rangle$ for every $u\\in\\mathcal{H}$.",
