      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 389,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:measurable-sorting-exists}\n    Let $(X, \\preceq, d)$ be a metric space equipped with a Borel partial order. Let $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the associated tuple Hausdorff space, and endow $\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ with the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of its subspace topology. Then there exists a Borel measurable sorting map $\\varsigma:\\mathscr{T}(X) \\to \\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1881,
        "references": [
          "prop:permutations-countable",
          "prop:induced-sorting",
          "prop:measurable-partition-sorting",
          "cor:local-continuity-sorting",
          "thm:measurable-partition"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__measurable-sorting-exists__proof.svg",
        "text": "First, note that $\\mathscr{P}$ is countably infinite, since it is countable by Proposition~\\ref{prop:permutations-countable} and contains at least one permutation of each length $n\\in\\mathbb{N}_0$. Hence there exists a bijection $\\varphi:\\mathscr{P} \\to \\mathbb{N}$. Consequently, the associated sorting map defined in Proposition~\\ref{prop:induced-sorting} is continuous when restricted to the sets $(\\mathscr{U}_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ (as defined in Proposition~\\ref{prop:measurable-partition-sorting}) by Corollary~\\ref{cor:local-continuity-sorting}. Since $(\\mathscr{U}_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ forms a measurable partition, and since the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of a subspace $A$ of a metric space $Y$ is the trace $\\{B \\cap A \\mid B \\in \\mathcal{B}(Y)\\}$, so that continuity on $\\mathscr{U}_n$ gives measurability with respect to the trace $\\sigma$-algebra, it follows by Theorem~\\ref{thm:measurable-partition} that the associated sorting map is measurable."
      },
