      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:pasting-lemma-measurable}\n    Let $(E, \\Sigma)$ be a measurable space and $(X_\\alpha, d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$ be a countable family of metric spaces for which $(\\mathcal{B}(X_\\alpha))_{\\alpha \\in A}$ denotes the corresponding Borel $\\sigma$-algebras induced by $(d_\\alpha)_{\\alpha \\in A}$. Moreover, let $(X,d)$ be the disjoint union metric space and let $\\mathcal{B}(X)$ denote the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra on $X$ induced by $d$. Then, $f: X \\to E$ is measurable if and only if \n    \\begin{align*}\n        f\\restriction_{X_\\alpha} &: X_\\alpha \\to E\\\\\n        x &\\mapsto f(x,\\alpha)\n    \\end{align*}\n    is measurable for each $\\alpha \\in A$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 470,
        "references": [],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__pasting-lemma-measurable__proof.svg",
        "text": "Recall the canonical injection\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\iota_\\alpha : X_\\alpha \\to X,\\qquad x \\mapsto (x,\\alpha).\n    \\end{align*}\n    By construction, $\\iota_\\alpha$ is an isometry from $(X_\\alpha,d_\\alpha)$ onto the subset $X_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\} \\subseteq X$. Moreover, each set $X_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\}$ is both open and closed in $X$. It is open because every point $(x,\\alpha)$ has a ball of radius, say, $1/2$ contained in $X_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\}$, and it is closed because its complement is the union of the other open components.\n\n    If $f$ is measurable, then for every $B \\in \\Sigma$ and every $\\alpha \\in A$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        (f\\restriction_{X_\\alpha})^{-1}(B)\n        = \\iota_\\alpha^{-1}(f^{-1}(B)) \\in \\mathcal{B}(X_\\alpha),\n    \\end{align*}\n    since $\\iota_\\alpha$ is continuous.\n\n    Conversely, assume that each $f\\restriction_{X_\\alpha}$ is measurable. We use that the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of a subspace $Y \\subseteq X$ is the trace $\\{B \\cap Y \\mid B \\in \\mathcal{B}(X)\\}$, so that $\\iota_\\alpha$ maps Borel sets of $X_\\alpha$ to Borel sets of the Borel subspace $X_\\alpha\\times\\{\\alpha\\}$, and hence of $X$. Let $B \\in \\Sigma$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        f^{-1}(B)\n        &= \\left\\{(x,\\alpha) \\in \\bigsqcup_{\\alpha\\in A}X_\\alpha ~\\middle|~ f(x,\\alpha) \\in B \\right\\}\\\\\n        &= \\bigcup_{\\alpha \\in A} \\iota_\\alpha\\big((f\\restriction_{X_\\alpha})^{-1}(B)\\big).\n    \\end{align*}\n    Each set $(f\\restriction_{X_\\alpha})^{-1}(B)$ belongs to $\\mathcal{B}(X_\\alpha)$ by assumption, and $\\iota_\\alpha$ is a homeomorphism onto the Borel set $X_\\alpha \\times \\{\\alpha\\}$. Hence each image\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\iota_\\alpha\\big((f\\restriction_{X_\\alpha})^{-1}(B)\\big)\n    \\end{align*}\n    is Borel in $X$. Since the family is countable, the union is Borel as well. Therefore, $f^{-1}(B) \\in \\mathcal{B}(X)$ for every $B \\in \\Sigma$, proving that $f$ is measurable."
      },
