            "references": [
              "defn:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-distance"
            ],
            "text": "To prove (2), suppose that $(X,d)$ is complete, equivalently that $(X,\\bar{d})$ is complete, and let $(\\bm{x}_n^\\pi)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ be a Cauchy sequence in $(\\mathscr{T}^\\pi(X), \\mathscr{d}_\\pi)$. Since $\\mathscr{d}_\\pi(\\bm{u}^\\pi, \\bm{v}^\\pi) = 1$ whenever $\\ell(\\bm{u}) \\neq \\ell(\\bm{v})$, there exists an $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and a common length $r \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$ such that $\\ell(\\bm{x}_n) = r$ for all $n \\geq N$. If $r = 0$, then $\\bm{x}_n^\\pi = \\bm{e}^\\pi$ for all $n \\geq N$, and the sequence converges to $\\bm{e}^\\pi$. Assume therefore that $r > 0$. By passing to the tail $n \\geq N$, we may extract a subsequence $(\\bm{x}_{n_k}^\\pi)_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_\\pi(\\bm{x}_{n_k}^\\pi,\\bm{x}_{n_{k+1}}^\\pi)<2^{-k} \\qquad \\forall k \\in \\mathbb{N}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Choose an arbitrary representative $\\bm{y}_1\\in\\bm{x}_{n_1}^\\pi$. Proceeding inductively for $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$, given $\\bm{y}_k \\in \\bm{x}_{n_k}^\\pi$ and an arbitrary initial representative $\\widetilde{\\bm{y}}_{k+1} \\in \\bm{x}_{n_{k+1}}^\\pi$, let $\\sigma_k \\in \\mathscr{P}_r$ attain the minimum in $\\mathscr{d}_\\pi(\\bm{x}_{n_k}^\\pi,\\bm{x}_{n_{k+1}}^\\pi)$ and set $\\bm{y}_{k+1} = \\widetilde{\\bm{y}}_{k+1} \\circ \\sigma_k \\in \\bm{x}_{n_{k+1}}^\\pi$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\max_{i \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}}\\bar{d}\\big(\\bm{y}_k(i),\\bm{y}_{k+1}(i)\\big) = \\mathscr{d}_\\pi(\\bm{x}_{n_k}^\\pi,\\bm{x}_{n_{k+1}}^\\pi) < 2^{-k}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Consequently, for each coordinate $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}$, the sequence $(\\bm{y}_k(i))_{k\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is Cauchy in $(X, \\bar{d})$ and therefore converges to a limit $y(i)\\in X$. Defining $\\bm{y}=(y(1),\\dots,y(r)) \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ and taking the identity permutation in Definition~\\ref{defn:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-distance} yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_\\pi(\\bm{x}_{n_k}^\\pi, \\bm{y}^\\pi) \\leq \\max_{i \\in \\{1, \\dots, r\\}} \\bar{d}\\big(\\bm{y}_k(i), \\bm{y}(i)\\big) \\xrightarrow{k \\to \\infty} 0.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Because $(\\bm{x}_n^\\pi)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is Cauchy and possesses a subsequence converging to $\\bm{y}^\\pi$, the entire sequence converges to $\\bm{y}^\\pi$. This proves that $(\\mathscr{T}^\\pi(X), \\mathscr{d}_\\pi)$ is complete, as desired.",
