            "text": "It remains to prove measurability in $\\omega$. For $n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$, define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        A_n = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid N(\\omega)=n\\} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid \\xi(\\omega)(X)=n\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\omega \\mapsto \\xi(\\omega)(X)$ is measurable, each $A_n \\in \\mathcal{F}$. Now let $B \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(X))$ be fixed. By measurability of $\\tau \\circ \\xi$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:measurable-enumeration} and Remark~\\ref{rem:point-process-measurability-equivalence}) and continuity of the permutation map $\\bm{x}\\mapsto\\bm{x}[\\sigma]$ of Definition~\\ref{defn:permutation-map} (Proposition~\\ref{prop:permuting-continuous}) for fixed $\\sigma\\in\\mathscr{P}_n$, the map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\omega \\mapsto \\mathbbm{1}_{A_n}(\\omega)\\mathbbm{1}_{B}(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma])\n    \\end{align*}\n    is measurable for every $n$ and every $\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$.\n    For $\\omega\\notin A_n$, the total permutation map returns the empty tuple, so the displayed expression is defined on all of $\\Omega$. For $\\omega\\in A_n$, the defining property of $\\tau$ gives $\\ell(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega)))=\\xi(\\omega)(X)=n$, and hence, including when $n=0$, $\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma]=\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))\\circ\\sigma$.\n    Therefore,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\omega \\mapsto \\frac{1}{n!}\\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n}\\mathbbm{1}_{A_n}(\\omega)\\mathbbm{1}_{B}(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma])\n    \\end{align*}\n    is measurable for each $n$. Finally,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,B)\n        = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n!}\\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n}\\mathbbm{1}_{A_n}(\\omega)\\mathbbm{1}_{B}(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma]),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and this is measurable because the sum is countable and, for each fixed $\\omega$, at most one term in $n$ is non-zero. Hence $\\omega \\mapsto \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,B)$ is measurable, as desired.",
