      "mappedToLean": true,
      "order": 397,
      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Tuples and Point Processes",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:point-process-tuple}\n    Let $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, and let $(X,d)$ be a complete and separable metric space. Let $\\xi :\\Omega \\to \\mathcal{N}(X)$ be a point process. Let $\\tau:\\mathcal{N}(X) \\to \\mathscr{T}(X)$ be a measurable enumeration as in Lemma~\\ref{lem:measurable-enumeration}. Define the mapping $\\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}} :\\Omega \\times \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(X)) \\to [0,1]$ by\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega, B) = \\frac{1}{N(\\omega)!} \\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_{N(\\omega)}} \\mathbbm{1}_B(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))\\circ \\sigma),\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $N(\\omega) = \\xi(\\omega)(X)$ is the total number of points of the realisation $\\xi(\\omega)$. Then, $\\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}$ is a Markov kernel from $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F})$ to $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(X)))$. Moreover,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,\\mathfrak{m}^{-1}(\\{\\xi(\\omega)\\}))=1,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and $\\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,\\cdot)$ is the uniform distribution on the class $(\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi(\\omega))$; in particular, it does not depend on the choice of $\\tau$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 734,
        "references": [
          "cor:tuple-induced-measure-permutation-invariant",
          "prop:finite-equivalence-classes",
          "lem:measurable-enumeration",
          "rem:point-process-measurability-equivalence",
          "defn:permutation-map",
          "prop:permuting-continuous"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/5_stochastic_markets.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__point-process-tuple__proof.svg",
        "text": "We must show that \n    \\begin{enumerate}\n        \\item $B \\mapsto \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega, B)$ is a probability measure for all $\\omega \\in \\Omega$, and\n        \\item $\\omega \\mapsto \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega, B)$ is $\\mathcal{F}/\\mathcal{B}([0,1])$-measurable for every $B\\in\\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(X))$.\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    For fixed $\\omega$, let $n=N(\\omega)$. Then\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,\\cdot) = \\frac{1}{n!}\\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n} \\delta_{\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))\\circ \\sigma},\n    \\end{align*}\n    which is a finite convex combination of Dirac probability measures, hence a probability measure. Each $\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))\\circ\\sigma$ induces $\\xi(\\omega)$ by Corollary~\\ref{cor:tuple-induced-measure-permutation-invariant}, which gives the support statement. By Proposition~\\ref{prop:finite-equivalence-classes}, every tuple in the class $(\\mathfrak{m}^\\pi)^{-1}(\\xi(\\omega))$ is produced by the same number of permutations, so the weights are uniform on the class.\n\n    It remains to prove measurability in $\\omega$. For $n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$, define\n    \\begin{align*}\n        A_n = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid N(\\omega)=n\\} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega \\mid \\xi(\\omega)(X)=n\\}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Since $\\omega \\mapsto \\xi(\\omega)(X)$ is measurable, each $A_n \\in \\mathcal{F}$. Now let $B \\in \\mathcal{B}(\\mathscr{T}(X))$ be fixed. By measurability of $\\tau \\circ \\xi$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:measurable-enumeration} and Remark~\\ref{rem:point-process-measurability-equivalence}) and continuity of the permutation map $\\bm{x}\\mapsto\\bm{x}[\\sigma]$ of Definition~\\ref{defn:permutation-map} (Proposition~\\ref{prop:permuting-continuous}) for fixed $\\sigma\\in\\mathscr{P}_n$, the map\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\omega \\mapsto \\mathbbm{1}_{A_n}(\\omega)\\mathbbm{1}_{B}(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma])\n    \\end{align*}\n    is measurable for every $n$ and every $\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n$.\n    For $\\omega\\notin A_n$, the total permutation map returns the empty tuple, so the displayed expression is defined on all of $\\Omega$. For $\\omega\\in A_n$, the defining property of $\\tau$ gives $\\ell(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega)))=\\xi(\\omega)(X)=n$, and hence, including when $n=0$, $\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma]=\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))\\circ\\sigma$.\n    Therefore,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\omega \\mapsto \\frac{1}{n!}\\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n}\\mathbbm{1}_{A_n}(\\omega)\\mathbbm{1}_{B}(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma])\n    \\end{align*}\n    is measurable for each $n$. Finally,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,B)\n        = \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n!}\\sum_{\\sigma \\in \\mathscr{P}_n}\\mathbbm{1}_{A_n}(\\omega)\\mathbbm{1}_{B}(\\tau(\\xi(\\omega))[\\sigma]),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and this is measurable because the sum is countable and, for each fixed $\\omega$, at most one term in $n$ is non-zero. Hence $\\omega \\mapsto \\mathsf{K}_{\\mathrm{perm}}(\\omega,B)$ is measurable, as desired."
      },
