      "statement": "\\label{thm:prokhorov-metric-weak-convergence-polish}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a complete and separable metric space, and let $\\mathcal{M}(X)$ denote the family of finite measures on $(X,\\mathcal{B}(X))$. Let $\\rho:\\mathcal{M}(X) \\times \\mathcal{M}(X) \\to [0,\\infty)$ be the function defined by \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\rho(\\mu, \\eta) = \\inf \\{ \\varepsilon > 0 \\mid \\forall B \\in \\mathcal{B}(X): \\mu(B) \\leq \\eta(B^\\varepsilon) + \\varepsilon \\mathrm{~and~}\\eta(B) \\leq \\mu(B^\\varepsilon) + \\varepsilon\\}. \n    \\end{align*}\n    Then $(\\mathcal{M}(X), \\rho)$ is a metric space, and $\\rho$ is called the Prokhorov metric. Moreover, \n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item a sequence $(\\mu_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}} \\subseteq \\mathcal{M}(X)$ converges weakly to $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{M}(X) $ if and only if $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty}\\rho(\\mu_n,\\mu) = 0$; \n        \\item $(\\mathcal{M}(X), \\rho)$ is a complete and separable metric space; and  \n        \\item $(\\mathcal{N}(X), \\rho)$ is a complete and separable metric space.\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
