      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Conditional Expectation and Regression",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:regression-theorem}\\index{Hilbert space regression}\n    Let $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $(\\mathcal{H}, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ be a real separable Hilbert space, and let $\\mathcal{G} \\subseteq \\mathcal{F}$ be a sub-$\\sigma$-algebra. For every random element $X:\\Omega \\to \\mathcal{H}$ with $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X\\rVert^2] < \\infty$,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid \\mathcal{G}] \\in \\argmin_{U \\in L^2(\\Omega, \\mathcal{G}, \\mathbb{P}; \\mathcal{H})} \\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X - U\\rVert^2],\n    \\end{align*}\n    where the minimisation is over $L^2$ equivalence classes, and the minimiser is unique. If $Y:\\Omega \\to E$ is a random element taking values in a standard Borel space $(E, \\mathcal{E})$, then there also exists a measurable function $g^\\star:E\\to\\mathcal{H}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid Y] = \\mathbb{E}[X \\mid \\sigma(Y)] = g^\\star(Y)\\qquad\\text{almost surely},\n    \\end{align*}\n    so the random element $g^\\star(Y)$ minimises $\\mathbb{E}[\\lVert X-U\\rVert^2]$ over $U\\in L^2(\\Omega,\\sigma(Y),\\mathbb{P};\\mathcal{H})$.",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": null,
