      "proofStatus": "verified",
      "section": "Product Measures, Kernels, and Disintegration",
      "statement": "{kallenberg2002foundations}}] \\label{thm:regular-conditional-distribution}\\index{regular conditional distribution}\n    Let $(S,\\mathcal{S})$ be a standard Borel space, let $(T,\\mathcal{T})$ be a measurable space, and let $X:\\Omega \\to S$ and $Y:\\Omega \\to T$ be random elements. Then there exists a Markov kernel $Q$ from $T$ to $S$, called a regular conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$, such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{P}(X \\in A, Y \\in B)\n        =\n        \\int_B Q(y,A)\\,\\mathrm{d}(\\mathbb{P}\\circ Y^{-1})(y)\n    \\end{align*}\n    for all $A \\in \\mathcal{S}$ and $B \\in \\mathcal{T}$, and $Q$ is unique $(\\mathbb{P}\\circ Y^{-1})$-almost everywhere. Moreover, writing $Q_y:=Q(y,\\cdot)$, every bounded measurable $g:S \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfies\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[g(X)\\mid Y]\n        =\n        \\int_S g\\,\\mathrm{d}Q_Y\n        \\qquad \\text{a.s.}\n    \\end{align*}",
