            "references": [],
            "text": "We must show first that $\\sigma(X)=\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$, where\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathcal{G}_\\infty = \\sigma(\\theta_n, n \\in \\mathbb{N}),\n    \\end{align*}\n    and second that $\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid\\mathcal{G}_N]\\to\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid\\sigma(X)]$ almost surely and in $L^1$ (respectively $L^2$) as $N\\to\\infty$. Each score $\\theta_n=\\langle X,e_n\\rangle$ is the composition of $X$ with a continuous linear functional. Consequently, every $\\theta_n$ is $\\sigma(X)$-measurable, and hence\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathcal{G}_\\infty \\subseteq \\sigma(X).\n    \\end{align*}\n    To prove the reverse inclusion, fix $x\\in\\mathcal{H}$ and $\\varepsilon>0$, and set\n    \\begin{align*}\n        x_n = \\langle x, e_n\\rangle,\\qquad n\\in\\mathbb{N}.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Parseval's identity gives\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\lVert X - x\\rVert^2 = \\sum_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} (\\theta_n - x_n)^2.\n    \\end{align*}\n    For each $N\\in\\mathbb{N}$, the partial sum $S_N = \\sum_{n = 1}^N (\\theta_n - x_n)^2$ is $\\mathcal{G}_N$-measurable and hence $\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$-measurable. Since $S_N\\to\\lVert X-x\\rVert^2$ pointwise on $\\Omega$, the squared distance $\\lVert X-x\\rVert^2$ is also $\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$-measurable. Therefore, the preimage of the open ball $B_\\varepsilon(x)$ satisfies\n    \\begin{align*}\n        X^{-1}\\bigl(B_\\varepsilon(x)\\bigr)=\\{\\lVert X - x\\rVert < \\varepsilon\\} = \\{\\lVert X - x\\rVert^2 < \\varepsilon^2\\} \\in \\mathcal{G}_\\infty.\n    \\end{align*}\n    To pass from open balls to arbitrary open sets without invoking an uncountable union, use the separability of $\\mathcal{H}$ to fix a countable dense subset $D\\subseteq\\mathcal{H}$. The family\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathcal{C}=\\{B_q(d)\\mid d\\in D,\\ q\\in\\mathbb{Q}_{>0}\\}\n    \\end{align*}\n    is a countable basis for the norm topology of $\\mathcal{H}$. By the preceding argument, $X^{-1}(C)\\in\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$ for every $C\\in\\mathcal{C}$. Since every open subset of $\\mathcal{H}$ is a countable union of elements of $\\mathcal{C}$, its preimage under $X$ also belongs to $\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$. Hence $X$ is $\\mathcal{G}_\\infty/\\mathcal{B}(\\mathcal{H})$-measurable, so $\\sigma(X)\\subseteq\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$. Together with the first inclusion, this yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\sigma(X) = \\mathcal{G}_\\infty.\n    \\end{align*}\n    Finally, since $(\\mathcal{G}_N)_{N\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ is an increasing filtration with $\\sigma\\bigl(\\bigcup_{N\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\mathcal{G}_N\\bigr)=\\mathcal{G}_\\infty$, L\\'evy's upward martingale convergence theorem yields\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid \\mathcal{G}_N] \\to \\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid \\mathcal{G}_\\infty] = \\mathbb{E}[Y \\mid \\sigma(X)]\n    \\end{align*}\n    almost surely and in $L^1$ for every $Y \\in L^1(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$, and in $L^2$ whenever $Y\\in L^2(\\Omega,\\mathcal{F},\\mathbb{P})$, as desired.",
