      "section": "A Topology for Orders",
      "statement": "\\label{thm:tuple-space-properties}\\index{tuple space!metrisation}\n    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. Then, for every $\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}^\\prime \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$ it holds that\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\item $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}^\\prime) < 1$ implies $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}^\\prime)$. \\label{eq:tuple-length}\n        \\item $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}, \\bm{x}^\\prime) < 1$ and $\\ell(\\bm{x}) > 0$ implies $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime) = \\max_{i\\in\\{1,\\dots, \\ell(\\bm{x})\\}} d(\\bm{x}(i), \\bm{x}^\\prime(i))$; without the restriction $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime) < 1$, $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{x}^\\prime) > 0$ implies $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{x}^\\prime) \\leq \\max_{i\\in\\{1,\\dots, \\ell(\\bm{x})\\}} d(\\bm{x}(i), \\bm{x}^\\prime(i))$. \\label{eq:tuple-collapse}\n    \\end{enumerate}\n    Moreover:\n    \\begin{enumerate}[(i)]\n        \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n        \\item if $(X,d)$ is a complete metric space, then $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ is a complete metric space; \\label{eq:tuple-complete}\n        \\item if $(X,d)$ is a separable metric space, then $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ is a separable metric space;\\label{eq:tuple-separable}\n        \\item if $(X,d)$ is a separable metric space and $\\preceq$ is any partial order on $X$, then $(\\mathscr{S}(X, \\preceq),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ is a separable metric subspace of $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$. \\label{eq:sorted-tuple-separable}\n    \\end{enumerate}",
      "status": "verified",
      "thesisIssue": null,
      "thesisProof": {
        "line": 1158,
        "references": [
          "eq:tuple-length",
          "eq:tuple-collapse",
          "eq:bounded-by-one",
          "eq:tuple-complete",
          "eq:tuple-separable",
          "eq:sorted-tuple-separable",
          "thm:labelled-properties"
        ],
        "source": "1 - theoretical foundations/2_chapter_market_representations.tex",
        "svg": "thesis-svg/thm__tuple-space-properties__proof.svg",
        "text": "We show properties \\eqref{eq:tuple-length} and \\eqref{eq:tuple-collapse} in tandem. The claims are trivial if one of the tuples is empty; the distance is either $0$ if both tuples are empty or $\\infty$ otherwise. Therefore, assume that $\\bm{x},\\bm{y}\\in\\mathscr{T}(X)$ are non-empty tuples. Then, by definition  \n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{y})\n        &= \\max\\left(\n        \\begin{aligned}\n            &\\max_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})}\n            \\min_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\bigl(|i-j| + d(x,y)\\bigr),\\\\\n            &\\max_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})}\n            \\min_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\bigl(|j-i| + d(y,x)\\bigr)\n        \\end{aligned}\n        \\right)\n    \\end{align*}\n    Note that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) < 1, \n    \\end{align*}\n    implies that \n    \\begin{align}\\label{eq:bounded-by-one}\n        \\max_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\min_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\bigl(|i-j| + d(x,y)\\bigr) < 1,\n    \\end{align}\n    and\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\max_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\min_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\bigl(|j-i| + d(y,x)\\bigr) < 1.\n    \\end{align*}\n    For natural numbers $i,j\\in \\mathbb{N}$, $|i-j| < 1$ implies $i = j$. If $\\ell(\\bm{x}) \\neq \\ell(\\bm{y})$, assume without loss of generality that $\\ell(\\bm{x}) > \\ell(\\bm{y})$; then for $i = \\ell(\\bm{x})$, every $(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})$ satisfies\n    \\begin{align*}\n        |i-j| + d(\\bm{x}(i), y) \\geq |i-j| \\geq 1,\n    \\end{align*}\n    and hence $\\ell(\\bm{x}) \\neq \\ell(\\bm{y})$ implies that $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) \\geq 1$. By contraposition, the claim follows. Moreover, by \\eqref{eq:bounded-by-one}, each inner minimum is at most $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) < 1$, and every $j \\neq i$ contributes at least $1$, so the minimum is attained at $j = i$. For each index $i$, the graph contains exactly the point $(i,\\bm{x}(i))$; the same-index point of the other graph is $(i,\\bm{y}(i))$. Hence,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        &\\max_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\min_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\bigl(|i-j| + d(x,y)\\bigr) < 1 \\implies \\\\\n        &\\max_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\min_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\bigl(|i-j| + d(x,y)\\bigr) = \\max_{i \\in \\{1,\\dots, n\\}} d(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{y}(i)) < 1,\n    \\end{align*}\n    where $n = \\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{y})$. A similar argument shows that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        &\\max_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\min_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\bigl(|j-i| + d(y,x)\\bigr) < 1 \\implies\\\\\n        &\\max_{(j,y) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{y})} \\min_{(i,x) \\in \\Gamma(\\bm{x})} \\bigl(|j-i| + d(y,x)\\bigr) = \\max_{i \\in \\{1,\\dots, n\\}} d(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{y}(i)) < 1,\n    \\end{align*}\n    which proves the claims \\eqref{eq:tuple-length} and \\eqref{eq:tuple-collapse}, except the final inequality of \\eqref{eq:tuple-collapse}. For the latter, if $\\ell(\\bm{x}) = \\ell(\\bm{y}) > 0$, taking $j = i$ in each inner minimum bounds both terms of $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x},\\bm{y})$ by $\\max_{i} d(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{y}(i))$.\n\n    To prove \\eqref{eq:tuple-complete}, let $(\\bm{x}_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}$ be a Cauchy sequence in $\\mathscr{T}(X)$. That is, for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists an $N\\in\\mathbb{N}$ such that \n    \\begin{align*}\n        m,n\\geq N \\implies \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}_m, \\bm{x}_n) < \\varepsilon.\n    \\end{align*}\n    In particular, there is $N_0 \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        m,n\\geq N_0 \\implies \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}_m, \\bm{x}_n) < 1, \n    \\end{align*}\n    and notice that by \\eqref{eq:tuple-length} we have that $n \\geq N_0$ implies $\\ell(\\bm{x}_n) = k \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$. If $k=0$, then the tail of the sequence is constantly the empty tuple and hence converges. Assume therefore that $k>0$. By \\eqref{eq:tuple-collapse} it follows that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}_m, \\bm{x}_n) < 1 \\implies \\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}_m, \\bm{x}_n) = \\max_{i \\in \\{1,\\dots, k\\}} d(\\bm{x}_m(i),\\bm{x}_n(i)).\n    \\end{align*}\n    The sequence $(\\bm{x}_n)_{n = N_0}^\\infty$ is Cauchy in $(X^k,d_\\infty^{(k)})$, with $d_\\infty^{(k)}(\\bm{x},\\bm{y}) = \\max_{i} d(\\bm{x}(i),\\bm{y}(i))$, which is complete and hence has a limit $\\bm{x} \\in (X^k, d_\\infty^{(k)})$. Since $X^k \\subseteq \\mathscr{T}(X)$, we have $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$. By the final inequality of \\eqref{eq:tuple-collapse}, $\\mathscr{d}_H(\\bm{x}_n,\\bm{x}) \\leq d_\\infty^{(k)}(\\bm{x}_n,\\bm{x})$, so $\\bm{x}_n$ converges to $\\bm{x} \\in \\mathscr{T}(X)$, as desired.\n\n    For \\eqref{eq:tuple-separable} and \\eqref{eq:sorted-tuple-separable}, it remains to show that $(\\mathscr{T}(X), \\mathscr{d}_H)$ is separable, provided $(X,d)$ is separable; if $(X, \\preceq, d)$ is a partially ordered metric space, then $\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq) \\subseteq \\mathscr{T}(X)$ and if $\\mathscr{T}(X)$ is separable, then $\\mathscr{S}(X,\\preceq)$ will also be separable, since metric subspaces of separable metric spaces are separable.\n\n    If $(X,d)$ is separable, then so is $(\\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\\ast)$ by Theorem~\\ref{thm:labelled-properties}. Since\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\Gamma(\\mathscr{T}(X)) \\subseteq \\mathscr{L}(X),\n    \\end{align*}\n    $(\\Gamma(\\mathscr{T}(X)),d_H^\\ast)$ is separable, as it is a subset of a separable metric space. As $\\Gamma:(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H) \\to (\\Gamma(\\mathscr{T}(X)),d_H^\\ast)$ is a bijective isometry, $(\\mathscr{T}(X),\\mathscr{d}_H)$ is separable as well."
      },
